        ! cent at the settled vector, so 1.3 leaves size_homelessness with almost
        ! nothing to do -- a gap of -0.7 per cent rather than the +11.3 that
        ! 0.0116 implied. It buys the homelessness rate by moving the target to
        ! the model rather than the model to the target, and THE PAPER MUST SAY
        ! SO wherever this number appears, as it must for severe_cost_burden_data.
        ! 0.01160 -> 0.01200 -> 0.01300 -> 0.01000, on the author's instruction of
        ! 11 September: "1% homelessness rate for a real h1". "Real" is the point:
        ! until bottom_productivity_level was raised, homelessness was arithmetic
        ! -- 95.5 per cent of the homeless could not afford the smallest unit at
        ! any price -- so size_homelessness had nothing to act on and h_1 did not
        ! control its own target.
        ! 0.4 PER CENT, 14 September: *"lower h1 until you hit 0.4%
        ! homelessness and accept whatever emp homeless is at that point"*,
        ! and earlier *"The homelessness being off is ok. We just need a number
        ! between 0.4-1%"*.  0.4 is the bottom of that band, chosen because
        ! the measured slope runs the wrong way for emp_homeless: at loop 1,
        ! same vector, only h_1 moving, h_1 1.196e-3 gave a rate of 0.598 per
        ! cent with 24.5 per cent of the homeless employed, and h_1 3.588e-3
        ! gave 1.066 per cent with 19.4.  A LOWER rate carries a HIGHER
        ! employed share, so the bottom of the band is the best available.
        ! ONE PER CENT, the top of the author's band rather than the bottom.
        ! 15 September: *"you can set h1 to 1% if you need to when you raise
        ! h2"*, and *"raise h2 such that for a reasonable h1 you can still get 1%
        ! homelessness and about 40-50% emp homeless"*.
