        ! ---- KAPPA_X, THE DISAMENITY OF AGGREGATE HOMELESSNESS ---------------
        ! One household's housing status entering another's utility, the only
        ! term in the model that does that outside the family. Priced from the
        ! contingent valuation in Loubiere et al. (2020): 57 euro a year among
        ! the 42 per cent of respondents willing to pay anything, which is 0.20
        ! per cent of average annual earnings. The HIGHER of their two figures is
        ! the baseline, because setting it high favours the policy that houses
        ! more people, so a ranking that survives it is the stronger claim; the
        ! lower one, 23 euro and 0.08 per cent, is the other end of the range.
        !
        ! IT CANNOT MOVE A POLICY FUNCTION. H is the aggregate headcount, taken
        ! as given by every household, so kappa_x*H is a constant inside the
        ! household's problem and shifts every value by the same amount. It
        ! changes welfare COMPARISONS ACROSS STEADY STATES, which is where the
        ! paper uses it, and it changes nothing in the calibration. That is why
        ! it can be added to a calibration already in flight.
        real(8) :: kappa_x=0.002d0

        ! ---- INTERNALISING THE HOMELESSNESS EXTERNALITY ----------------------
        ! The author, 18 September: *"i want to have an option to allow the code
        ! to include a cost to each household who becomes homeless, that captures
        ! their full cost to the economy from their own personal homelessness"*,
        ! so that the OPTIMAL homelessness can be read the way omega_c = 1 gives
        ! the optimal drug use -- both make the chooser face the whole social
        ! cost of their own choice and then report what survives.
        !
        ! THE CHARGE IS kappa_x, FLAT, AND THE ARITHMETIC IS WHY. H here is
        ! model_fraction_homeless, a SHARE. A household of mass dm moving to the
        ! street raises H by dm/M, every one of the M units of mass loses
        ! kappa_x*dm/M, and the total is kappa_x*dm. Per unit of own mass that is
        ! kappa_x, with the population size cancelling and NO dependence on how
        ! many others are already homeless -- the H in kappa_x*H is what makes
        ! that term a constant to each household, and the MARGINAL damage has no
        ! H in it at all.
        !
        ! IT IS NOT SCALED BY THE CHOOSER'S HOUSEHOLD SIZE, unlike flow utility
        ! itself, because what a household owes depends on whom it harms and not
        ! on how many people live with it.
        !
        ! UNLIKE kappa_x*H, THIS MOVES POLICY FUNCTIONS. That is the point: it is
        ! the Pigouvian charge on the housing choice. It therefore belongs to a
        ! counterfactual arm and cannot be switched on inside the benchmark
        ! calibration. At 0 the model is the one that came before, which is the
        ! no-op this is built against.
        integer :: internalise_homelessness_cost=0

        ! The CURVATURE on leisure, sigma_ell in the note, not the Frisch
        ! elasticity itself. With hours at 0.40 the implied Frisch elasticity is
        ! (1/sigma_ell)*(1-n)/n = (1/3)*(0.6/0.4) = 0.5, which is the number the
        ! calibration table reports and the one taken from Chetty (2012).
        real(8), parameter :: leisure_curvature=3
        real(8), parameter :: risk_aversion_housing=2
        real(8), parameter :: relative_share_of_housing_0=&
            (1-perceived_probability_lower)*0.278+perceived_probability_lower*0.31
        ! Starts at the value the utility was actually using while gamma was
        ! disconnected, so a cold start begins where the model has been solving
        ! rather than at a number nothing ever faced.
        real(8) :: relative_share_of_housing=0.278
        real(8) :: distance_relative_share_of_housing
        ! ZERO, AND DELIBERATELY. The internal update for this parameter is
        ! STALE: it steps on model_avg_housing_spending_renters against
        ! housing_share_of_expenditure_data, which is the INCOME-denominator object from
        ! before gamma was moved to expenditure, and it is then overwritten by a
        ! second update aimed at a different moment entirely, the mass of
        ! renters spending over X. Freeing the tie without this would have let
        ! that stale path drive the parameter against the wrong target.
        ! next_batch.py owns it across points instead, like the other sixteen.
        ! RESTORED TO ITS OWN START VALUE, 11 September. THE FREEING WAS
        ! NEVER COMPLETED.
        !
        ! tie_gamma_to_theta was set to 0 on the author's instruction,
        ! recorded beside it: "gamma was the one targeted moment with no
        ! instrument: this parameter was pinned at 0.28*(1-theta), so nothing
        ! could move housing's share of expenditure." Releasing the tie is
        ! half of that. The adjuster was left at zero, so gamma still could
        ! not move and the moment still had no instrument.
        !
        ! AND WITH THE SWEEP OFF IT STOPS BEING ONLY A MISSING BRACKET. The
        ! joint loop's do-while has no iteration cap; it runs until every
        ! distance is under its stopping rule. Every other dead adjuster in
        ! that test carries an 'if (adjuster==0) distance=0' guard, so its
        ! term retires. gamma's only reachable zeroing is gated on
        ! tie_gamma_to_theta==1, which is false, so its distance stays live
        ! against an adjuster that cannot move it: THE LOOP NEVER TERMINATES.
        ! This was masked while sweep_addiction_scalar was 1, because
        ! re_do_simulation_with_fixed_parameters zeroes every distance and
        ! the loop exits after one pass.
        !
        ! 5e-2 is relative_share_of_housing_adjuster_start, the value
        ! damp_on_flip already grows it back toward, so nothing else changes.
        ! The guard below is added anyway, so pinning gamma again retires its
        ! term instead of hanging the loop.
        ! RELEASED 12 September: the author named gamma. Was
        ! (1-freeze_to_three_block)*5e-2, zero under the stage-1 freeze.
        ! HELD 16 September. The author: *"i think we have to go back....and
        ! just calibrate the following: aleph, b, kappa, tau_u, SUD homeless,
        ! homeless, SUD"*. Seven moments, seven instruments, everything else
        ! frozen where it stands.
        ! BACK IN THE CALIBRATION, on the author's instruction of 16 September:
        ! *"i think we also have to get gamma also into the calibration....it is
        ! not close enough"*. It was 17.8 per cent off when I held it.
        ! PINNED 17 September. The author: *"Then gamma should give, keep h2 on
        ! the 30% target"*, then *"pin gamma to what it is now and accept being
        ! off"*.
        !
        ! WHY IT HAD TO GIVE. h_2 and gamma were fighting over the same price.
        ! h_2 hits the renters' 30 per cent burden by making the cheapest rental
        ! CHEAP; gamma hits the aggregate housing share of expenditure by making
        ! housing EXPENSIVE. Measured within n32, monotonically:
        !
        !     h_2 0.11850 -> gamma's moment 0.26501   (on the 0.268 target)
        !     h_2 0.11269                    0.24511
        !     h_2 0.10610                    0.23580
        !     h_2 0.09989                    0.22630
        !     h_2 0.09404                    0.21523
        !     h_2 0.08854                    0.20443
        !
        ! h_2 fell 25 per cent and gamma's moment fell from ON target to 24 per
        ! cent below it. That is joint identification, not slow convergence, and
        ! the author chose which one gives.
        !
        ! THE INCOME DENOMINATOR IS NOT AN ESCAPE. The author asked whether
        ! gamma could target housing over income at 0.202 instead. The model
        ! reads 0.29472 there, 46 per cent high against gamma's 25, and it would
