        real(8) :: sd_fixed_employment_newborn=0.309 !0.309

        ! SIGMA_Y, the dispersion of the permanent productivity draw, in logs.
        ! This is the number reported in the calibration table, so the grid is
        ! built from it directly rather than inherited from whatever width the
        ! KKK process happened to leave behind. The value below reproduces the
        ! grid the frozen process used to deliver, to the last digit, so setting
        ! it here changes nothing until the number itself is changed.
        !
        ! IT IS SET TO THE DATA. In the SCF 2022, households aged 26 to 65 with
        ! positive wage income have an sd of log earnings of 1.036, and 1.019
        ! net of age effects, which this model carries separately in eps(j).
        ! Netting off the transitory variance of 0.063 in Storesletten et al.
        ! (2004) leaves 1.00, and that is the value below. The stylized model
        ! uses the same number.
        !
        ! On the nine node grid it delivers a bottom half share of earnings of
        ! 16.5 percent against 17.2 in the SCF and a top tenth share of 38.5
        ! against 41.1. p90/p10 comes out at 20 against 10, but that is the
        ! discretisation rather than the dispersion: nine nodes over six
        ! standard deviations put the tenth and ninetieth percentiles on the
        ! second and eighth nodes, so the ratio is lumpy where the shares are
        ! not. The shares are the moments to read.
        !
        ! What it replaced: 0.458, which was not chosen at all. It was whatever
        ! width the KKK process happened to leave on the frozen grid, and it
        ! gave a bottom half share of 33 percent, an almost equal society.
        real(8) :: sd_permanent_productivity=1.00d0
        ! HELD AT ZERO, and not by the freeze flag: this is the DEAD instrument
        ! for sigma_y under the KKK grid the model actually runs. See
        ! kkk_sigma_scale_adjuster above. If fixed_productivity ever goes to 1
        ! the two swap roles and this is the one to wake.
        real(8) :: sd_permanent_productivity_adjuster=0.d0
        real(8) :: distance_sd_permanent_productivity=1
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_sd_permanent_productivity=10.d0**(-3)
        integer, parameter :: calibrate_sd_permanent_productivity=1
        ! THE MOMENT. The share of labour productivity held by the bottom half of
        ! the distribution: 17.2 percent in the SCF 2022, households aged 26 to 65
        ! with positive wage and salary income. sigma_y moves against it, and the
        ! mapping is monotone, so the step below is a plain secant on one number.
        real(8), parameter :: bottom_half_earnings_share_data=0.172d0
        real(8) :: model_bottom_half_earnings_share=0
        real(8) :: persistence_employment_surviving=0.9
        real(8) :: sd_employment_surviving=0.2

        ! THE AUTHOR'S OWN INCOME PROCESS, PROMOTED OUT OF THE SUBROUTINE.
        !
        ! These four numbers plus the two grid widths are KKK_process, the
        ! two-component process of Guvenen, Karahan, Ozkan and Song's "use it or
        ! lose it": a fixed effect drawn once at birth with persistence 0.5 and
        ! innovation sd 0.309, and a persistent component with persistence 0.9
        ! and innovation sd 0.2. The two are NOT carried as two state variables.
        ! The process collapses them onto ONE grid whose WIDTH grows with age,
        ! because the cross sectional variance of log productivity does:
        !
        !     var(1) = sigma_eta^2 + sigma_alpha^2/(1-rho_alpha^2)
        !     var(a) = rho_shock^2 var(a-1) + sigma_eta^2
        !
        ! and the grid at age a runs from -s_deviation_bottom*sd(a) to
        ! +s_deviation_top*sd(a). That is the FANNING OUT the author means: the
        ! same node index is a wider deviation from the mean at fifty than at
        ! twenty-six, so inequality within a cohort grows with age. Here the fan
        ! is from an sd of 0.409 at age one to 0.459 by the end of working life.
        !
        ! They were subroutine locals with initialisers, which meant no HANDOVER
        ! could reach them and none of them could be calibrated. They are global
        ! now for the same reason y_grid_kappa was promoted: an instrument that
        ! cannot be set from a point file is not an instrument.
        real(8) :: persistence_fixed_employment_newborn=0.5
        ! NAMED kkk_ RATHER THAN PLAIN. KKK_process and tauchen_method both take
        ! dummy arguments called s_deviation_top and s_deviation_bottom, and a
        ! module variable of the same name is an ambiguous reference inside them:
        ! the compiler refuses it outright, which is the good case. The prefix is
        ! also the HANDOVER name, so the point file and the declaration agree.
        real(8) :: kkk_s_deviation_top=3
        real(8) :: kkk_s_deviation_bottom=3

        ! THE COMMON SCALE OF THE TWO INNOVATION SDS, and the instrument that
        ! calibrates the KKK grid's dispersion. On the author's instruction of
        ! 3 September: *"Ok, you can calibrate sigma then"*.
        !
        ! WHY ONE NUMBER AND NOT TWO. The bottom half's share of earnings is a
        ! function of the DISPERSION of log income and of nothing else -- for a
        ! lognormal it is exactly Phi(-sigma) -- so it is one moment and it can
        ! identify one instrument. Calibrating both sds against it would be
        ! under-identified: infinitely many pairs deliver the same sigma.
        !
        ! The pair therefore splits into a RATIO and a SCALE, and only the scale
        ! is free. The ratio sigma_fixed/sigma_shock = 0.309/0.200 is Guvenen,
        ! Karahan, Ozkan and Song's and it is what decides how much of the
        ! dispersion is a birth endowment and how much accumulates over the life
        ! cycle -- the FANNING OUT. Multiplying both by the same k multiplies
        ! every var(a) by k^2 and so every sd(a) by k, which leaves sd(65)/sd(26)
        ! at 1.122 whatever k is. The shape of the age profile is untouched; only
        ! the level of inequality moves.
        !
        ! WHY IT IS NOT A FUDGE. GKOS estimate the dispersion of log earnings
        ! RESIDUALS, net of education, occupation, experience and sector. This
        ! model carries ONE productivity type per household, so that single
        ! number has to carry all permanent heterogeneity, including everything
        ! those controls removed. At the estimated sds the poorest household in
        ! the economy earns 0.232 of mean productivity and can pay the cheapest
        ! rent out of income, so the process as published does not merely miss an
        ! inequality statistic -- it deletes the population that becomes homeless.
        real(8) :: kkk_sigma_scale=1.0

        ! THE VALUE THE PRODUCTIVITY GRID WAS LAST BUILT AT.
        !
        ! The grid in GKKOC_intergenerational_employment_process sat behind
        ! "if (loop_count<1)", so it was built on the first loop and never
        ! again, and kkk_sigma_scale is read nowhere else that reaches the
        ! household. The instrument therefore moved and its own moment could
        ! not answer: in n24 the parameter took 349 distinct values between
        ! 2.2198 and 1.5671 while "sd of log y at age 1" printed one value,
        ! 0.90795, for all 347 blocks -- the value implied by the kkk the run
        ! STARTED with. n23 showed the same thing at its own opening value,
        ! 0.81342. The rule was integrating against a dispersion it could not
        ! affect, which is why sigma_y never converged in any run.
        !
        ! The sentinel is negative so the first pass always builds.
        real(8) :: kkk_sigma_scale_built=-1.d0
        ! ITS ADJUSTER. The share falls in sigma, so a model share above target
        ! means the economy is too equal and the scale has to rise, which is the
        ! same sign the sd_permanent_productivity step uses. The magnitude is set
        ! from d(share)/d(scale) = -phi(sigma)*sigma_base, about -0.16 near the
        ! target, so a step of 5 covers roughly three quarters of the gap in one
        ! pass and does not overshoot.
        ! UNFROZEN 11 September. The author: "sigma_y should match the share of
        ! income at the bottom 50%" -- so it is calibrated, not held.
        !
        ! kkk_sigma_scale is the LIVE instrument and sd_permanent_productivity is
        ! the dead one, not the other way round. gaps.py decides it from the grid
        ! actually in use: _DEAD_SIGMA is kkk_sigma_scale when fixed_productivity
        ! is 1 and sd_permanent_productivity otherwise, and run_one_point.sh's
        ! default is fixed_productivity=0. Both were gated on the SAME freeze
        ! flag, so unfreezing that flag would have put TWO instruments on one
        ! moment and had them fight. They are split here instead.
        ! SIGMA_Y FREED WITHOUT TOUCHING THE FREEZE FLAG, 16 September. The
        ! author: "can you also fix sigma_y?". It was gated by
        ! (1-freeze_to_three_block), and freeze_to_three_block is 1, so the
        ! adjuster was zero. The FLAG IS NOT FLIPPED, because it also sets
        ! max_move_per_loop (line 6059) and a merge at line 35404; flipping it
        ! would change two unrelated behaviours. The gate is removed here for
        ! this parameter alone.
        ! PINNED 17 September. The author: *"I suggest to just pin sigma_y
        ! since its close enough"*.
        !
        ! kkk_sigma_scale is set to the value the closed form gives, 1.9506,
        ! and held there. For a lognormal the bottom-half income share is
        ! Phi(-sigma), sigma is exactly linear in this scale, and the model
        ! sits a stable 0.041 above the closed form -- 0.043 at sd 0.908 and
        ! 0.038 at sd 0.647. Inverting for the 0.172 target gives sigma 0.797
        ! and this scale. Block 1 of n31 read 0.17049, nine tenths of a per
        ! cent off, from arithmetic rather than search.
        !
        ! WHY PIN RATHER THAN LEAVE IT LIVE. With its own two per cent step the
        ! rule walked AWAY from that value: kkk went 1.9506, 1.9896, 2.0294,
        ! 2.0700, 2.1114 over four blocks and the share went 0.17049, 0.17013,
        ! 0.16509, 0.16080 -- from 0.9 per cent off to 6.5. A closed form got
        ! there in one block and the instrument spent four undoing it.
        !
        ! The pin is safe: this adjuster does not pass through damp_on_flip and
        ! has no inline floor, so zero times the gap is zero and nothing
        ! resurrects it. The distance at 45067 already merges to zero when both
        ! sigma adjusters are off, so a pinned parameter will not hold the loop
        ! open on a target it is not chasing.
        real(8) :: kkk_sigma_scale_adjuster=0.d0

        ! ITS OWN STEP RATE, 17 September, in the same idiom as b, theta,
        ! omega_c and kappa_phi_d. The author: *"I don't understand why it's so
        ! hard to get it deterministically? It's the share of income in the
        ! bottom 50% of the employed"*.
        !
        ! It was not hard, it was throttled. Every step in n29 was exactly
        ! +0.100 per cent, which is max_rel_step_all=1e-3 binding on every
        ! single block while an adjuster of 5.0 asked for far more. The moment
        ! moved 0.02 per cent a block against a 22 per cent gap: 110 blocks,
        ! five hours, for something the response itself can do quickly.
        !
        ! AND THE RESPONSE IS STRONG. When the grid rebuild first landed, sd of
        ! log y fell 29 per cent, 0.908 to 0.640, and the moment moved 60 per
        ! cent, 0.139 to 0.223. An elasticity near two. The parameter was never
        ! the weak link.
        real(8) :: kkk_sigma_scale_max_rel_step=0.02d0
        ! WHAT kkk_s_deviation_bottom BUYS, and why it is the KKK grid's version of
        ! y_grid_kappa. The bottom node's mass is the normal mass below
        ! -kkk_s_deviation_bottom + half a spacing, so widening the grid pushes
        ! mass off the bottom node: 3.00 gives 0.621 per cent, 3.25 gives 0.318,
        ! 3.50 gives 0.155. The design target from the bands is 0.198.
        ! It is the instrument for how many households are poor enough to be
        ! priced out of the cheapest rental, exactly as y_grid_kappa was on the
        ! quantile grid.

        ! THE CROSS SECTIONAL SD OF LOG PRODUCTIVITY AT AGE ONE, on whichever
        ! grid is live. It is sd_permanent_productivity under the quantile grid
        ! and sqrt(var(1)) from the recursion above under KKK, and the newborn
        ! draw needs whichever one it is: create_newborn_employment_probability
        ! integrates a lognormal over the node intervals and would otherwise use
        ! a width the grid does not have. Filled where the grid is built.
        real(8) :: sd_log_y_newborn=0

        real(8) :: relative_share_of_leisure=(1-perceived_probability_lower)*8.5+perceived_probability_lower*3.1
        
        real(8) :: distance_relative_share_of_leisure
        real(8) :: relative_share_of_leisure_adjuster=(1-freeze_to_three_block)*((1-freeze_relative_share_of_leisure)*(5*10.d0**(-1)))
        real(8), parameter :: relative_share_of_leisure_adjuster_start=5*10.d0**(-1)
        real(8) :: previous_gap_relative_share_of_leisure=0
        integer :: have_gap_relative_share_of_leisure=0
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_relative_share_of_leisure=10.d0**(-3)
        
        real(8) :: relative_share_of_addiction=0.13
        real(8) :: distance_relative_share_of_addiction
        ! ZERO: theta is pinned at 0.16.  See its declaration.
        ! REMOVED 16 September: this was a DECOY. It was declared and never
        ! read -- the update at line 47435 multiplies
        ! relative_share_of_addiction_ADJ, one letter different -- so every
        ! reader and the gaps table checked this, saw zero, and reported theta
        ! HELD while it stepped every block and drifted 0.21 -> 0.20461 across
        ! n03's 277 blocks. The author caught it from the value moving. Deleting
        ! it rather than leaving it to trap the next reader; theta is held by
        ! hold_theta=1, which zeroes the _adj that is actually used.
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_relative_share_of_addiction=10.d0**(-3)
                
        ! LAMBDA, THE ADDICTION HAZARD, AND IT IS CALIBRATED NOW. This is the
        ! probability of acquiring the disorder in a period given that the
        ! household uses, so it is what the note's internal table calls lambda and
        ! matches to the substance use disorder rate. It used to be a parameter,
        ! a compile time constant, and the value it held was 0.037.
        !
        ! THAT NUMBER IS A PREVALENCE, NOT A HAZARD, which is how the model came
        ! to put 32.6 per cent of adults in the disorder. NSDUH gives a disorder
        ! rate of 3.68 per cent for adults 26 and over, and 0.037 is that figure
        ! written in as an ANNUAL onset probability. Over sixty years of adult
        ! life against a recovery rate of 0.025 it compounds: in steady state the
        ! flow balance onset*experimenters = recovery*addicted pins the ratio of
        ! the two at 0.037/0.025 = 1.48, and the model delivered 1.11 once deaths
        ! and clean newborns are allowed for. Prevalence was the arithmetic of two
        ! constants rather than anything the solver chose.
        !
        ! The onset that DELIVERS 3.68 per cent, at the share who use and that
        ! recovery rate, is near 0.0016, a factor of twenty three smaller. That is
        ! the starting value; the bracket finds the rest.
        !
        ! Sign: a higher hazard means more of the people who use acquire the
        ! disorder, so prevalence RISES in it and the update subtracts the gap.
        real(8) :: probability_of_becoming_addicted_if_experiment=&
            (1-perceived_probability_lower)*0.0016+perceived_probability_lower*0.062
        real(8) :: probability_of_becoming_addicted_adjuster=5*10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: distance_probability_of_becoming_addicted
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_probability_of_becoming_addicted=10.d0**(-3)

        ! NSDUH 2024, adults 26 and over, MODERATE OR SEVERE drug use disorder,
        ! svyrdudany in (2,3) on the file in data/. Weighted share 0.0454.
        !
        ! It was 0.0368, which matched no cut of that variable. Together with the
        ! old participation rate it implied that 72 per cent of users carry a
        ! disorder; the survey's own split of mild against moderate-or-severe
        ! puts that at 45 per cent, and the two rates have to come from the same
        ! variable or the weights built from them mean nothing.
        ! RAISED 18 PER CENT WITH b, 18 September, on the author's *"The Sud to
        ! user ratio stays the same so that too moves at 18% up"*.
        !
        ! 0.0368*1.18 = 0.043424, and the ratio the onset is bracketed on is
        ! unchanged: 0.043424/0.10502 = 0.4135, the same as 0.0368/0.089, which
        ! is the author's *"SUD has to be about 41% of b"*.
        !
        ! WHY BOTH HAD TO MOVE. The benchmark is now the PRE-reform economy, so
        ! every LEVEL target that scales with drug use sits 18 per cent above its
        ! data value and the omega_c search brings the whole drug block down onto
        ! the data at once. Left at 0.0368, the onset would have been fitted to a
        ! post-reform disorder rate inside a pre-reform economy, and lambda_d
        ! would have landed 18 per cent BELOW the data after the search -- which
        ! is the recalibration the scheme exists to avoid.
        !
        ! THE RATIO TARGETS ARE NOT SCALED and should not be: tau_u and the SUD
        ! share of the homeless are both shares OF users, so they are
        ! approximately invariant when the level moves. Approximately: the
        ! homeless population does not fall by 18 per cent when drug use does, so
        ! sud_homeless will drift, and that drift is a prediction of the
        ! experiment rather than a calibration error.
        real(8), parameter :: sud_rate_data=0.043424

        ! THE ELEVEN THE NOTE CALLS CALIBRATED AND THE CODE HELD FIXED.
