        real(8), parameter :: voucher_coverage_data=0.266
        ! RETARGETED TO 0.139 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 1 September.
        !
        ! It was 0.0485, the U-3 unemployment rate, which is a PERSON level rate
        ! with the labour force as its denominator. This is a HOUSEHOLD model and
        ! model_fraction_unemployed counts every working-age household with
        ! emp_of /= 1 -- that is emp_of 3 on UI and emp_of 2 with nothing --
        ! over all working-age households. Those are different numerators and
        ! different denominators, and one state space cannot satisfy both.
        !
        ! The comparable figure is the one already printed beside
        ! "DIAG households with no wage income, working age": SCF 2022 puts 13.9
        ! per cent of households aged 26 to 64 with NO wage income. That share
        ! already contains the unemployed, so it is 0.139 and not 0.139 plus a
        ! separate unemployment rate; adding them would count the same households
        ! twice.
        !
        ! What this buys: at 0.0485 the model had only about five per cent of
        ! working-age households without wage income to draw the homeless from,
        ! against 54 per cent of the homeless being out of work in the data. The
        ! note at probability_of_ui says the same thing from the other side --
        ! "homelessness could only ever be drawn from the 5.7 per cent
        ! temporarily unemployed on UI, never from households with nothing".
        !
        ! What it does NOT buy, and this is measured rather than assumed:
        ! joblessness is currently PROTECTIVE here, the homelessness rate among
        ! the unemployed being 0.43 times the working-age rate. Enlarging the
        ! pool does not by itself put anyone on the street.
        ! 4.9 PER CENT, on the author's instruction of 2 September. It was
        ! 0.139, which is the SCF 2022 share of households aged 26-64 with NO
        ! WAGE INCOME -- the figure the diagnostic at 5969 is labelled with --
        ! and the author's reason for it then was "those are people not
        ! working". 4.9 is the conventional unemployment rate.
        !
        ! WORTH CHECKING BEFORE THIS IS RELIED ON, and not checked here: whether
        ! model_fraction_unemployed is the same OBJECT as a published
        ! unemployment rate. It is a share of the model's population in the
        ! unemployed state, and the model has no separate participation margin
        ! for it, so it may be nearer the no-wage-income share than the BLS rate.
        ! If so the two figures are both right about different things and the
        ! model's counterpart is the 13.9. The moment stays scored either way;
        ! this note is about which number it should be scored against.
        ! BACK TO 0.139 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 3 September: *"I think maybe
        ! we need to have more unemployment. Can we revert to all those
        ! unemployed and not in the labor force? We had 13.9% I think. That is
        ! better than 4.9%"*.
        !
        ! The note above already said this and flagged it as unchecked: "whether
        ! model_fraction_unemployed is the same OBJECT as a published
        ! unemployment rate... it may be nearer the no-wage-income share than the
        ! BLS rate. If so the two figures are both right about different things
        ! and the model's counterpart is the 13.9." It is checked now, and it is.
        ! model_fraction_unemployed counts every working-age household with
        ! emp_of /= 1 -- on UI, or with nothing -- over all working-age
        ! households. That is a HOUSEHOLD share with no participation margin, so
        ! its data counterpart is the SCF 2022 share of households aged 26 to 64
        ! with NO WAGE INCOME, 13.9 per cent, not the U-3 person rate of 4.9.
        !
        ! IT IS REPORT-ONLY FOR A SINGLE POINT. The one other use of this
        ! constant is the separation-rate adjuster in the outer calibration loop,
        ! which run_one_point.sh does not run: pi_eu sits at 0.09185 with the
        ! handed-in probability_becoming_unemployed of 0.0122, unadjusted. So no
        ! equilibrium object moves and the archive pools across the change.
        !
        ! The gap goes from +87.5 per cent against 0.049 to -33.9 against 0.139.
        ! RETARGETED TO 0.049 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 4 September, after the
        ! saturation diagnosis below. Their words: *"maybe we should stay with an
        ! unemployment rate of 4.9% then, it seems to be a better fit"*, and *"it
        ! would allow more reasonable numbers"*.
        !
        ! THE OBJECTION WAS PUT AND THE AUTHOR REAFFIRMED, so it is recorded here
        ! rather than argued again. The model has NO LABOUR FORCE PARTICIPATION
        ! MARGIN -- emp_of is 1 employed, 2 unemployed with nothing, 3 on UI, and
        ! there is no out-of-the-labour-force state -- so unemployed over
        ! (employed + unemployed) IS what this moment already computes. In the data
        ! roughly 38 per cent of the working age population is out of the labour
        ! force and the model counts all of them as unemployed, which is why the
        ! honest counterpart was the SCF household no-wage-income share of 13.9.
        ! At 4.9 the model will put a wage earner in about 95 per cent of
        ! working-age households against 86 in the data, so household joblessness
        ! is understated roughly threefold.
        !
        ! WHAT IT BUYS, and it is not small. At 0.139 the only instrument was a
        ! separation rate that had to reach 0.055, where min(pbu*kappa_uem, 1)
        ! puts EVERY mentally ill worker at certain unemployment and employment
        ! given SMI collapses to 1.1 per cent against its own 30 per cent target --
        ! a moment that is computed and NOT scored, so nothing would have caught
        ! it. At 0.049 the separation rate falls to about 0.0032, nothing
        ! saturates, and the SMI employment margin becomes economics again.
        ! 0.049 -> 0.1393, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION of 5 September: *"maybe we
        ! need to bring the unemployment rate back up to 13.9%?"*. This RESTORES the
        ! earlier target and the block above explains why it was abandoned; that
        ! reason no longer holds.
        !
        ! pi_eu IS THE HOUSEHOLD NO-WAGE-INCOME SHARE, not the labour force
        ! unemployment rate: the model prints "households with no wage income,
        ! working age" and it is the SAME NUMBER as the pi_eu moment, to every
        ! digit. So 0.1393, the SCF share of working-age households with no wage
        ! earner, is the concept this moment actually measures and 0.049 was the
        ! wrong quantity attached to the right diagnostic.
        !
        ! WHY IT WAS BACKED OFF, AND WHY THAT IS FIXED. At 0.1393 the separation
        ! rate must reach about 0.055, where min(pbu*kappa_uem,1) saturates and puts
        ! EVERY mentally ill worker at certain unemployment: employment given SMI
        ! collapses to 1.1 per cent against its 30 per cent target. The note above
        ! says the danger was that this is *"a moment that is computed and NOT
        ! scored, so nothing would have caught it"*.
        !
        ! kappa_uem IS SCORED NOW, reconciled into next_batch.py's loss on
        ! 5 September, and it owns its own moment with a rule that LOWERS the
        ! parameter when SMI employment falls below target. The saturation can no
        ! longer happen silently: it is one of the nineteen gaps the walk reads
        ! every iteration.
        ! 0.1393 -> 0.049, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION of 6 September: *"Maybe
        ! what we need is a lower unemployment rate? The 4.9%...would that help?"*
        ! and, when the concern below was put to them, *"Let the method run with
        ! the new unemployment rate target"*.
        !
        ! THE ARITHMETIC THAT MOTIVATES IT IS SOUND. At the 0.268 housing-weight
        ! point the model has 14.06 per cent of working-age households with no
        ! wage income and 29.51 per cent of THEM homeless, and 0.1406 x 0.2951 =
        ! 0.0415 against a measured h_1 of 0.0582. Cutting the pool to 4.9 per
        ! cent at the same conditional rate gives 0.0145 against the annual target
        ! of 0.01097 -- from 5.3 times out to 1.3. It works through the population
        ! at risk rather than the price of the street, so unlike every other lever
        ! measured on 6 September it does not drag emp_homeless down with it.
        !
        ! THE CONCERN, RECORDED BECAUSE IT WAS RAISED AND OVERRULED. This moment
        ! is the HOUSEHOLD NO-WAGE-INCOME SHARE, not the labour-force unemployment
        ! rate: the model prints "households with no wage income, working age" and
        ! it equals the pi_eu moment to five digits. 0.1393 is the SCF share of
        ! working-age households with no wage earner; 0.049 is the BLS
        ! labour-force rate, a different denominator and a different universe that
        ! excludes everyone out of the labour force. Those households are the ones
        ! this model puts at risk of homelessness. The author moved the target the
        ! OTHER way on 5 September for exactly this reason, and the comment block
        ! that used to sit here said 0.049 was "the wrong quantity attached to the
        ! right diagnostic".
        !
        ! WHAT WOULD ANSWER THE CONCERN WITHOUT MOVING THE TARGET is the
        ! CONDITIONAL rate: 29.51 per cent of no-wage households homeless is the
        ! indefensible number, not the size of the pool. Eight per cent there
        ! gives h_1 = 0.011 with the pool untouched. replacement_rate_ui was made
        ! calibratable on 6 September precisely to attack it and has not been
        ! swept from a good base.
        real(8), parameter :: unemployment_rate_data=0.049
        real(8), parameter :: unemployment_rate_sud_data=0.1625

        ! THETA'S OPERATIVE ADJUSTER, AND THE ONE THE FREEZE HAS TO NAME.
        !
        ! relative_share_of_addiction_ADJUSTER is a different variable, declared
        ! elsewhere and not used in theta's update. Freezing that one held
        ! nothing: theta ran live through every run of 11 September while being
        ! reported as held, moving 0.267182 to 0.26656 and taking the drug
        ! spending share from -2.5 per cent to +8.2. Two names one syllable
        ! apart, and the only way to tell them apart is to follow the one the
        ! update actually reads.
        !
        ! Held now on the author's instruction: *"I think we can hold theta
        ! constant in the full calibration"*.
        real(8) :: relative_share_of_addiction_adj=(1-hold_theta)*(5*10.d0**(-2))
        real(8) :: distance_relative_share_of_addiction_1
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_relative_share_of_addiction_1=10.d0**(-3)

        ! SIZED TO b's CURRENT SCALE, 15 September, so that the damping below
        ! can actually engage. The 0.2 was written when b sat near 0.02 to 0.25
        ! -- the comment beside the update says so, "an adjuster of 0.2 asks for
        ! 0.002, about a tenth of b". b is now 0.007, and at that scale 0.2
        ! asks for a step of 26 per cent where the per-loop ceiling is 2.
        !
        ! THE CAP THEN BINDS EVERY LOOP AND THE DAMPING IS DEAD CODE. Halving
        ! the adjuster on a sign flip does nothing while the raw step is ten
        ! times the ceiling: it takes about seven flips before the step drops
        ! under the cap, and every flip costs an overshoot. Measured in j03 --
        ! participation went 0.110 -> 0.071 in ONE loop, straight over a target
        ! of 0.089 and a gate band of +/-10 per cent, because 2 per cent of b
        ! is about 15 per cent of participation at an elasticity of -4.8.
        !
        ! 0.008 makes the raw step about 2 per cent at the gap j03 had, so the
        ! ceiling binds only at the widest gaps and the damping owns everything
        ! near the target -- which is the behaviour the update's own comment
        ! says is wanted, "the cap is expected to bind while the gap is wide and
        ! to stop binding as it closes".
        ! RAISED 17 September, from 2e-3. The author: *"And b seems way too
        ! low"*, on a run where b sat at 0.026 against 0.089.
        !
        ! WHY NOW AND NOT BEFORE. The cooldown windows in n32 measured this
        ! bracket as a passenger -- b tripled and halved with the parameter
        ! frozen -- and the honest reading then was that no step size closes a
        ! ratio that large. What changed is the rest of the table. Eleven
        ! moments are now inside eight per cent, including sigma_y pinned, h_2
        ! at 4.3 and c_bar, pi_v, pi_eu, beta and tau_ell at or under one, so
        ! the general-equilibrium churn that was swamping b is much smaller
        ! than it was. A stronger bracket has a chance it did not have this
        ! morning.
        !
        ! It is still not a fix for the seven-node grid, which is what makes
        ! participation knife-edge -- adjacent productivity nodes are 135 per
        ! cent apart and three of them hold 88 per cent of the mass. That
        ! remains the author's call.
        real(8) :: addiction_scalar_adjuster=1*10.d0**(-2)
        real(8) :: distance_addiction_scalar
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_addiction_scalar=10.d0**(-3)

        ! B OSCILLATES WITHOUT DAMPING, because participation is far more elastic
        ! in it than a fixed adjuster assumes. Measured: b rose 48.7 per cent and
        ! participation fell from 0.176 to 0.0136, past a target of 0.0513 and by
        ! a factor of thirteen. An undamped rule then reverses and overshoots the
        ! other way for ever, and it takes the SUD rate with it, because the
        ! households who convert are drawn from the ones who use.
        !
        ! Halving the step whenever the gap changes sign turns the rule into a
        ! bisection, which converges on any monotone response however steep.
        ! Growing it back slowly when the sign holds keeps it from creeping once
        ! the oscillation has been squeezed out. Same idiom as delta_d's.
        ! AND THE CEILING THE GROWTH RULE RESTORES IT TO, for the same reason:
        ! left at 0.2 the adjuster climbs back at 1.2 a loop to a value the cap
        ! swallows whole, undoing the damping every time the gap holds its sign.
        real(8), parameter :: addiction_scalar_adjuster_start=2*10.d0**(-3)
        real(8), parameter :: addiction_scalar_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: addiction_scalar_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: addiction_scalar_floor=10.d0**(-4)

        ! WAIT FOR PARTICIPATION TO SETTLE BEFORE STEPPING b AGAIN.
        !
        ! THE DEFECT, MEASURED. f26 froze every parameter at block 13 and
        ! watched participation come to rest:
        !
        !     block 13  0.0878   <- the step is taken here
        !     block 14  0.0767      -13 per cent, still moving
        !     block 15  0.0760      -0.9
        !     block 16  0.0758      -0.26
        !     block 17  0.0757      -0.13, at rest
        !
        ! FOUR BLOCKS to settle after one step. The rule steps EVERY block, so
        ! it reads a moment still in transit and steps again on it, and again,
        ! each step compounding into a map that has not finished responding to
        ! the last one. That is how participation ran from 0.235 to 0.006 in
        ! four separate runs while the instrument was trying to stop it.
        !
        ! It is also exactly the error this calibration's own notes warn about
        ! in the human reader -- a moment read mid-transit is not a measurement,
        ! and the settling test needs the moment flat across several blocks. The
        ! update rule was never held to the standard the notes hold me to.
        !
        ! THE FIX, and it changes WHEN the rule fires and nothing else. The
        ! author's additive form is untouched: parameter + adjuster times gap,
        ! capped. It simply does not fire while the moment is still moving.
        ! A COOLDOWN, NOT A TEST ON THE MOMENT. The first draft held b whenever
        ! participation had moved more than two per cent since the last block,
        ! and that was wrong for a reason j09 showed in eight blocks: during the
        ! cold-start transient participation moves three to nine per cent a block
        ! ON ITS OWN, so the gate held forever and b stepped twice in eight
        ! blocks while participation fell 0.235 -> 0.193 regardless. It did not
        ! prevent anything; it disarmed the instrument.
        !
        ! The measurement was about what happens AFTER A STEP -- f26 froze at
        ! block 13 and participation took four blocks to come to rest. A cooldown
        ! implements that. A test on the moment's own movement does not, because
        ! the moment moves for reasons that have nothing to do with b.
        ! SHARED BETWEEN b AND THE ONSET, because they act on the SAME margin.
        ! j10 gave b the cooldown and left the onset alone: the onset then
        ! stepped +0.5 per cent every block for fourteen blocks in a row, +6.8
        ! per cent in total, while b took three steps, and participation went
        ! 0.226 -> 0.088 -> 0.036 anyway. Raising the onset DETERS USE -- that is
        ! the measured negative-elasticity channel recorded above -- so it pushes
        ! participation down on exactly the extensive margin b is trying to hold.
        ! Throttling one instrument while the other pushes unchecked buys
        ! nothing.
        !
        ! So the counter is shared: neither steps unless the margin has had
        ! blocks_since_drug_step blocks to settle since EITHER of them last
        ! moved.
        integer :: blocks_since_drug_step=0
        ! THE ONSET'S OWN COOLDOWN COUNTER. See the gate for why it cannot share
        ! b's: b increments and resets the shared one after the onset has already
        ! read it, so the onset never saw it expire and never stepped once.
        integer :: blocks_since_onset_step=0
        ! TWO, NOT FIVE, for the same reason the adjuster was raised. Five
        ! blocks of waiting made sense while every step was read through a
        ! refilling drug stock; with the rest of the table settled the bracket
        ! needs to be able to answer a swing inside the swing's own timescale,
        ! which n32 measured at three to four blocks.
        integer, parameter :: b_step_cooldown=2
        ! The band theta and b are held in. See the two update rules for why
        ! they are absolute rather than only trust regions.
        ! THE CEILING IS SET BY THE BREAK POINT, not by taste. The stylized
        ! model's appendix scan finds that by theta = 0.23 the mortality channel
        ! no longer reverses the income gradient and the average user stops
        ! being poorer than average: a drug that valuable is not deterred by a
        ! death that arrives later. Above that point the model cannot produce
        ! the fact it exists to explain, whatever the death penalty is set to,
        ! which is why sweeping kappa_phi_d at theta = 0.667 moved nothing.
        !
        ! 0.25 rather than 0.23, because the break was measured in the two
        ! period model and this one is annual over sixty years, so its own break
        ! need not sit in the same place. A calibration that wants to go above
        ! 0.25 is then reporting that it cannot hit the spending share without
        ! losing the gradient, which is worth seeing rather than hiding.
        real(8), parameter :: theta_lo=0.05d0
        ! RAISED TO 0.30 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION. The reasoning above still
        ! stands and the guard that enforces it is unchanged: theta may not rise
        ! while the income gradient is inverted, so the extra slack is available
        ! only where the model still puts the users at the bottom.
        ! RAISED AGAIN, TO 0.5, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION. The guard above is
        ! unchanged: theta may not rise while the income gradient is inverted,
        ! so the extra slack is available only where the model still puts the
        ! users at the bottom. The ceiling also has to clear theta's OWN
        ! declared value, which is 0.4265: at 0.30 the parameter started above
        ! the highest value the calibration was allowed to give it and the
        ! clamp pulled it down on the first read, so the declared value was
        ! never the starting point it was written to be.
        ! 0.7 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, AND SO 0.55 IS A VALUE NOT A CLAMP. The handover clamps to this
        ! ceiling, so asking for 0.55 with the ceiling at 0.50 would have set
        ! 0.50 and reported success: the same silent bind that put theta at
        ! 0.29357 for a whole calibration when the ceiling was 0.30. The
        ! headroom is deliberate -- a ceiling exactly at the value being tried
        ! cannot tell a converged parameter from a pinned one.
        real(8), parameter :: theta_hi=0.70d0

        ! HOW FAR THETA MAY MOVE IN ONE LOOP, AS A FRACTION OF ITSELF.
        !
        ! On the author's instruction of 11 September: *"we might need to
        ! adjust theta together with the rest of the parameters, but probably
        ! very very slowly, like 0.1% each time"*, and the reason is the trap
        ! documented at theta's update. The spending share is drug spending
        ! over INCOME, so as b rises and participation falls the users who are
        ! left are rich, the share falls for that reason alone, and the update
        ! reads it as a call to raise theta. Theta then chases b instead of
        ! settling, and the pair can walk together for a long time.
        !
        ! THE TRUST REGION ALREADY IN THE UPDATE DOES NOT BIND. It caps the
        ! step at halving or doubling, which at theta = 0.27 needs a gap of
        ! 5.4 while the gap is clamped to 1. So the only limit was the
        ! coefficient: 0.05 times the gap, which is 1.5 per cent of theta at a
        ! gap of 0.08 and 0.4 per cent at a gap of 0.02.
        !
        ! 0.001, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION OF 11 September: "i think the
        ! adjuster on theta should only move it by 0.1% between iterations at
        ! most". Asked once with the arithmetic attached -- at 0.001 theta moves
        ! 0.00027 a loop from 0.27, so 0.27 to 0.40 is 480 loops -- and
        ! reaffirmed. So it stands at 0.001.
        !
        ! THE WALL CLOCK WAS MEASURED ON 11 September AND MY ESTIMATE WAS WRONG
        ! BY A FACTOR OF EIGHT. A loop is 1.5 minutes, not twelve: the COLD first
        ! solve is 3 to 4 minutes and every loop after it warm starts from the
        ! converged value function and distribution. Measured on the 02:46 run,
        ! loops 8, 14, 20 and 22 at 03:02, 03:12, 03:21 and 03:23 -- fourteen
        ! loops in twenty-one minutes. So 480 loops is TWELVE HOURS, not four
        ! days, and the author's 0.1 per cent is affordable overnight.
        !
        ! WHAT TO WATCH, since the cost of the choice is real: if the trace
        ! shows THETA CAPPED on every loop in the same direction for tens of
        ! iterations, theta is not converging slowly, it is travelling, and the
        ! loop's other parameters are settling against a theta that has not
        ! arrived. It is a HANDOVER parameter, so raising it costs no rebuild.
        real(8) :: theta_max_rel_step=0.001d0

        ! OMEGA_C'S OWN STEP CAP, 0.5 per cent a loop.  Author, 12 September:
        ! *"just make the steps in omega_c very small, maybe 0.1% between
        ! iterations"*, then *"we can do 0.3% if that works ok"* once the cost
        ! of 0.1 was put to them.  The global max_rel_step_all is 1 per cent and
        ! omega_c's first measured step under it was +0.2997 per cent, 0.09 to
        ! 0.090270, so 0.3 is very nearly what it was already doing and this cap
        ! is a ceiling rather than a brake.
        !
        ! WHY 0.1 WAS NOT TAKEN.  The first converged child gap was +7.97 per
        ! cent against a target of +18.34, which wants omega_c near 0.18 rather
        ! than 0.09.  Doubling at 0.1 per cent a loop is about 700 loops; at 0.3
        ! it is about 230.  At 0.1 this cap would have been the binding
        ! constraint on the whole calibration rather than a safety margin.
        ! 0.05 PER CENT A LOOP, on the author's instruction of 13 September:
        ! *"if we adjust omega_c at about 0.05% per iteration"*.  It was 0.005.
        ! This is the rate that worked in the stylized model, where the same
        ! parameter on the same moment converged from -36.6 per cent to exact
        ! over about twelve hundred passes without disturbing anything else.
        ! A TENTH OF A PER CENT, on the author's instruction of 14 September.
        ! It was 0.05 from their instruction of 13 September; they have doubled
        ! the three slow instruments and halved the global rate, so the ordering
        ! is now 0.1 for the three and 0.5 for everything else.
        real(8) :: omega_max_rel_step=0.001d0
        real(8) :: theta_proposed_1
        real(8) :: theta_capped_1

        ! GAMMA IS TIED TO THETA FOR NOW, on the author's instruction:
        ! gamma = 0.28*(1-theta), so housing takes 28 per cent of whatever the
        ! drug does not. The two are exponents in the same Cobb Douglas bundle,
        ! so calibrating them independently lets the housing share drift as
        ! theta moves and the pair stop summing to anything meaningful. At
        ! theta = 0.38 this is 0.1736, and the simulation should return about 27
        ! per cent of income spent on housing.
        !
        ! It is a TIE and not a reparameterisation: gamma's own bracket is
        ! skipped while tie_gamma_to_theta is one, and the author will free it
        ! again. apply_gamma_tie is called wherever theta can change -- after
        ! the state is read, after a handover and after theta's own update --
        ! rather than once at setup, because theta moves in all three places.
        ! FREED, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION. gamma was the one targeted moment
        ! with no instrument: this parameter was pinned at 0.28*(1-theta), so
        ! nothing could move housing's share of expenditure.
        integer, parameter :: tie_gamma_to_theta=0
        real(8), parameter :: gamma_share_of_non_drug=0.28d0
        ! B'S CEILING HAD NO ARGUMENT BEHIND IT AND IT BOUND. At 0.05 the
        ! bracket sat exactly on it: participation came out at 76.0 per cent
        ! against a target of 8.90, the update asked for b to rise by a factor of
        ! 2.5, and the ceiling refused every time. Nothing else in the
        ! calibration can be read while that is true, because with three quarters
        ! of the population using, the users ARE the population: the average
        ! income of a user came out at 0.82 of the mean and of a household with
        ! the disorder at 1.007, which is not a finding about the income gradient
        ! but an arithmetic consequence of the extensive margin being wrong.
        !
        ! ONE RATHER THAN A GUESS AT THE RIGHT VALUE. theta's ceiling is set by an
        ! economic break point, the value above which mortality stops reversing
        ! the gradient, and it should bind when the model cannot do its job. b's
        ! should only bind where the model stops making sense, and that is where
        ! the drug is worth so little at the first unit that nobody uses at any
        ! income: the marginal value of the first unit is theta/b, so at b = 1 and
        ! theta in [0.05, 0.25] it is 0.05 to 0.25, of the same order as the
        ! marginal utility of consumption. Above that there is no interior
        ! participation to find. Where inside the band participation is matched is
        ! for the bracket to discover, not for this line to decide.
        ! THE FLOOR WAS 1e-4 AND b HAS TO GO WELL BELOW IT. b is the Stone-Geary
        ! endowment, so the marginal value of the first unit is theta/b: a
        ! SMALLER b makes starting to use more attractive, not less. With the SUD
        ! mortality multiplier flat at 8 and on the hazard, nobody in the model
        ! uses at any age, and b is the parameter that decides whether anyone
        ! uses at all. It cannot restore participation from a floor four orders
        ! of magnitude above where it needs to sit; the update's own comment puts
        ! b at order 1e-8, which is why its step is multiplicative.
        ! THE FLOOR IS 10^-10 AND NOT 10^-8, so that it cannot bind at the value
        ! the author expects b to take. The marginal value of the first unit is
        ! theta/b, so participation FALLS in b and the floor is the END OF THE
        ! SCALE WITH THE MOST USE ON IT: a floor sitting inside the plausible
        ! range refuses exactly the moves the calibration needs and reports
        ! nothing, which is the failure the ceiling had at 0.05 and which is
        ! recorded a few lines below. 10^-8 was inside that range, being the
        ! value b was expected to need; 10^-10 is two decades clear of it.
        ! THE AUTHOR LOWERED THIS ON 29 AUGUST: b may be as small as it likes so
        ! long as it is strictly positive. It was 1e-10. It has never bound --
        ! the search sits near 0.017 and b's elasticity on the drug user share
        ! is about minus fifteen, so 1e-8 would put essentially every household
        ! on drugs -- but a floor that cannot bind costs nothing and a floor
        ! that silently binds costs a mislabelled point, so it goes down.
        real(8), parameter :: b_lo=1.d0*10.d0**(-30)
        real(8), parameter :: b_hi=1.0d0

        real(8) :: gap_addiction_scalar_1=0,previous_gap_addiction_scalar=0
        ! b'S OWN RATE, ten times the two slow brackets and half the global cap.
        !
        ! The author, 13 September: *"allow b to move at 0.5% between
        ! iterations"*.  b went through plain cap_step, which reads
        ! max_rel_step_all -- one per cent under freeze_to_three_block, not the
        ! half a per cent the comment on kappa_phi_d_level_adjuster claimed --
        ! so b was the FASTEST of the three, not the slowest.  That is the wrong
        ! way round: b is the instrument for the drug participation rate and the
        ! two slow brackets are the ones that were outrunning it.
        !
        ! Naming the rate here rather than moving max_rel_step_all keeps it off
        ! the other nineteen calibrated parameters, for the reason set out at
        ! cap_step_rel.
        ! HALF A PER CENT, which the author's 14 September rule gives it as one
        ! of "the rest".  Unchanged from their 13 September instruction, and now
        ! equal to the global rate rather than five times it.
        ! 0.1 PER CENT, 14 September: *"No parameter should move fast"*.  Was
        ! 0.005, and b was measured stepping the full +0.50 per cent a loop in
        ! run60b, the fastest of any ordinary instrument there.
        ! THE DRUG BLOCK MOVES AT 0.5 PER CENT, 14 September, on the author's
        ! *"ok raise the drug block instruments back to 0.5%"*.
        !
        ! WHY THE BLOCK AND NOT EVERYTHING.  What exploded this morning was the
        ! value-of-life floor, which assigned aleph +104 per cent in one step;
        ! that is fixed separately and the floor no longer touches aleph at all.
        ! The 0.1 per cent cap then became the binding constraint on the search
        ! instead of a safety measure: over nineteen loops of stage 1 the drug
        ! users moment fell 83.8 per cent while its instrument moved 0.70, about
        ! one hundred and twentieth of the speed.  aleph, h_1, kappa_uem and
        ! everything else on the global cap stay at 0.1 per cent.
        real(8), parameter :: drug_block_max_rel_step=0.005d0
        ! ITS OWN CAP, 2 PER CENT, NOT THE DRUG BLOCK'S HALF A PER CENT.
        ! Measured 15 September across six runs: with b five times its target
        ! (0.449 against 0.089) this instrument crept 3.000e-3 -> 3.045e-3 in
        ! three loops, half a per cent a loop, the cap binding every loop. At
        ! that rate it needs about 280 loops merely to cross its own measured
        ! bracket, 0.003 to 0.012, and 140 to double.
        !
        ! AND IT IS BEING OUTRUN BY THE EQUILIBRIUM IT IS STEERING. In the
        ! 16-loop run at 0.018 the price moved 3 per cent in total while b fell
        ! by a factor of 25, from 0.103 to 0.0042, and settled on the dead
        ! branch. An instrument that slow is not steering b, it is watching it.
        !
        ! THREE TENTHS OF A PER CENT, AND THE TWO PER CENT ABOVE WAS WRONG.
        ! Two per cent was set from the FAR-FIELD elasticity, about -1 across
        ! runs and -4.8 over the early loops of j03. The elasticity near the
        ! abstention threshold is nothing like that, and it ACCELERATES:
        !
        !     loop 7->8    b_SG +2.0%   participation 0.168 -> 0.142   e = -8.5
        !     loop 8->9    b_SG +2.0%   participation 0.142 -> 0.110   e = -12.5
        !     loop 9->10   b_SG +2.0%   participation 0.110 -> 0.070   e = -22.6
        !
        ! At -22.6 a two per cent step moves participation THIRTY-SIX per cent
        ! while the kappa gate's band is twenty per cent wide, so a single step
        ! clears the band from either side and the run can never be inside it.
        ! j03 and j04 both crossed 0.110 -> 0.070 in one loop, and j04 did it
        ! with the adjuster already reduced, because at a gap of 0.0215 the raw
        ! step was still 2.4 per cent and the CAP bound one last time.
        !
        ! THE CONDITION THE CAP MUST MEET: one step may not cross the band. At
        ! e = -22.6 that is 20/22.6 = 0.9 per cent, and the elasticity was still
        ! steepening at the last measurement, so three tenths leaves room for it
        ! to reach -60 before a single step can jump the band again.
        !
        ! AND THE DESCENT IS ONE-WAY, which is why this matters more than speed.
        ! Participation is hysteretic: j03 reversed both instruments for five
        ! loops and it kept falling. There is one pass at the target.
        real(8) :: addiction_scalar_max_rel_step=0.003d0
        integer :: have_gap_addiction_scalar=0
        real(8) :: lambda_secant_slope_1=0

        ! LAMBDA'S CLAMP IS A TRUST REGION AND HAS TO SHRINK LIKE ONE. The secant
        ! is clipped to a factor of four either way, which stops a bad step but
        ! does not stop a ringing one: the SUD rate went 0.0513 over target,
        ! 0.0112 under, then 0.1824 over, five times the target, because each
        ! secant step lands on the clamp and reverses. Halving the factor on a
        ! sign flip makes it a bisection in the same way aleph's radius is, and
        ! the floor keeps it a real step rather than a stall.
        real(8) :: lambda_clamp=1.01
        real(8), parameter :: lambda_clamp_start=1.01
        real(8), parameter :: lambda_clamp_floor=1.001
        real(8) :: gap_lambda_addiction_1=0,previous_gap_lambda_addiction=0
        integer :: have_gap_lambda_addiction=0

        real(8) :: value_of_life_adjuster=1*10.d0**(1)

        ! ALEPH'S TRUST REGION HAS TO SHRINK OR IT IS A TWO CYCLE. The adjuster
        ! is ten and the gap is tens, so the step is hundreds and ALWAYS lands on
        ! the clamp. A fixed clamp then means the rule is not "step by the gap",
        ! it is "multiply by 1.5 or by 0.5", and the sign alternates every loop
        ! because each move overshoots. Measured over five loops: 16.5, 24.75,
        ! 12.375, 18.5625, 9.28125 — ratios of exactly 1.5, 0.5, 1.5, 0.5, with a
        ! net drift down of a quarter every two loops toward the floor.
        !
        ! It costs more than aleph. size_homelessness discards its bracket
        ! whenever the value of life moves more than
        ! bracket_value_of_life_tolerance, because endpoints priced at different
        ! values of life are not a bracket, and aleph moving fifty per cent every
        ! loop discards it every loop. Homelessness slammed 0.041, 0, 0.0798 with
        ! no bracket ever surviving to bisect on.
        !
        ! Halving the RADIUS on a sign flip turns the same rule into a
        ! multiplicative bisection: 1.5 and 0.5 become 1.25 and 0.8, then 1.125
        ! and 0.889, and aleph pins down geometrically instead of ringing.
        ! Growing it back when the sign holds keeps it from creeping once the
        ! ringing has stopped. Same idiom as b's and delta_d's.
        ! ALEPH'S OWN RATE, a tenth of a per cent, on the author's instruction
        ! of 14 September.  It had NO rate of its own and went through cap_step
        ! at the global one, so naming the three slow instruments required
        ! giving this one a cap to be slow with.
        !
        ! NOTE WHAT THIS DOES NOT BOUND: the value-of-life FLOOR is deliberately
        ! uncapped and stays so.  A constraint that stops households preferring
        ! to be dead has to hold at every iterate, not be approached at a tenth
        ! of a per cent a loop, and it announces itself in the log when it binds.
        ! ALEPH AT 0.5 PER CENT, not 1: the author, 15 September, "i think aleph
        ! can go to 0.5%%", "not 1%%". Its gap is -10.7 per cent, so this is about
        ! 23 loops rather than 11.
        ! A QUARTER OF A PER CENT, on the author's instruction of 16 September:
        ! *"aleph should not be updated so fast i think....maybe around 0.25%"*.
        ! It was half a per cent, which they set earlier.
        !
        ! WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE NUMBER SUGGESTS: aleph is SUPERLINEAR in
        ! this parameter. Measured 16 September, value_of_life 58.7 -> 126 took
        ! VSL 39 -> 148, so the elasticity is about 1.9 and a step here lands
        ! nearly twice as hard on the moment. Half a per cent on the parameter is
        ! about one per cent on aleph; a quarter is about a half.
        !
        ! And the parameter is not only a statistic, it is a FLOW UTILITY term --
        ! flow_utility_1 = flow_utility_1 + value_of_life -- so it changes
        ! behaviour as well as the moment. Jumping it from 58.7 to 126 in one
        ! seed put aleph at 148 and knocked b to 0.010, tau_u to 0.999 and the
        ! disorder rate to 0.004 on the way.
        real(8) :: value_of_life_max_rel_step=0.0025d0
        real(8) :: value_of_life_trust=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: value_of_life_trust_start=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: value_of_life_trust_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: value_of_life_trust_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: value_of_life_trust_floor=10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: gap_value_of_life_1=0,previous_gap_value_of_life=0
        integer :: have_gap_value_of_life=0
        real(8) :: distance_value_of_life
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_value_of_life=10.d0**(-2)

        ! LIVE AGAIN. max_food_cost is the allotment once more, so it is
        ! calibrated on aggregate food stamp spending over labour income, which
        ! is what food_stamps_share_data measures.
        ! RELEASED 12 September: the author named c_bar. Was
        ! (1-freeze_to_three_block)*5e-2, zero under the stage-1 freeze.
        ! LIVE AGAIN, same instruction of 13 September.  It was retired with the
        ! no-food-stamp variant, and the vectors we calibrate carry max_food_cost
        ! near 0.026, so the programme is on and its moment had no instrument.
        ! Back to the 5e-2 it carried before the stage-1 freeze.
        ! PINNED, on the author's instruction of 14 September: *"you should
        ! probably also update c bar to get the right food stamps to income
        ! ratio....i think you can almost deterministically find it and then pin
        ! it"*, and then *"yes pin it"*.
        !
        ! SOLVED, NOT SEARCHED.  snap = max(0, cbar - 0.3y), so spending is
        ! S(cbar) = int_0^{cbar/tau} (cbar - tau*y) f(y) dy and the boundary term
        ! vanishes at the eligibility limit, leaving dS/dcbar = mass * recipient
        ! share EXACTLY.  Both factors print every loop.  At the point this was
        ! solved from: mass 50.889, share 0.079280, S 0.179823, output 61.667, so
        ! dS/dcbar = 4.0345 and the local exponent is 1.952.  Spending must rise
        ! x1.4403, which brackets cbar in [0.10488, 0.10663] -- the linear step is
        ! the upper end because S is convex in cbar.  cbar = 0.105 is seeded.
        !
        ! WHY IT MUST BE PINNED RATHER THAN LEFT LIVE.  The author: *"c bar
        ! affects the value of life and if it's too high, you'll choose the
        ! lowest income too low"*.  cbar is a means-tested consumption floor and
        ! the value-of-life constraint clears negative mass by raising
        ! bottom_productivity_level, so the two set the same thing -- what the
        ! poorest household consumes.  Left live they are one equation in two
        ! unknowns and neither is identified.
        ! LIVE AGAIN 15 September: "i think you should include c bar in the live
        ! ones". cbar sat at -49.4 per cent with its only instrument pinned.
        ! The runtime re-zero at apply_first_block_freeze does not reach it:
        ! first_block_only is 0, so that subroutine returns before the assignment.
        ! HELD 16 September. The author: *"i think we have to go back....and
        ! just calibrate the following: aleph, b, kappa, tau_u, SUD homeless,
        ! homeless, SUD"*. Seven moments, seven instruments, everything else
        ! frozen where it stands.
        ! REOPENED 16 September on the author's instruction: "i need you to now
        ! add mu_H, pi_eu, newborn_endowment, pi_v, and cbar", plus "can you
        ! also fix sigma_y?", "set h2 to get 5-7%% cost burdened" and "maybe
        ! you can also adjust kappa_find_smi to hit its target?". Held at zero
        ! for the seven-instrument restriction of 15 September; the value
        ! restored here is the one it carried before that restriction.
        ! PINNED 17 September with the set of six; see discount_factor_adjuster.
        ! It was raised to 1.0 this morning to let c_bar move at all, and it
        ! worked -- c_bar went from 22 per cent off to 0.2 -- so it is pinned at
        ! the value that arrived rather than reverted to the old rate.
        ! RELEASED 18 September, on the author's *"C bar is too high...."*.
        ! Restored to the 5*10^-2 it carried before it was pinned, from git.
        !
        ! IT WAS THE WORST ROW IN THE TABLE AND IT WAS HELD. Food stamps over
        ! income read 0.00943 against 0.0042, 125 per cent over, with nothing
        ! chasing it because I pinned this adjuster earlier in the session.
        !
        ! AND IT IS THE REASON emp_homeless CANNOT MOVE. The allotment is 0.1147
        ! against a cheapest rent of 0.0615, so the benefit ALONE is 1.9 times
        ! the rent and the taper only removes thirty cents per dollar of income.
        ! No level of bottom-node productivity prices out a household that is
        ! handed nearly twice the rent before it earns anything. One pinned
        ! parameter was holding a badly missed moment AND a structurally stuck
        ! one, which is the same lesson beta taught two hours earlier: a pin is
        ! only safe while the economy it was made in is the economy you are in.
        ! GAIN RAISED 18 September, on the author's *"we need to move those
        ! parameters I wrote above much faster so they converge in 15 iterations"*.
        ! Realised step was 0.21 per cent with c_bar 101 per cent off;
        ! 2.40 gives ten per cent.
        ! Raising the STEP CAP did nothing for it: the cap was not binding, the
        ! GAIN was. The gap is measured on one scale and the parameter lives on
        ! another, so the product was a fraction of a per cent a step. Set so the
        ! ten per cent cap governs and the existing damping handles sign flips.
        real(8) :: max_food_cost_adjuster=3.6d0
        real(8) :: distance_max_food_cost
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_max_food_cost=10.d0**(-4)

        ! OFF THE FREEZE, on the same instruction.  Its do while term loses the
        ! freeze guard with it, so the parameter and its distance go live
        ! together -- see the update block, which is now guarded on the adjuster
        ! for the reason max_food_cost's is.
        ! HELD 16 September. The author: *"i think we have to go back....and
        ! just calibrate the following: aleph, b, kappa, tau_u, SUD homeless,
        ! homeless, SUD"*. Seven moments, seven instruments, everything else
        ! frozen where it stands.
        ! REOPENED 16 September on the author's instruction: "i need you to now
        ! add mu_H, pi_eu, newborn_endowment, pi_v, and cbar", plus "can you
        ! also fix sigma_y?", "set h2 to get 5-7%% cost burdened" and "maybe
        ! you can also adjust kappa_find_smi to hit its target?". Held at zero
        ! for the seven-instrument restriction of 15 September; the value
        ! restored here is the one it carried before that restriction.
        ! PINNED 17 September, on the author's question *"I think piv too is
        ! stable, no?"*. They were right and my caution was overdone.
        !
        ! I had held pi_v back because it overshoots to about 0.29 mid-run. The
        ! full n32 path shows that is only the refill and it settles on target:
        !
        !     blk 1  0.01943   blk 8  0.29110   blk 24  0.26610
        !     blk 2  0.11663   blk 16 0.26956   blk 31  0.26542
        !     blk 3  0.24749                    blk 34  0.26329
        !
        ! On 0.266 from block 16 onward, and -0.1 per cent at the end of n24 as
        ! well. The overshoot is a stock filling from empty, not the parameter
        ! being wrong, and it will still happen with the parameter pinned --
        ! it simply will not be chased.
        real(8) :: voucher_probability_adjuster=0.d0
        real(8), parameter :: voucher_probability_adjuster_start=1*10.d0**(-1)
        real(8) :: previous_gap_voucher_probability=0
        integer :: have_gap_voucher_probability=0
        real(8) :: distance_voucher_probability
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_voucher_probability=10.d0**(-3)

        ! RELEASED 12 September with beta, same instruction.  Was
        ! (1-freeze_to_three_block)*(1e-1), zero under the stage-1 freeze, so the
        ! unemployment rate sat at -13.5 per cent unmanaged.  Its do-while term is
        ! released with it.
        ! HELD 16 September. The author: *"i think we have to go back....and
        ! just calibrate the following: aleph, b, kappa, tau_u, SUD homeless,
        ! homeless, SUD"*. Seven moments, seven instruments, everything else
        ! frozen where it stands.
        ! REOPENED 16 September on the author's instruction: "i need you to now
        ! add mu_H, pi_eu, newborn_endowment, pi_v, and cbar", plus "can you
