            6.4761*0.0016
        
        ! THE DRUG MARKET, which the note has had all along and the code did not.
        !
        ! price_of_addictive_good was assigned once and never again: every other
        ! occurrence in the file was a write statement. So the note's supply
        ! curve, its elasticity, its normalisation and item 4 of the stationary
        ! equilibrium described machinery that was not there, and every price
        ! counterfactual moved a number the households never saw.
        !
        ! Production costs C(V) = (nu_1/nu_2) V^nu_2, so the competitive supply
        ! price is nu_1 V^(nu_2-1) and the elasticity of supply is 1/(nu_2-1).
        !
        ! SET FROM CANNABIS, not from a midpoint of legal markets. There is no
        ! direct estimate for an illicit drug, and the cigarette and gasoline
        ! literatures were the wrong place to bracket it from: those goods are
        ! produced and traded under ordinary commercial law. Cannabis is not.
        ! It remains a Schedule I substance federally, so even a state licensed
        ! producer operates without interstate trade, without ordinary banking
        ! and under federal prohibition, which is the set of frictions an illicit
        ! supplier faces. Mace, Patel and Seegert (2020) estimate a supply
        ! elasticity of 1.46 from Washington State administrative data. That is
        ! the number, so nu_2 = 1 + 1/1.46 = 1.6849.
        !
        ! THE BENCHMARK NORMALISES RATHER THAN CLEARS, and that is not a dodge:
        ! with one good and one price there is nothing in the data to pin the
        ! level of nu_1 against, so nu_1 is chosen each loop to put the price at
        ! one at whatever quantity the benchmark produces. The market clears at
        ! p_v=1 by construction, and nu_1 is saved.
        !
        ! THE COUNTERFACTUALS CLEAR. They read the benchmark's nu_1, so the
        ! supply curve is fixed, and the price then moves with the quantity
        ! households choose: a policy that cuts drug use cuts the price, which
        ! feeds back into use. That feedback is the reason the supply elasticity
        ! is in the paper at all, and it was absent.
        ! SETTABLE, BECAUSE 1.46 IS A CANNABIS NUMBER AND THIS IS NOT A CANNABIS
        ! MARKET. Mace, Patel and Seegert estimate it from Washington State
        ! administrative data -- a LICENSED, taxed, canopy-capped market, and
        ! licence caps are exactly what make a supply curve slope up in the short
        ! run. Heroin, cocaine and diverted oxycodone have nothing of the sort.
        !
        ! WHAT THE LITERATURE SAYS. There is no direct econometric estimate for
        ! an illicit drug. The National Academies review records that studies of
        ! illegal drug markets assume very high elasticities, and Rhodes et al.
        ! (2002) assume very elastic cocaine supply because the production
        ! technology is simple and cheap. The cost structure agrees: the
        ! retail-to-farmgate ratio for cocaine is about 262 to 1, so production
        ! is roughly 0.4 per cent of the retail price, and commercialisation
        ! estimates put the fall at 96 per cent for cocaine and 98 for heroin.
        ! Essentially all of the retail price is prohibition risk and
        ! distribution labour, both of which scale with quantity -- a flat
        ! marginal cost curve.
        !
        ! PERFECTLY ELASTIC NEEDS NO SPECIAL CASE, and it was a mistake to think
        ! it did. curvature is 1+1/elasticity, so as elasticity grows the
        ! exponent on quantity goes to zero and implied_price = shifter, which
        ! the benchmark normalises to one. The gap is then identically zero at
        ! EVERY quantity: the market clears at a price of one by construction and
        ! there is nothing to iterate. Clearing is a step only when the curve
        ! slopes up. At elasticity 1e6 the implied price is 0.9999976 against a
        ! price of 1, so the ordinary code path converges on loop one.
        !
        ! PASS IT TO THE BENCHMARK AS WELL AS THE ARMS. The shifter is computed
        ! on the benchmark path as 1/q**(curvature-1) and READ by the
        ! counterfactual. Pass a new elasticity only to the arms and the shifter
        ! still carries the 1.46 normalisation while the arm evaluates q**0
        ! against it, which pegs the price at the wrong level rather than at one.
        ! That is the same shape as the stale-constant defect found earlier
        ! today, where the benchmark used a live value and the counterfactual a
        ! frozen one.
        ! THE LITERAL IS DELIBERATELY NOT 1.46d0, AND THAT IS NOT A TYPO.
        !
        ! `1.46` with no suffix is a DEFAULT-REAL literal: float32, widened to
        ! double, so the value actually held is 1.4600000381469727 and not 1.46.
        ! The original was `real(8), parameter :: drug_supply_curvature=
        ! 1+1/drug_supply_elasticity`, which divided that widened float32 in
        ! double. A variable's initialiser cannot reference another variable, so
        ! it has to be written out -- and writing it as 1.46d0 changes the
        ! curvature by 1.06e-8, which is exactly what made the exclusion null
        ! miss bit-identical reproduction at the ninth digit on b, lambda_d, h_1,
        ! pi_V, beta and w_bot. real(1.46,8) reproduces the original exactly.
        !
        ! THE SAME TRAP IS ALL OVER THIS FILE. Every `real(8) :: x=<literal>`
        ! without a d0 suffix holds a float32-rounded value. "Correcting" any of
        ! them moves every number that depends on it in the eighth digit, so
        ! none should be touched casually, and certainly not in the same round as
        ! a change whose whole acceptance test is bit-identical reproduction.
        real(8) :: drug_supply_elasticity=1.46
        real(8) :: drug_supply_curvature=1+1/real(1.46,8)
        real(8) :: drug_supply_shifter=1
        real(8) :: distance_drug_market=0
        ! 5e-3, BECAUSE 1e-3 IS BELOW THE NOISE FLOOR AND MEASURABLY UNREACHABLE.
        !
        ! tf_rationed ran 73 loops on the 1e-3 rule without exiting. The budget
        ! cleared at loop 47 and held; the drug gap then oscillated in a fixed
        ! band of roughly [1.5e-3, 3.7e-3] with no downward trend. The ONLY loop
        ! at which the gap was inside 1e-3 was loop 23, when the budget was still
        ! at 0.0925 and far outside, so the two conditions were never satisfiable
        ! together.
        !
        ! WHY THE FLOOR IS WHERE IT IS, and this is the number to fix rather than
        ! the tolerance. The implied supply price is nu_1 V^(1/eps_s), so an error
        ! in V is amplified into the price gap by 1/eps_s = 0.685, and V comes
        ! from a distribution solved only to stopping_rule_stationary_distribution
        ! = 6e-4 when include_addiction is 1. On top of that the iteration's own
        ! gain is |eps_d|/eps_s = 3.14/1.46 = 2.15, so the same noise is fed back
        ! amplified: a damped step is stable only for damping below 0.635, and at
        ! drug_market_adjuster = 0.5 it contracts at 0.575 a loop in theory while
        ! the observed contraction was about 0.96 -- the iteration was no longer
        ! doing the work, the noise was.
        !
        ! FOR THE PAPER, tighten the DISTRIBUTION rule first so V stops being
        ! noisy, and only then tighten this one. Loosening this alone hides the
        ! problem; it does not fix it. Note also that none of this instability
        ! would arise at the ESTIMATED demand elasticity: at -0.63 the gain is
        ! 0.43 and the market clears in a handful of loops. The convergence
        ! difficulty and the failed elasticity validation are one defect.
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_drug_market=5*10.d0**(-3)
        ! Multiplies the four transition stopping rules and NOTHING else. Both
        ! operands are literals, so this blend cannot carry an uninitialised
        ! value the way the median_income cutoff did -- 0*NaN=NaN was today's
        ! bug and the pattern is worth distrusting on sight.
        real(8), parameter :: transition_tolerance_factor=&
            solve_transition_dynamics*2.d0+(1-solve_transition_dynamics)*1.d0
        ! 0.5 -> 0.2, 19 September, because 0.5 is ABOVE the stability
        ! threshold at this model's demand elasticity and the price iteration
        ! diverges.
        !
        ! THE ARITHMETIC, from this model's own measured response. The map is
        ! p' = p + a*(implied(p) - p) with implied = shifter*V(p)^(1/eps_s), so
        !     d(implied)/dp = (1/eps_s)*eps_d          near the fixed point
        !     multiplier    = 1 + a*(d(implied)/dp - 1)
        ! Measured on the omega_c = 1 plus internalisation arm, b was 0.0935 at
        ! p = 0.75 and 0.0227 at p = 1.00, so eps_d = -4.92 and
        ! d(implied)/dp = 0.685*(-4.92) = -3.37. At a = 0.5 the multiplier is
        ! 1 - 2.185 = -1.185: magnitude above one, sign alternating, which is
        ! divergent oscillation. Observed as exactly that -- distance_drug_market
        ! 0.0756, 0.164, 0.117, 0.349 across four loops, the same shape
        ! stopping_rule_drug_market's comment records for universal Treatment
        ! First.
        !
        ! Stability needs a < 2/4.37 = 0.458. The older comment put the
        ! threshold at 0.635, but it used |eps_d| = 3.14; at the measured 4.92
        ! the threshold falls below 0.5 and the default no longer clears it.
        !
        ! At 0.2 the multiplier is 1 - 0.874 = 0.126, contracting an order a
        ! loop, and it stays stable even if the true elasticity were as large as
        ! 12. The cost is a slower approach from far away, which is what
        ! drug_price_start is for.
        !
        ! IT IS NOT V NOISE, WHICH WAS THE STANDING HYPOTHESIS AND WAS TESTED.
        ! With the price held fixed the same economy at the same 6e-4
        ! distribution tolerance converged cleanly: CF MOMENTS distance 1.628,
        ! 3.9e-3, 2.9e-3, 6.6e-4, 8.2e-4, 1.2e-3, 3.1e-4, 5.9e-5, 4.9e-5. The
        ! economy is fine; the price map was the unstable part.
        real(8) :: drug_market_adjuster=0.2
        ! THE MOST THE DRUG PRICE MAY MOVE IN ONE LOOP, as a share of itself.
        ! See the cap in clear_drug_market for what it replaces and why.
        real(8), parameter :: max_drug_price_move=0.005
        real(8) :: previous_price_of_addictive_good=0
        real(8) :: previous_drug_price_gap=0
        integer :: have_drug_price_secant=0

        real(8), parameter :: price_of_addictive_good_benchmark=1
        
        ! THE DRUG PRICE ALONG A TRANSITION PATH.
        !
        ! price_of_addictive_good stays a SCALAR. Every other market in the
        ! transition carries a time index, and TRANSITION_LESSONS section 12
        ! concluded the price had to be dimensioned like them -- about a hundred
        ! read sites, two of which (calculate_utility's local copy and the
        ! elasticity test's save/restore) have no time argument at all.
        !
        ! THAT IS NOT NECESSARY. The path loop visits ONE PERIOD AT A TIME, and
        ! the value function is already time indexed, so the scalar only ever
        ! needs to hold the current period's price. Re-pointing it from a path
        ! array at the top of each period -- the same trick as reslot_policies --
        ! makes every one of those hundred sites correct without touching them.
        !
        ! Checked before relying on it: price_of_addictive_good appears in
        ! exactly two OpenMP clauses and BOTH are shared(), not private(), so a
        ! scalar read inside the parallel regions stays correct.
        !
        ! previous_price IS PER PERIOD TOO, and that is the part that is easy to
        ! get wrong. clear_drug_market takes a secant step against the price it
        ! last saw; with one scalar, period t's secant would use period t-1's
        ! price from THIS loop rather than period t's price from the LAST loop,
        ! which is not the derivative it thinks it is measuring.
        real(8), dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: price_of_addictive_good_path=1
        real(8), dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: previous_price_of_addictive_good_path=0

        ! THE SECANT'S OTHER TWO PIECES OF STATE, AND WHY THEY ARE PATHS.
        !
        ! previous_price_of_addictive_good was carried per period from the
        ! start, and its comment claimed the secant therefore differenced one
        ! period across LOOPS. It did not. previous_drug_price_gap and
        ! have_drug_price_secant are module scalars, last written by the
        ! PREVIOUS PERIOD of the SAME loop, so the slope had an across-loops
        ! numerator over an across-periods denominator -- two different
        ! experiments divided by one another.
        !
        ! It was survivable rather than fatal: loop 0 seeds previous == current,
        ! which the 1e-10 guard turns into a damped step, and from loop 1 the
        ! trust region and the half-a-per-cent cap bound the error to picking a
        ! wrong direction within half a per cent. It is still wrong, and the fix
        ! is to give the other two the same per-period treatment.
        real(8), dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: previous_drug_price_gap_path=0
        integer, dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: have_drug_price_secant_path=0
        real(8), dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: previous_probability_of_voucher_path=0
        real(8), dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: previous_budget_residual_path=0
        integer, dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: have_pi_v_secant_path=0
        real(8) :: largest_distance_drug_market=1
        ! PI_V ALONG THE PATH. In the arms pi_v is what clears the government
        ! budget; the transition measured distance_govt_budget, put its maximum
        ! across periods in the do-while, and moved NOTHING in response -- a
