        ! RELEASED 17 September, on the author's *"Beta seems to be too high, I
        ! think we need to open it again"* and *"We need to get beta back to 3"*.
        ! Restored to the 5*10^-3 it carried before it was pinned, taken from git
        ! rather than chosen, so the step size is the one it was calibrated with.
        !
        ! WHAT PINNING IT COST. Wealth over output read 3.21 when beta was pinned
        ! and 4.01 after the housing ladder went to 1/3/5, because a dearer ladder
        ! moves the wealth side and beta could not answer. A pinned parameter is
        ! only safe while the economy around it is the one it was pinned in, and
        ! this economy is not.
        real(8) :: discount_factor_adjuster=5*10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: distance_discount_factor
        ! A RATIO OF THREE CANNOT BE HIT TO ONE PART IN THIRTY THOUSAND. The old
        ! tolerance of 1e-4 was written against a mortgage share between zero and
        ! one and is meaningless against wealth over output. One part in three
        ! hundred is what the outer loop can actually deliver.
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_discount_factor=10.d0**(-2)

        ! WEALTH OVER OUTPUT, the moment that identifies beta. The model
        ! counterpart is total household net wealth over GDP including imputed
        ! rent. With include_ownership at zero that net wealth is financial
        ! assets alone: the rental stock sits on the rental companies' books and
        ! not on any household's, so the national accounts counterpart is
        ! nonresidential capital rather than total household net worth.
        !
        ! BEA fixed assets over NIPA output puts private nonresidential fixed
        ! assets at roughly two years of GDP and all private fixed assets,
        ! residential included, at roughly three.
        !
        ! BACK TO 3.0 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 2 September, having been 2.5
        ! for about ninety minutes on the same day.
        !
        ! WHY IT WENT TO 2.5 AND WHY THAT WAS HALF WRONG. Two arguments were put
        ! for lowering it. The first is the residential-stock argument above,
        ! which is sound and stands: include_homeownership is 0, the rental stock
        ! sits on the rental companies' books, so the counterpart is
        ! nonresidential capital at roughly two years of GDP rather than all
        ! private fixed assets at three. The second was that 3.0 could not be
        ! REACHED, and that was unfounded. It rested on discount_factor being
        ! exhausted near 0.99995, which assumed beta below one for no reason: the
        ! model is finite-horizon backward induction, the handover has always
        ! clamped to [0.5, 1.05], and nothing above one had ever been tried.
        !
        ! Measured since, all else equal:
        !
        !     discount_factor   beta      w_bot (target 0.0250)
        !        0.99980       2.4528        0.0247
        !        1.00200       2.7287        0.0250
        !        1.00500       3.1277        0.0251
        !        1.01500       4.3665        0.0250
        !
        ! 3.0 is crossed between 1.00200 and 1.00500, with no bound pinned and
        ! nothing non-finite. w_bot does NOT pay for it -- it is a share, so more
        ! patience lifts numerator and denominator together, and the two are close
        ! to orthogonal in this instrument. The binding cost is aleph.
        ! 2.25 ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, 5 September: *"i think we can even
        ! target a wealth to output of 2.25, i think there are papers that use
        ! that"*. No citation is attached because none was given.
        !
        ! HASH-INERT: wealth_to_output_data is in model_hash.TARGET_CONSTANTS,
        ! whose values are blanked before the semantic hash is taken, so the
        ! archive is not reset and every existing point still scores.
        real(8), parameter :: wealth_to_output_data=3.0d0
        real(8) :: wealth_to_output_model_1
        real(8) :: gap_discount_factor_1
        real(8) :: previous_gap_discount_factor=0
        integer :: have_gap_discount_factor=0
        real(8), parameter :: discount_factor_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: discount_factor_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: discount_factor_adjuster_floor=10.d0**(-5)
        real(8), parameter :: discount_factor_adjuster_start=5*10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: discount_factor_lo=-1
        real(8) :: discount_factor_hi=-1
        real(8) :: discount_factor_lo_moment=-1
        real(8) :: discount_factor_hi_moment=-1
        real(8), parameter :: bracket_discount_factor_floor=10.d0**(-6)
        
