        ! Seeded at the calibrated value. From 340 the first step subtracted
        ! adjuster*(96.0-7.5) = 885, which is 2.6 times the parameter itself, so it
        ! was clamped to its floor of 0.01 and then spent five iterations climbing
        ! back. The step is now bounded relatively as well, below.
        ! ALEPH IS SEEDED WHERE THE TARGET PUTS IT, not where the old units did.
        ! At 143.06 the model produced a value of life of 1,068 times average
        ! labour income against a target of 80, and the trust region halves aleph
        ! at most once an iteration, so four iterations were spent walking down a
        ! ladder whose bottom is known: the ratio is close to linear in aleph, so
        ! 80/1068 of 143.06 is about 11. Every block downstream of the risk term
        ! was reading a model in which dying was worth thirteen times what the
        ! calibration says, which is why drug quantities sat on their floor.
        real(8) :: value_of_life=(1-perceived_probability_lower)*11.0d0+perceived_probability_lower*120
        real(8) :: value_of_life_step_1=0
        
        ! A FREE SCALE ON NEWBORN WEALTH, on the author's instruction of
        ! 1 September: "give all newborns some initial wealth to match their
        ! homelessness rate". max_initial_endowment is pinned to average labour
        ! income, so the endowment had no free parameter and could not be
        ! calibrated on anything; this multiplies it and can.
        !
        ! Why the homelessness rate of the young is the right target for it.
        ! Measured: newborn wealth 0.000 gives an age-26 homelessness rate of
        ! 16.2 per cent, 0.096 gives 13.4, and by age 29 wealth of 0.277 gives
        ! 0.24. So the rate is steeply decreasing in newborn wealth somewhere
        ! between 0.096 and 0.277, and one number placed in that interval sets it.
        ! THE RENTAL LADDER'S RATIOS, SETTABLE RATHER THAN HARD CODED.
        !
        ! h_3 = rung_ratio_h3 x h_2 and h_4 = rung_ratio_h4 x h_2, defaulting to
        ! the 2 and 3 the author specified on 1 September. They are runtime
        ! values with handover cases so that a different ladder costs a vector
        ! change and not a model hash -- the same reason number_of_beta_types is
        ! one, and the reason the archive survived dropping the second beta.
        !
        ! WHY THE RATIOS ARE SUSPECT AT 2 AND 3. Measured at point_92c88c5ec8:
        ! two thirds of renters (67.5 per cent) sit in the TOP rung, and 68.2 per
        ! cent of those pay more than half their income in rent, while units 0
        ! and 1 have NO cost burdened households at all. So rent50, at +163 per
        ! cent, is manufactured entirely by the top rung's price. A household
        ! wanting more than 0.110 of housing has to jump to 0.165 with nothing in
        ! between, and the jump is what makes it cost burdened.
        !
        ! It also explains what gamma has been doing. The code DECLARES
        ! relative_share_of_housing at 0.278; the search has driven it to 0.135,
        ! half that, trying to pull housing over income from 0.298 down to 0.202.
        ! It could not work: the moment is set by the grid, not by the
        ! preference, so a defensible taste parameter was being spent to
        ! compensate for a ladder. The author's reading, and it is the right one.
        real(8) :: rung_ratio_h3=2.d0
        real(8) :: rung_ratio_h4=3.d0
        ! THE NEWBORN ENDOWMENT, BRACKETED, AND ONE-SIDED.
        !
        ! Its moment is band 1's share of the homeless against
        ! val_hud_share_1(1) = 0.260. Raising the endowment gives the youngest --
        ! who are almost all employed -- the liquidity to rent, which takes them
        ! off the street, so the share FALLS in this parameter and a model ABOVE
        ! target calls for a LARGER endowment.
        !
        ! ONE-SIDED, on the author's rule of 3 September: "If you see that you
        ! need to bring up the youngest endowment since their share is too high,
        ! then you can turn on that instrument. Keep it off if the share of young
        ! is below the data". Below target the endowment can only push the share
        ! further down, so the guard HOLDS it rather than moving it the wrong
        ! way. Same shape as the THETA HELD guard, and it announces itself for
        ! the same reason: a parameter that is held has to be distinguishable
        ! from one that has converged.
        real(8) :: newborn_endowment_scale=1.d0
        ! OFF THE FREEZE, on the author's instruction of 13 September: *"newborn
        ! endowment should be used to match the amount of young homeless"*, which
        ! is the moment its bracket already carries -- the share of the homeless
        ! in the youngest HUD age band, homelessness_by_age_group(1,1) against
        ! val_hud_share_1(1). The bracket was written and then frozen, so the
        ! moment has been reported with nothing acting on it. Same one-line
        ! detachment applied to weight_on_child, kappa_phi_d_level, smi_onset_level
        ! and voucher_probability.
        ! HELD 16 September. The author: *"i think we have to go back....and
        ! just calibrate the following: aleph, b, kappa, tau_u, SUD homeless,
        ! homeless, SUD"*. Seven moments, seven instruments, everything else
        ! frozen where it stands.
        ! REOPENED 16 September on the author's instruction: "i need you to now
        ! add mu_H, pi_eu, newborn_endowment, pi_v, and cbar", plus "can you
        ! also fix sigma_y?", "set h2 to get 5-7%% cost burdened" and "maybe
        ! you can also adjust kappa_find_smi to hit its target?". Held at zero
        ! for the seven-instrument restriction of 15 September; the value
        ! restored here is the one it carried before that restriction.
        real(8) :: newborn_endowment_adjuster=2*10.d0**(-1)
        real(8) :: distance_newborn_endowment=1
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_newborn_endowment=10.d0**(-2)
        ! THE ENDOWMENT ENTERS THE BUDGET CONSTRAINT, NOT THE INITIAL
        ! DISTRIBUTION, on the author's instruction of 1 September: "instead of
        ! pegging them to a financial grid, just give them an endowment only in
        ! the first period. that would be continuous ... and it would show in
        ! the BC not the initialization."
        !
        ! Endowing the initial distribution meant placing every newborn ON an
        ! asset grid point, and the grid is coarse where it matters: at the
        ! inherited curvature there was ONE rung between zero and a year's rent.
        ! So the instrument was a step function with four usable steps, it could
        ! not be bisected, and a scale of zero did not even deliver zero wealth,
        ! because the snap takes the smallest point STRICTLY above the target.
        !
        ! A transfer received in the first period has none of that. Newborns
        ! start at zero assets and get newborn_endowment_scale times average
        ! labour income as cash in hand at age 26, so the endowment is continuous
        ! in the scale, exact at zero, and the household -- not the grid --
        ! decides how much of it to carry into age 27.
        !
        ! It is a per-age array rather than a scalar because that is the shape
        ! every site that adds a transfer to resources already takes, so the
        ! endowment rides the plumbing the inheritance transfer used before the
        ! residual was routed to the government. Only element 1 is ever nonzero.
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: newborn_age_transfer=0.d0
        integer :: endowment_is_a_transfer=1
        real(8) :: max_initial_endowment=7
        real(8) :: max_initial_endowment_adjuster=0
        real(8) :: distance_max_initial_endowment
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_max_initial_endowment=10.d0**(-3)
        
        ! WARM START. These three are the values the calibration of 16 August
        ! converged to, and starting from them saves roughly fifteen equilibrium
        ! solves on every restart. They are starting points, not settings: all
        ! three still iterate. The proper fix is to write the calibrated values to
        ! a file at the end of each loop and read them at startup, so a killed run
        ! resumes instead of beginning again; that is not done yet, and this run
        ! having been killed once is what made it worth doing at all.
        ! h_1, AND IT IS WHAT CARRIES HOMELESSNESS.  The author, 13 September:
        ! *"pick h1 so that you have the right homelessness rate"*.  That is
        ! already the wiring -- size_homelessness is bracketed on
        ! model_fraction_homeless -- so nothing changes about the bracket.
        !
        ! WHAT CHANGES IS THE DECLARATION, which read 0.304 while the parameter
        ! calibrates to about 2.4e-05, four orders of magnitude away.  It is a
        ! starting value for a calibrated parameter, so it never mattered while a
        ! vector file was supplied, and it mattered the moment one was not: the
        ! declared ladder put the street ABOVE the cheapest rental and the run
        ! script refused to start.  It now starts where the parameter lives.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness=2.4d-5

        ! THE NON-HOUSING CONSUMPTION PENALTY OF THE STREET, psi. Consumption is
        ! worth (1-psi) of itself to a homeless household: no storage, no cooking,
        ! possessions lost, a higher price for everything bought in small amounts.
        ! It is a UTILITY penalty and not a budget one -- the household still pays
        ! for c and simply gets less from it -- so nothing in the budget constraint
        ! or in market clearing moves with it.
        !
        ! WHY IT EXISTS, on the author's instruction of 4 September: *"we may want
        ! to basically have a non-housing consumption penalty when you are homeless
        ! (and you choose that penalty to match the average income of the
        ! homeless)"*.
        !
        ! The moment it owns is y_h, income of the homeless, 0.72 of average in the
        ! model against 0.191 in the data. Every other instrument tried on it acts
        ! on employment TRANSITIONS and cannot reach households whose housing
        ! choice never depended on employment -- 99.3 per cent of this model's
        ! homeless can pay rent from current income. Measured on
        ! increased_prob_if_homeless across its whole permitted range, 0.2 to 0.95:
        ! y_h moved 0.8 per cent while the SMI share of the homeless fell 59 per
        ! cent, because that parameter's two halves are a 1.3 point change in a
        ! small probability and a 52 point change in a large one, and the large one
        ! lands on whoever is ALREADY unemployed.
        !
        ! This penalty is first order and certain, which is the character of the
        ! rent saving it has to offset, and it is PROPORTIONAL to consumption, so
        ! it costs a comfortable household more than a poor one. That is what makes
        ! it a composition instrument rather than another lever on the level.
        !
        ! ZERO REPRODUCES THE MODEL EXACTLY. Verified as a no-op before anything
        ! was calibrated on it.
        real(8) :: consumption_penalty_homeless=0.d0
        
        ! ON, AND IT WAS OFF. At zero the update below is size_homelessness minus
        ! zero times the gap, so the parameter never moved and the homelessness
        ! target was reached by hand between runs instead. That was workable while
        ! the rental grid was stable. It is not now: taking the mortgage
        ! eligibility overwrite out of housing_size moved the smallest rental, so
        ! the hand set 0.21 leaves homelessness at 0.007 per cent against a target
        ! of 0.399 and no iteration closes it.
        !
        ! The sign is already right. size_homelessness scales the service flow of
        ! the street, so raising it raises homelessness, and the update subtracts
        ! the adjuster times (model minus data), which rises when the model is
        ! below target. 20 moves it about 0.08 per iteration at the current gap,
        ! which crosses the [0,1] range in roughly a dozen equilibrium solves.
        ! LIVE AGAIN WHILE THE THREE-BLOCK RUNS. The author, 11 September:
        ! *"concede h2 and set it lower to 5% or so. And get h1 to match"*. h_1
        ! now has two instruments pointed at it -- the rung, which the lower
        ! severe_cost_burden_data target pushes down, and this one directly.
        ! HELD, BECAUSE h_1 IS NOW TIED TO h_2. The author, 14 September: *"I
        ! want h1 to be set to 1% of h2."*  A tied parameter is not a calibrated
        ! one, and leaving the adjuster live would have the bracket push h_1 away
        ! from the tie every loop while set_h1_from_h2 pulled it back.
        !
        ! WHAT THIS COSTS, AND IT HAS TO BE SAID. h_1 was the instrument for the
        ! homelessness RATE. With it tied and h_2 externally set, NOTHING
        ! brackets on homelessness any more -- it is hit by choosing h_2 by hand,
        ! which is what the author asked for ("h2 should be set externally to
        ! some value to allow homelessness to be 0.4%"). distance_size_homeless-
        ! ness is zeroed with the adjuster so the loop cannot be held open by a
        ! moment nothing moves.
        ! DECLARED LIVE, 14 September.  This was 0.d0 and h_1 got its 20 from
        ! apply_first_block_freeze alone, so releasing the freeze would have
        ! silently left homelessness with no instrument -- and homelessness is
        ! one of the eight targets the author asked to hit.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_adjuster=20.d0
        ! h_1 AS A FRACTION OF h_2. One per cent, on the author's instruction.
        real(8) :: h1_share_of_h2=0.01d0
        ! UNTIED, 14 September: *"don't pin h1 to 1% of h2. Let h1 move
        ! freely"*.  At 1 the tie overwrote size_homelessness at setup with
        ! h1_share_of_h2*smallest_house_size, so the handover value was
        ! discarded and every run in the archive sat at the same h_1.  At 0
        ! the handover value stands and the bracket moves it.
        integer, parameter :: tie_h1_to_h2=0

        ! H_1 TARGETS THE EMPLOYED SHARE OF THE HOMELESS, 15 September, on
        ! the author's instruction: "h1 show target emphomeless=46% as long as
        ! it's in the 0.4-1% homelessness", and "h1 has to rise if emp homeless
        ! is low, and decline if its high".
        !
        ! THE SIGN NEEDS NO CHANGE. The step is size_homelessness - adjuster*gap,
        ! so a gap below zero raises h_1. With gap = model - target on the
        ! employed share, an employed share BELOW target raises h_1, which is
        ! what was asked.
        !
        ! THE BAND IS A GUARD, NOT A TARGET. h_1 is no longer fitted to
        ! fraction_homeless_data, so nothing stops it wandering; the guard below
        ! holds the homelessness rate inside [0.004, 0.01] and says so when it
        ! binds. A run that sits on a band edge is reporting that the employed
        ! share cannot be had inside the band, which is a finding and not a bug.
        ! BACK ONTO THE HOMELESSNESS RATE, on the author's instruction of
        ! 15 September: *"if emp homeless gets higher when h1 is closer to 0.4%
        ! then target h1 to 0.4% so that emp homeless is closer to 46%"*.
        !
        ! THE CONDITIONAL HOLDS, measured across six runs -- h_1 0.0040 gives
        ! emp_homeless 0.271, 0.0041 gives 0.247, 0.0052 gives 0.224, 0.0060
        ! gives 0.178, 0.0080 gives 0.158, 0.0085 gives 0.154. Monotone.
        !
        ! fraction_homeless_data IS ALREADY 0.004, so pointing h_1 back at the
        ! homelessness rate targets exactly the 0.4 per cent asked for, and the
        ! band guard becomes redundant rather than wrong.
        !
        ! AND IT WILL NOT BE ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. The best emp_homeless measured at
        ! h_1 = 0.0040 is 0.271 against a target of 0.46, so this closes part of
        ! the gap and something else has to carry the rest.
        integer, parameter :: h1_targets_emp_homeless=0
        ! FLOOR RAISED 17 September, from 0.004. The author: *"We want to get
        ! emp homeless as high as possible while still having h1 between
        ! 0.4%-1%"*, then *"try to get h1 higher until you get emp homeless
        ! closer"*.
        !
        ! WHY THE FLOOR AND NOT A TARGET. Inside the band the gap is zero and
        ! h_1 stops, so a floor of 0.004 parked h_1 at the WORST end of the
        ! author's range for their own objective. End-of-run pairs put
        ! emp_homeless far higher in the middle of the range than at its
        ! bottom: h_1 0.00319 gave 0.0198, 0.00396 gave 0.0621, 0.00557 gave
        ! 0.1934 and 0.00741 gave 0.1609. Those are four different runs with
        ! different dispersions and different b, so the shape is a correlation
        ! and NOT a controlled bracket -- note 0.00741 sits above 0.00557 in
        ! h_1 and below it in emp, which a clean monotone curve would not do.
        !
        ! 0.0055 is therefore a step in the direction the author asked for, not
        ! a claim about the argmax. It stays inside the approved 0.4 to 1 per
        ! cent and n23 ran at 0.0074 without trouble, so there is room to go
        ! further if emp_homeless is still climbing when h_1 arrives.
        ! FLOOR RAISED AGAIN 18 September, 0.0055 -> 0.0080, and this time
        ! against a CONTROLLED reading rather than a cross-run correlation.
        !
        ! WHAT n66 MEASURED. Five blocks at size_homelessness HELD constant at
        ! 0.020539 -- the band floor of 0.0055 was pushing it up by the one per
        ! cent cap, so the printed value was flat to four digits -- gave a
        ! homelessness rate of 0.00597, 0.00531, 0.00533, 0.00529, 0.00533 and
        ! an employed share of 0.2494, 0.2343, 0.2259, 0.2190, 0.2148. The rate
        ! is flat and the employed share falls in every single block. That is
        ! the converged level of emp_homeless at a rate of 0.53 per cent, near
        ! the bottom of the author's range, and it is 0.215 against a target of
        ! 0.46 and an acceptance floor of 0.30.
        !
        ! WHY THE BAND AND NOT A POINT. Homelessness is very nearly a step
        ! function of size_homelessness -- the note at the step cap below says
        ! so and measured it -- so a point target at 0.0100 has no resting place
        ! and would oscillate across it. A band of [0.0080, 0.0100] has the gap
        ! go to zero the moment the rate reaches 0.8 per cent, which parks h_1
        ! in the top half of the author's approved 0.4 to 1.0 per cent with the
        ! 1.0 per cent ceiling still guarding the other side.
        !
        ! AND IT IS A TEST, NOT A FIX. The two bodies of evidence on record
        ! disagree about the sign. The six-run set quoted above has the rate and
        ! the employed share moving OPPOSITE ways, which is also what a pure
        ! accounting confound would give, since the rate IS the denominator of
        ! the employed share: a run whose rate is high because more unemployed
        ! reached the street reports a lower employed share with no causal
        ! content at all. The four end-of-run pairs quoted below have them
        ! moving TOGETHER. Neither is a bracket. This run moves h_1 and nothing
        ! else, from a rate of 0.53 per cent to 0.80, and reads the answer off
        ! the same economy. If emp_homeless falls, the confound was not a
        ! confound and the next move is a point target at 0.004.
        ! FLOOR TO 0.0040, 18 September 03:30, AND THE BRACKET IS WHY.
        !
        ! n67 ran the test the note above designed, one constant moved, seeded
        ! from n66 verbatim. The answer is FLAT, which is neither of the two
        ! signs on record:
        !
        !                       n66 rate 0.00533   n67 rate 0.00623
        !   node 1 employed         0.254856           0.253678
        !   node 1 on the street    0.058076           0.066598   +14.7%
        !     share of its employed  22.79%             26.25%
        !   all homeless            0.271833           0.317         +16%
        !   emp_homeless             0.2138             0.2103     -1.6%
        !
        ! h_1 DOES recruit node 1's employed onto the street. It recruits the
        ! middle distribution's unemployed at the same rate, and emp_homeless is
        ! the ratio of the one to the other, so the ratio does not move. AND IT
        ! COSTS b: 0.1034 at n66 against a target of 0.10502, 0.128 at n67 and
        ! still climbing. A cheaper street frees resources, and the b
        ! participation condition is R > p_v*b_SG*(alp/gam) with R net of
        ! housing -- the same inequality that made the ladder b's dominant lever.
        !
        ! SO THE FLOOR GOES TO THE AUTHOR'S OWN 0.004 AND h_1 STOPS MOVING. At a
        ! rate of 0.0053 the band [0.0040, 0.0100] has the gap at zero and
        ! size_homelessness HELD, which parks h_1 at the value that gave three of
        ! the author's four acceptance conditions. It is not a claim that 0.0053
        ! is optimal; it is a refusal to keep paying b for a moment h_1 cannot
        ! move.
        real(8), parameter :: h1_band_lo=0.0040d0
        real(8), parameter :: h1_band_hi=0.010d0

        ! AND THE TRAVERSE HAS TO DAMP, for aleph's reason. The adjuster is
        ! twenty and the gap is a rate, so a gap of 0.031 moves the parameter by
        ! 0.62 on a parameter bounded in [0,1]: it went 0.531 to 0 in one step,
        ! then 0 to 0.0798, then back to 0. Homelessness is the paper's headline
        ! moment and it was being read off a parameter slamming between its
        ! bounds.
        !
        ! The bracket cannot rescue it yet. A bracket whose endpoints were priced
        ! at different values of life is correctly discarded, aleph is still
        ! moving twenty per cent a loop against a five per cent tolerance, so the
        ! bracket is thrown away every loop and the traverse is all there is.
        ! Damping the traverse is what makes the traverse usable in the meantime,
        ! and it costs nothing once the bracket does survive.
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_adjuster_start=20
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_adjuster_floor=10.d0**(-2)

        ! THE PARAMETER'S OWN FLOOR IS NOT ZERO, AND IT CANNOT BE.
        !
        ! h_1 is housing_size(0)*size_homelessness, and housing enters utility
        ! as h**((1-sigma)*relative_share_of_housing) with no shifter. At a risk
        ! aversion above one that exponent is NEGATIVE, so h_1 = 0 is not a very
        ! small dwelling, it is MINUS INFINITY: homelessness stops being a bad
        ! outcome and becomes an impossible one, and the model reports zero
        ! homeless whatever else is true of it. The sweep measured exactly
        ! 0.000000 homeless at b = 10^-4 and again at b = 0.12 with the
        ! parameter banked at zero, which is not a result about housing, it is
        ! the model refusing to price the street. Zero was the lower end of the
        ! update's own clamp, so the traverse was allowed to walk into it.
        ! THE FLOOR MOVED OFF THE RATIO AND ONTO THE STREET ITSELF.
        !
        ! It was 10**(-2) and it guarded the wrong quantity. The street is
        ! housing_size(0)*size_homelessness, a PRODUCT, so a floor on the ratio
        ! is a floor on the street only at a fixed rung. The author asked twice,
        ! on 1 September, for the rung substantially higher AND the street
        ! substantially lower; with the ratio floored at 10**(-2) those two are
        ! jointly reachable only up to a rung of about 0.55, above which the
        ! floor forces the street back up. That is an artefact of flooring the
        ! ratio, not a property of the model.
        !
        ! The danger the floor exists for is real and is ABSOLUTE: housing enters
        ! utility as h**((1-sigma)*relative_share_of_housing) with no shifter, so
        ! at a risk aversion above one a street of ZERO is minus infinity rather
        ! than a very small dwelling, homelessness stops being a bad outcome and
        ! becomes an impossible one, and the model reports exactly zero homeless
        ! whatever else is true. That danger is at zero, not at 10**(-2).
        !
        ! So the ratio may now go to 10**(-6), and street_size_floor below holds
        ! the product away from zero where it is actually built.
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_floor=10.d0**(-6)

        ! The absolute floor on the street's service flow. Four orders of
        ! magnitude below the benchmark street of 0.0058, so it never binds on
        ! anything the search would want, and far enough from zero that the
        ! negative exponent stays finite.
        real(8), parameter :: street_size_floor=10.d0**(-6)

        ! AND ONE STEP MAY NOT CROSS THE RANGE. The adjuster is twenty and the
        ! gap is a rate: at a gap of 0.088 the step is 1.76 on a parameter
        ! bounded in [0.01,0.9], twice the whole range, so the first step lands
        ! on a bound whatever the gap is and the damping never gets to bite.
        ! That is the 0.531 to 0 to 0.0798 to 0 recorded above, and it happened
        ! again at 0.3747 to 0. Capping the move leaves nine loops to traverse
        ! the range, which is the pace the adjuster was sized for in the first
        ! place, and the damping then works on top of it as intended.
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_max_step=10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_step_1=0
        real(8) :: gap_size_homelessness_1=0,previous_gap_size_homelessness=0
        integer :: have_gap_size_homelessness=0
        real(8) :: distance_size_homelessness
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_size_homelessness=10.d0**(-3)

        ! ITS OWN CAP AT ONE PER CENT, on the author's instruction of 16
        ! September: *"h1 could be updated faster. maybe at 1%"*.
        !
        ! IT WAS THE ONLY ONE OF THE SEVEN STILL ON THE GLOBAL TENTH OF A PER
        ! CENT. Every other live instrument was given its own cap at some point
        ! and this one never was. To take h_1 from 0.0065 to its 0.004 target at
        ! a tenth of a per cent a block needs about 476 blocks -- not slow,
        ! immobile -- which is why h_1 has drifted with the drug block all night
        ! instead of being driven anywhere. At one per cent it is about 48.
        !
        ! RAISED TO FIVE PER CENT 18 September. With the floor moved to 0.0080
        ! the rate has to travel from 0.0053, and at one per cent a block h_1
        ! drifts rather than arrives -- n66 spent five blocks moving the
        ! parameter 0.021162 to 0.021587, one per cent in total, while the gap
        ! it was closing stayed where it was. Five per cent is deliberately not
        ! the tenth the other four instruments were given: the bracket note
        ! directly below measures homelessness as very nearly a STEP function of
        ! this parameter, so the risk here is overshoot, not immobility.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_max_rel_step=0.05d0

        ! A BRACKET, BECAUSE THE LINEAR STEP CANNOT LAND ON THE TARGET.
        !
        ! Homelessness is very nearly a step function of size_homelessness, since
        ! the housing choice is discrete and the marginal households jump together.
        ! Measured over the thirteen iterations of the run archived in
        ! scratchpad/cal_run/stale_state: at 0.3836 the model gives 0.0 per cent,
        ! at 0.4634 it gives 1.22, at 0.3788 it gives 10.9 and at 0.5586 it gives
        ! 34.5, against a target of 0.399. So the parameter has to be placed inside
        ! a window narrower than the adjuster's own step.
        !
        ! It could not be. A gap of one percentage point moves the parameter by
        ! 20*0.01 = 0.2, which is wider than the whole window, so the update
        ! overshot every time it crossed: it slammed to the zero clamp, ratcheted
        ! back up in exact steps of 0.0798 while the model read zero, crossed the
        ! threshold, overshot to zero again, and never converged. The adjuster is
        ! not wrong, it is a range traverser, which is what the comment above says
        ! it was sized for.
        !
        ! So the step now only runs until there is a bracket, and after that the
        ! interval is bisected. lo is the largest value known to leave the model
        ! BELOW target, hi the smallest known to leave it at or above, which is
        ! well defined because homelessness rises in size_homelessness: a better
        ! street puts more households on it. Negative means not found yet.
        !
        ! The bracket is deliberately NOT written to the warm start file. The
        ! calibration state is read back positionally, so adding fields to it is a
        ! format change, and losing the bracket on a resume costs two iterations to
        ! rebuild whereas a misread field would be silent and wrong.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_lo=-1
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_hi=-1

        ! AND THE BRACKET HAS TO BE ABLE TO THROW ITSELF AWAY.
        !
        ! Bisecting assumes the moment is a function of this parameter alone. It
        ! is not, because every other parameter moves in the same outer loop, and
        ! one of them moves enormously: over the four iterations that formed the
        ! first bracket the value of life went 0.01, 80.96, 133.41, 168.27, 192.86.
        ! A household's willingness to live on a street that kills it is not
        ! comparable across a change like that, so the two endpoints were priced
        ! under different models and the interval between them means nothing.
        !
        ! It showed up as the moment moving the wrong way. Measured: 0.3836 gave
        ! 0.00 per cent, 0.4634 gave 1.22, then the midpoint 0.4235 gave 20.56 and
        ! the next midpoint 0.4036 gave 32.11. Lowering the parameter raised
        ! homelessness twice in a row, which cannot happen if a better street puts
        ! more households on it. A bracket that ignores this converges neatly onto
        ! a stale endpoint and reports a calibrated parameter that was never
        ! calibrated against anything.
        !
        ! So each endpoint now carries the moment measured there, and a new
        ! evaluation that contradicts monotonicity discards the bracket and goes
        ! back to traversing. The pattern is the one the labour supply search
        ! already uses: when the answer lands outside the bracket, the bracket was
        ! wrong, so throw it away rather than trusting it.
        !
        ! This makes the update honest, not convergent. If the violations keep
        ! coming, the message they carry is that this parameter cannot be
        ! calibrated while the value of life is still moving under it, and the
        ! answer to that is to sequence the loop rather than to bisect harder.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_lo_moment=-1
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_hi_moment=-1

        ! The monotonicity check above catches a stale bracket only once it has
        ! already produced a contradictory evaluation, which costs an equilibrium
        ! solve to learn. The cause is known, so it can be tested for directly:
        ! a bracket is valid only if its two endpoints were priced under the same
        ! value of life. This records aleph when the bracket is opened, and any
        ! evaluation arriving after aleph has moved by more than the tolerance
        ! throws the bracket away before it is used.
        !
        ! Hard sequencing, holding this parameter fixed until aleph converges, is
        ! the other way to get the same guarantee and it is NOT what is done here.
        ! The two are jointly determined: the value of life is measured over the
        ! whole distribution, the homeless included, and the street is what makes
        ! dying cheap. Freezing one to converge the other can settle on a
        ! different fixed point, or on none. Refusing to trust a bracket that
        ! spans two different models is the weaker claim and the safe one.
        real(8) :: value_of_life_at_bracket=-1
        real(8), parameter :: bracket_value_of_life_tolerance=0.05
        
        ! THIRTEEN, AND PLACED BY QUANTILE RATHER THAN EVENLY IN LOGS. Nine
        ! nodes spread uniformly over plus and minus three standard deviations
        ! put neighbouring incomes a factor of 2.07 apart and left the bottom of
        ! the distribution as three lumps carrying 0.43, 2.61 and 9.99 per cent.
        ! The homelessness target is 0.399 per cent, SMALLER THAN THE BOTTOM
        ! LUMP, so the attainable rates near it were about zero, 0.43 and 3.04
        ! per cent and no h_1 could land between them: that is the step function
        ! the bracket kept being discarded on. At thirteen nodes packed toward
        ! the bottom the first carries 0.164 per cent and six of them lie below
        ! the fifteenth percentile, where the housing and drug margins sit.
        ! SEVEN NODES, on the author's instruction of 2 September: *"you can then
        ! reduce the productivity grid size to be 7 or so where the bottom 3
        ! cover the lower side and the top 4 the rest. It will speed it up"*.
        !
        ! The design is theirs and it is built around the homelessness margin.
        ! With y_grid_kappa raised to 3.2 alongside, the bottom of the grid comes
        ! out as
        !
        !     node   mass %   cum %   y/mean   rent at h_2=0.039
        !       1     0.198    0.20   0.0302     101.4%   priced out
        !       2     1.618    1.82   0.0650      52.9%   rents, under 80
        !       3     4.829    6.64   0.1177      32.0%
        !       4-7  93.36   100.00
        !
        ! which is the author's specification almost exactly: about 0.2 per cent
        ! who cannot afford the cheapest rental at all, and a second poor node
        ! that can afford it WHEN EMPLOYED and cannot on unemployment benefit.
        ! That second margin is the one meant to put unemployed households on the
        ! street, which the seventeen node grid never did -- 92 to 100 per cent of
        ! its homeless were homeless BY CHOICE and earned average income.
        !
        ! THE RISK THIS REOPENS, and why kappa moved with it. The note below
        ! records that a coarse grid once broke h_1's bracket: the bottom lump
        ! carried 0.43 per cent against a 0.399 per cent target, so the attainable
        ! homelessness rates were about zero, 0.43 and 3.04 per cent and nothing
        ! could land on the target. At kappa 3.2 the first node carries 0.198 per
        ! cent, BELOW the target rather than above it, so the target is reached by
        ! node one entire plus part of node two rather than by a node boundary.
        ! That is what makes seven nodes safe here where it was not before, and it
        ! is the reason kappa is not left at 2.8.
        ! EIGHT NODES FROM 7 September, on the author's instruction: *"maybe we
        ! should add 1 more productivity between node 3 and 4"*, *"it would help
        ! even more to smooth things"*.
        !
        ! Node 4 alone carried 39.9 per cent of households at seven nodes and the
        ! gap from node 3 to node 4 was a factor of 2.2, so the grid was coarsest
        ! exactly where the mass is. A coarse grid makes households identical in
        ! blocks, and identical households cannot respond at the margin -- they
        ! move together, which is what turned a two per cent step into a
        ! sixty-fold jump in drug use on 7 September.
        ! BACK TO SEVEN, 7 September 23:45, on the author's instruction:
        ! *"let's go back to 7 grid points, i think it might help"*, reaffirmed
        ! after being shown the paragraph above: *"i suggest just go back to 7
        ! grids and let it run again"*.
        !
        ! THEIR MECHANISM, and it is a claim about MASS rather than about node
        ! positions: *"i think the 3rd grid from the bottom was just high enough
        ! to make the homelessness rate possible. we the added one taking away
        ! from the 3rd grid, it makes more poor households"*. The eighth node went
        ! in between nodes 3 and 4, so it drew mass out of node 3 and pushed the
        ! quantile boundaries down, putting more households below the level at
        ! which the cheapest rental is affordable. That is a direct account of why
        ! h_1 sits at +32 per cent and will not come down: the involuntary
        ! component is set by how many households cannot afford the rung at all.
        !
        ! THE OBJECTION, RAISED AND OVERRULED, recorded because it may yet be
        ! right. The paragraph above says seven nodes put 39.9 per cent of
        ! households in node 4 with a factor of 2.2 gap below it, and that this
        ! coarseness "turned a two per cent step into a sixty-fold jump in drug
        ! use on 7 September" -- the same signature as the jump in b at 23:09
        ! tonight, where every instrument stepped normally and b moved 9.9 per
        ! cent with an inverted sign. If that returns at seven nodes, this is the
        ! cause and this comment is where to look.
        !
        ! WHAT WAS CHECKED FIRST. The commit that set eight, b6c22ac8, touched
        ! ONLY this file, so nothing else moved with it and reverting the size
        ! alone cannot leave a hybrid. y_grid_kappa is already 3.2, which is the
        ! value the seven-node design was tuned at -- the node table above, node 1
        ! at 0.198 per cent mass, BELOW the h_1 target so the target is reached by
        ! node one entire plus part of node two rather than by a node boundary.
        !
        ! WHAT IT COSTS: this is a parameter with 414 uses dimensioning most of
        ! the model's arrays. The model hash moves, all 305 points on the previous
        ! economy are orphaned, and the loop must SEED rather than iterate.
        ! THIRTEEN FROM 17 SEPTEMBER, on the author's *"Ok, let's do 13 and see
        ! what happens"*. Seven put 121 per cent between adjacent nodes, which is
        ! what the warning above about a two per cent step becoming a sixty-fold
        ! jump in b was describing, and it made h_1 uncontrollable: the only
        ! households poor enough to be homeless were the bottom node, so h_1 WAS
        ! that node's mass and no price lever could move it.
        !
        ! The three numbers that come with the size, solved on a replica of
        ! KKK_process that reproduces the live grid to every printed digit:
        !
        !   kkk_s_deviation_bottom  2.694536   bottom node holds 0.700 per cent
        !   kkk_sigma_scale         1.921876   holds the bottom half's share of
        !                                      productivity at 0.2013081, where
        !                                      seven nodes left it
        !   merge_bottom_nodes      2          unchanged: at thirteen nodes only
        !                                      node 2 is still below the level
        !                                      seven nodes was lifting
        !
        ! WHAT IT BUYS, at the same 0.700 per cent priced out: the step between
        ! nodes falls 121 -> 46 per cent, the mass parked at
        ! bottom_productivity_level falls 6.45 -> 1.67 per cent, and the bottom
        ! node earns 0.0880 rather than 0.0507 against a food stamp floor that
        ! does not move, which is the only handle emp_homeless has.
        integer, parameter :: employment_base_size=13
        ! THE CHILD'S INHERITED PENALTY AS A STATE, on the author's instruction:
        ! *"you can basically have another state var: 1,2,3, where 1 is a child
        ! of a parent with SUD, 2 is for a child with parent who was homeless,
        ! and 3 is for a child of a parent with both. and each of these reduces
        ! the productivity of the child in each y productivity it lives"*, and
        ! *"you dont need move productivity grids....you have 7 like before. and
        ! then you multiple the actual productivity by the penalty for each case
        ! (1/2/3)"*.
        !
        ! WHY IT HAD TO BECOME A STATE. The penalty used to be a level shift
        ! applied ONCE, to the newborn's draw, and the AR(1) that carries
        ! productivity through life then pulled it back toward the mean at
        ! persistence_employment_surviving = 0.9. The present value of a shift
        ! that decays at 0.9 over a forty year working life is 41 per cent of a
        ! shift that does not, so a nominal 24 per cent penalty was worth an
        ! effective 10.7 per cent and the child disorder ratio it had to explain
        ! came out at 1.11 against 2. A permanent multiplicative factor cannot be
        ! carried by a process whose only persistence is 0.9; it needs its own
        ! index, and this is it.
        !
        ! HOW IT IS PACKED. NOT a new array rank -- the value function and the
        ! distribution are rank seven and every one of the hundreds of index
        ! sites would have to change. The class is folded into the productivity
        ! index instead: index (c-1)*employment_base_size + k carries base node k
        ! scaled by child_penalty_factor(c-1), and employment_process is block
        ! diagonal, so a household never changes class. Every existing
        ! employment_grid(age,employment_index) site is then correct with no edit
        ! at all, because the grid it reads already has the penalty in it.
        !
        ! WHAT IT COSTS: the state space, and so the solve, is
        ! child_penalty_classes times bigger. At 1 the model is exactly the one
        ! that came before -- one class, factor one, no expansion -- which is the
        ! null this was built against.
        ! TWO CLASSES FROM 16 September, on the author's instruction "yes, set
        ! child_penalty_classes to 2 and rebuild", after they asked why the child
        ! SUD odds ratio was so low and whether the code was wrong.
        !
        ! IT WAS, FOR THIS PURPOSE. At ONE class the else-branch below puts
        ! delta_d in the BIRTH SHIFT, which erodes at
        ! persistence_employment_surviving = 0.9 -- a half-life of about seven
        ! periods, so the penalty has largely decayed before the child is old
        ! enough to be at risk. child_penalty_factor(1) = (1-delta_d), the
        ! PERMANENT form, was computed every loop and never used, because with
        ! one class only child_penalty_factor(0) = 1 is in play.
        !
        ! That is why the scan over the author's full [0, 0.4] range returned a
        ! straight line from 1.00995 to 1.06042 against a target of 2.0, and why
        ! it contradicted the declaration comment on penalty_child_addicted,
        ! which computes what a PERMANENT 61 per cent earnings cut would do and
        ! gets a ratio of 2. Both were right about different objects: the comment
        ! about a permanent cut, the scan about a decaying one.
        !
        ! At TWO classes class_1 = d_index, so the parent's disorder at death is
        ! a permanent class carrying (1-delta_d) on the child's productivity,
        ! which is what delta_d is meant to be.
        !
        ! NOTHING IS LOST. The two-class branch keeps the housing term
        ! (1-penalty_child_homeless)**((h_index-1)*include_child_penalty)
        ! unchanged, and penalty_child_homeless is 0.0, so that term is one
        ! either way.
        !
        ! WHAT IT COSTS: the state space doubles, so the solve is slower, and
        ! every moment in the table moves. The scan's answer of "2 is not
        ! reachable" was measured under ONE class and does not carry over.
        ! BACK TO ONE, on the author's *"revert to 1 class and pin delta_d until
        ! the rest of the calibration is over ... because the two classes is
        ! expensive"*. The two-class run it was set for could NOT answer: cold
        ! started, the SUD-parent class stayed at exactly 0.0 mass for two blocks,
        ! so the odds ratio had an empty denominator and the scan never ran. The
        ! class fills generationally and a cold start does not reach that quickly.
        !
        ! SO delta_d IS NOT "SET THERE" -- nothing was measured, and delta_d stays
        ! where it was. The permanent measurement still has to be taken, and it
        ! needs a warm distribution BUILT AT TWO CLASSES, not a cold start.
        !
        ! AND THE ONE-CLASS CHILD PENALTY IS STILL TRANSITORY. Everything in the
        ! block below about the birth shift eroding at 0.9 remains true, so the
        ! child SUD row will read near 1.0 for the rest of the calibration and
        ! should be IGNORED rather than chased. Two classes has to come back
        ! before the final model is believed.
        ! TWO CLASSES, 18 September, on the author's instruction to move to the
        ! two-class model, calibrate delta_d, converge the equilibrium and then
        ! scan omega_c. This is the change the delta_d target needs: at one
        ! class the penalty sits in the birth shift and erodes at
        ! persistence_employment_surviving = 0.9, so the scan over [0, 0.4]
        ! returned a straight line from 1.00995 to 1.06042 against a target of
        ! 2.0. At two classes class_1 = d_index, the child is born into a block
        ! whose nodes are already scaled, and the penalty is permanent.
        !
        ! IT DOUBLES THE STATE SPACE. employment_grid_size becomes
        ! employment_base_size*2 = 26, blocks go from about four minutes to
        ! somewhere between twelve and thirty, and the distribution file goes to
        ! roughly 265 MB. The economy moves, so b, pi_V and the rest have to
        ! re-converge before the odds ratio is readable.
        integer, parameter :: child_penalty_classes=1
        integer, parameter :: employment_grid_size=employment_base_size*child_penalty_classes
        integer :: use_unemployment_by_productivity=1
        ! Persistent income risk over working life. See the block beside
        ! employment_process for why this is not fixed_productivity=0.
        integer :: persistent_income_risk=0
        real(8) :: rho_productivity=0.98d0

        ! THE BOTTOM OF THE GRID IS NOW TWO CALIBRATED INSTRUMENTS, on the
        ! author's instruction of 3 September: *"you'd have to tinker with the
        ! lowest productivity (and its mass) such that you match the share of
        ! the homeless who are employed....it should almost be a target for the
        ! calibration"*.
        !
        !   y_grid_kappa             sets the bottom node's MASS, exactly
        !                            (1/employment_grid_size)**y_grid_kappa
        !   bottom_productivity_level sets the bottom node's LEVEL, as a
        !                            multiple of mean productivity
        !
        ! They are the instruments for the two moments that say WHO the homeless
        ! are, both from Meyer, Wyse, Meyer, Grunwaldt and Wu (2024), NBER 32323,
        ! and both untargeted until now: the employed share of the homeless at
        ! 0.46, and the income of the homeless at 0.191 of average. Two moments,
        ! two instruments. On the seventeen node grid the model had them at 0.91
        ! and 1.015 -- its homeless were comfortable and nearly all worked.
        !
        ! bottom_productivity_level=0 LEAVES THE GRID ALONE, which is the default
        ! and reproduces the quantile construction exactly.
        !
        ! MEAN PRODUCTIVITY STAYS EXACTLY ONE. The author allowed it to drift --
        ! *"It's ok to override the exact average... It doesn't have to be
        ! exactly 1"* -- and then gave the better instruction: *"choose the point
        ! on the y grid such that it gives the right productivity and the right
        ! mass exactly for what we need that still preserves the mean of 1"*.
        ! The nodes above the bottom carry a compensating factor
        ! (1-m_1 y_new)/(1-m_1 y_old), which restores the mean exactly and leaves
        ! their relative spacing alone. Verified: 1.0000000000 before and after,
        ! at overrides of 0.020, 0.030 and 0.045.
        real(8) :: y_grid_kappa=3.2d0
        real(8) :: bottom_productivity_level=0.d0
        real(8) :: bpl_old_1=0
        ! The rate the value-of-life constraint raises the productivity floor
        ! at, per loop, while any household still prefers death.
        real(8), parameter :: bottom_productivity_floor_rate=0.01d0
        ! HOW MANY BOTTOM NODES bottom_productivity_level COVERS.
        ! 1 is the old behaviour exactly. 2 sets nodes 1 and 2 to the same
        ! level, which IS a merged node of their combined mass, and the
        ! rescale above uses their mass-weighted mean so the grid mean stays
        ! one. It exists because node 2 carries fifteen times node 1's mass
        ! and neither can afford the cheapest rental.
        real(8) :: merge_bottom_nodes=1.d0

        ! WHERE THE MERGE STARTS. The merge above collapses nodes 1..n onto one
        ! level, so it can only ever lift the bottom node along with everything
        ! it merges. The author, 6 September: *"keep the lowest productivity at
        ! 0.2% of the population and add the second lowest to the 3rd lowest.
        ! then 0.2% will be shut out of the smallest h2, and half will be
        ! employed homeless"*.
        !
        ! That needs the merge to act on nodes [k..n] instead of [1..n], so node
        ! one can stay poor and unhoused while node two is lifted out of the
        ! priced-out region. merge_from_node=2 with merge_bottom_nodes=3 is
        ! exactly the operation they describe.
        !
        ! WHY IT MATTERS FOR THE CALIBRATION, not just for the grid. The cheapest
        ! rental costs 1.64 times the WHOLE net income of the bottom node, and
        ! nodes one and two together are 3.9 per cent of the population against a
        ! homelessness target of 0.4. Merging node two upward leaves 0.24 per
        ! cent priced out, and it makes the homeless BE the bottom node, so that
        ! node's employment rate becomes emp_homeless -- the first handle on that
        ! moment that does not trade against h_1. Both price levers are exhausted
        ! at h_1 near 0.008 with emp_homeless at zero, measured; this attacks the
        ! residual from the quantity side instead.
        !
        ! ONE REPRODUCES THE OLD BEHAVIOUR EXACTLY. At merge_from_node=1 the
        ! low block is empty, its sum is zero, and the rescale factor below is
        ! algebraically the expression that was there before.
        real(8) :: merge_from_node=1.d0
        integer, parameter :: productivity_below_which_to_reduce_ss=employment_grid_size
        real(8) :: scaling_of_ss=(1-reduce_ss_by_half)*1+reduce_ss_by_half*0.5
        real(8) :: scaling_of_ss_adjuster=0
        real(8) :: distance_scaling_of_ss
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_scaling_of_ss=10.d0**(-3)
        
        real(8), parameter :: increased_risk_of_death_homeless=0.6 
        ! THIS IS THE MULTIPLIER MINUS ONE, not the multiplier. Survival is built
        ! as phi - k*(1 - phi) at line 16620ish, so the death probability comes out
        ! (1 + k) times the baseline. At k = 6.9 the effective mortality rate ratio
        ! was 7.9, which is not what the written model says.
        !
        ! SEVEN, FOR AN EFFECTIVE EIGHT, from 7 September. The author: *"wasnt the
        ! increased risk of death when addicted supposed to be 8??"*, *"we talked
        ! about it very extensive"*. They are right and this file was wrong.
        !
        ! code/stylized/stylized.py carries smr_d = 8.0 and says why: *"RAISED
        ! FROM 6.9 TO 8.0 so the two models use the same number. The full model
        ! calibrates kappa_phi_d externally at the MIDPOINT of the six to ten
        ! range Hjorthoj et al. (2023) report, and citing the same source for the
        ! same object while carrying two different values is exactly the
        ! inconsistency the AE's letter objects to."* The stylized model was
        ! raised; THIS ONE WAS NOT, so the two models cited Hjorthoj for the same
        ! object at 8.0 and 6.9 -- the inconsistency the letter objects to, live
        ! in the code. The comment this replaces asserted both models used 6.9,
        ! which stopped being true when the stylized model moved.
        !
        ! CLAUDE.md's standing rule is that a bug found in one model is checked
        ! against the other before it is closed. This is that rule failing in the
        ! other direction: a FIX made in the stylized model was never carried
        ! across, and nothing checked.
        !
        ! The LEVEL is calibrated in the second stage inside the Degenhardt range,
        ! so this is a starting value; what matters is that the two models now
        ! start from the same number and the units are the same in the code and
        ! in the note.
        !
        ! ONE DIFFERENCE REMAINS AND IS NOT ADDRESSED HERE. The stylized model
        ! applies the ratio to the HAZARD, phi**smr_d, which is what a ratio of
        ! rates means; this file multiplies the death PROBABILITY by (1+k). The
        ! two agree closely while the annual death probability is small and
        ! diverge at old ages -- at a baseline of 0.10 the probability form gives
        ! 0.80 and the hazard form 0.57. That is a separate correction and it
        ! needs the author.
        real(8) :: increased_risk_of_death_addicted=&
            have_increased_death_from_addiction*7.0+(1-have_increased_death_from_addiction)*size_of_penalty*5
        real(8) :: increased_risk_of_death_homeless_addicted=0

        ! THE MORTALITY MULTIPLIERS VARY WITH AGE, AND THE DATA FOR IT WAS
        ! ALREADY IN THE REPOSITORY AND UNREAD.
        !
        ! data/us_mortality_multipliers.csv has sixty rows, ages 26 to 85, with
        ! shape_sud, the CDC drug poisoning profile normalised to one at its
        ! peak, and rr_homeless, Baggett's all cause ratio by age. The code read
        ! neither file and used two flat excesses instead.
        !
        ! Equation (25) of the note:
        !
        !   kappa_phi_d(j) = 1 + (kappa_bar_phi_d - 1) * shape_sud(j)
        !   kappa_phi_H(j) = max{1, RR(j) / [(1-s) + s*kappa_phi_d(j)]}
        !
        ! with s the SUD share of the homeless, because Baggett's ratio is
        ! measured on a population that mostly also carries a SUD and using it
        ! raw would credit housing with a mortality effect that belongs to the
        ! disorder.
        !
        ! Survival is then MULTIPLICATIVE in the two, as the note has it. The old
        ! code added the two excesses, which is a different and larger number
        ! whenever both are large, and both are large exactly for the households
        ! this paper is about.
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: shape_sud_profile=0
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: rr_homeless_profile=0
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: kappa_phi_d_by_age=1
        ! SMI prevalence by age in the model, against the profile the onset
        ! identity is built to reproduce. The aggregate cannot say whether a
        ! shortfall is the newborn draw failing to arrive or the differential
        ! mortality of SMI households eroding it with age, and those call for
        ! opposite fixes. Read at age one it is the draw; read as a slope against
        ! the profile it is the mortality.
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: model_smi_mass_by_age=0
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: model_mass_by_age=0
        ! DRUG USE BY SINGLE AGE. The nine age groups put model ages 40 to 60 in
        ! one bucket, which is data 65 to 85, so a profile reading 1.0 in group
        ! nine cannot say whether onset begins the year work ends or fifteen
        ! years later. The two have different causes and different fixes.
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: model_users_by_age=0
        ! THE DENOMINATOR HAS TO BE COUNTED IN THE SAME PLACE AS THE NUMERATOR.
        ! model_mass_by_age is accumulated three thousand lines away in a
        ! different loop nest, and dividing one by the other gave a use rate of
        ! exactly 200 per cent at every age from 26 to 80: the two passes do not
        ! visit each household the same number of times. Counted here, beside the
        ! users, whatever the nest does to one it does to the other.
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: model_alive_by_age=0
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: kappa_phi_h_by_age=1
        ! ASSIGNED, at the midpoint of the literature range. All cause standardised
        ! mortality ratios for opioid use disorder run 6 to 10, so 8 is the middle
        ! of it rather than an old point estimate. This used to be calibrated on
        ! the drug price participation elasticity; that elasticity is now an
        ! untargeted validation moment instead.
        real(8) :: kappa_phi_d_level=8.0
        ! y_u's INSTRUMENT, WIRED 11 September. Until now the comment at the y_u
        ! print called kappa_phi_d_level "its instrument" and nothing connected the
        ! two: there was no adjuster, no distance and no update block, so y_u was a
        ! target with a target value that nothing in the loop ever moved toward,
        ! and the level was set by hand from the vector.
        !
        ! The author: "I also wonder if we need to start adjusting the probability
        ! of death to match the average income of a user... It is way too low",
        ! then "take the death probability to 5", then "after that let it adjust
        ! slowly, not more than 0.5% per iteration".
        !
        ! THE SIGN. The mortality penalty is the price of using, so a high one
        ! leaves only the most desperate willing to pay it and the users are poor.
        ! y_u BELOW target therefore calls for a LOWER level. The step is
        ! kappa - adjuster*(target - model), which is negative-going when the model
        ! is below target, so it needs no sign work. Measured across 8.0 -> 6.5,
        ! y_u went 0.333 -> 0.533, confirming the direction.
        !
        ! THE BOUNDS ARE THE LITERATURE'S. The author: "There are more studies thag
        ! find anywhere between 2-9", which widens the six-to-ten of Hjorthoj et al.
        ! (2023) that the hand-set 8.0 came from.
        ! NO LONGER TIED TO THE FREEZE, 12 September. The author: "i think you
        ! need to include kappa_phi_d". It was (1-freeze_to_three_block)*2.0,
        ! which is zero under stage 1, so the bracket never fired and y_u drifted
        ! unsteered from 0.2706 to 0.3489 over five loops.
        !
        ! This makes the first block SEVEN moments: h_1, h_2, b, lambda_d, aleph,
        ! omega_c and now y_u. Same one-line detachment already applied to
        ! weight_on_child_adjuster for the same reason.
        !
        ! The half-a-per-cent-a-loop cap still comes from plain cap_step, so the
        ! author's "not more than 0.5% per iteration" continues to hold.
        ! PINNED 12 September on the author's "let's set the mortality risk to
        ! 3.5 and pin it", after "it seems to make it hard to converge no b".
        !
        ! Their diagnosis, and the trace carries it.  This parameter is the
        ! excess mortality of drug users, and while it was bracketed on y_u it
        ! fell every loop -- 4.6137, 4.5676, 4.5219, 4.4767, 4.4319, 4.3876 --
        ! because y_u was below target and the step is
        ! kappa - adjuster*(data - model).  A falling death risk keeps more
        ! addicts alive, so the STOCK rises, and lambda_d rose monotonically
        ! over the same twelve loops to +179 per cent while its own instrument,
        ! addiction_scalar_1, was pinned to one per cent a loop by
        ! max_rel_step_all and moved a total of +5.1 per cent.  Two brackets
        ! were pushing the addict stock in opposite directions through the
        ! economy and the mortality one was winning.
        !
        ! Pinning it costs y_u its instrument.  y_u was the best-converging
        ! moment in the run -- -63.4 per cent at loop 1 to -11.7 at loop 13,
        ! monotone for ten loops -- so this is a real cost, knowingly paid: a
        ! moment cannot be chased while its driver is being moved by another
        ! bracket.  3.5 sits inside the range the author cited from the
        ! literature, "anywhere between 2-9".
        !
        ! The update block at 43069 is guarded by
        ! if (kappa_phi_d_level_adjuster>0), and its else branch at 43114 sets
        ! distance_kappa_phi_d_level to zero, so pinning it here also takes it
        ! out of the do-while cleanly.  No orphaned distance, no infinite loop.
        ! UN-PINNED, on the author's instruction of 13 September: *"calibrate the
        ! death risk to match yu but move it at no more than 0.1% per
        ! iteration"*.  It was pinned on 12 September because it fought
        ! lambda_d: a falling death risk keeps more addicts alive so the stock
        ! rises, and the two brackets pushed the addict stock in opposite
        ! directions with the mortality one winning.  That history is above and
        ! it has not been repealed by measurement -- watch lambda_d.  What is
        ! different now is the RATE: 0.1 per cent a loop against the global half
        ! a per cent, so the mortality bracket can no longer outrun the stock's
        ! own instrument.
        ! PINNED AT 2 AND NOT UPDATED AT ALL, on the author's instruction of
        ! 14 September: *"yes, pin kappa_phid=2...dont update it"*, after
        ! *"thats why the model collpses! keep it fixed at 2"* and *"because you
        ! allowed kappaphid to be so low! that explains why we had such a bad
        ! equilibirum"*.
        !
        ! WHY THE 0.1 PER CENT CAP WAS NOT ENOUGH.  kappa_phi_d_level is
        ! bracketed on y_u, the income of drug users, and y_u sat at 0.333
        ! against a target of 0.74 -- 55 per cent below.  So the update printed
        ! "LOWERED: y_u is below target" every single loop and walked the
        ! parameter monotonically off the pin, 2 -> 1.978 in twenty loops, with
        ! kappa_phi_d_level_lo = 1 waiting underneath.  The cap set the SPEED of
        ! the collapse, not whether it happened; only a zero adjuster stops it.
        !
        ! y_u's instrument is multiple_of_probabilities_of_employment_if_addicted,
        ! which is what it is for.  It is held at 1 in the current block and opens
        ! in the next one.
        ! MOVING AGAIN, DOWN AND SLOWLY, on the author's instruction of 14
        ! September: *"I guess we need to allow kappa_phid to move down slowly....
        ! At maybe 0.1% between iterations"*.  This reverses the pin made earlier
        ! the same day at their instruction, and the reversal is recorded rather
        ! than the earlier answer being quietly overwritten.
        !
        ! WHY IT IS THE RIGHT LEVER NOW.  y_u decomposes: 38 per cent of users
        ! have the disorder and earn 0.734 of average income against a datum of
        ! 0.711, which is ON TARGET, while the 62 per cent who are experimenters
        ! earn 0.343 -- less than half as much -- and the blend is y_u = 0.490
        ! against 0.740.  So the failure is the EXPERIMENTATION MARGIN, not the
        ! addicted.  kappa_phi_d is the mortality multiplier and so the price of
        ! using: lowering it makes use worth trying to households further up the
        ! income distribution, which is exactly the margin that is broken.
        !
        ! WHAT HAS NOT CHANGED.  The update is still ONE-SIGNED while y_u is below
        ! target -- the step is kappa - adj*(target - y_u), so every loop lowers
        ! it -- and kappa_phi_d_level_lo is 1, where the file's own measurement
        ! puts drug use at 95 to 97 per cent.  The 0.1 per cent cap in
        ! kappa_phi_d_max_rel_step sets the SPEED, not whether it arrives: 2 -> 1
        ! is about seven hundred loops, but 2 -> 1.64 is only two hundred, and
        ! that is already well into the range where the drug block degrades.  The
        ! adjuster is 2 so that the step saturates the cap; the cap is what
        ! governs.
        ! PINNED AT 1.7, on the author's instruction of 14 September: *"Try kappa
        ! 1.7"*.  The descent had reached 1.70819 and the next gated step would
        ! have carried it past; this holds it there so every other instrument
        ! settles AROUND kappa = 1.7 and y_u can be read at b on target rather
        ! than mid-descent.
        !
        ! WHAT THE DESCENT ESTABLISHED, and it corrects two calls made here
        ! earlier today.  Correcting each gated y_u to b = 0.089 with the fitted
        ! slope of 2.585:
        !
        !     kappa >= 1.80   eight steps, mean corrected y_u 0.4903
        !     kappa <  1.80   six steps,   mean corrected y_u 0.5317
        !
        ! The lever is DORMANT above about 1.8 and bites below it, harder the
        ! lower it goes -- the last two steps took corrected y_u from 0.524 to
        ! 0.585.  The fitted elasticity went 0 -> -0.53 -> -1.06 as the parameter
        ! travelled.  "The lever is spent" was reported here twice from the flat
        ! stretch above 1.8, before the informative range had been reached, and
        ! both times it was wrong.  The rule it breaks is already in this file: a
        ! result measured at one value of a parameter is a result about that value
        ! until it is measured at another -- which cuts BOTH ways, and a null
        ! measured in a dead region is not a null.
        ! LIVE AGAIN AND GATED.  The author, 15 September: *"you need to keep
        ! lowering kappa to match yu"*.  It is the y_u instrument and it chases
        ! y_u on its own, so theta and h_2 can move underneath it without y_u
        ! being left behind.
        !
        ! The gate is what makes this safe: a step is taken only when b is within
        ! kappa_gate_b_tolerance of its target, so kappa never reads a refill as
        ! signal.  Without it, every step during a transient pushed the same way,
        ! because the update is one-signed while y_u is below target.
        ! PINNED, AND y_u IS UNTARGETED.  The author, 15 September: *"i guess we
        ! can set kappa=2 and let yu be untargeted"*, then *"yes, set kappa=2 and
        ! delta_d=0.24"*.
        !
        ! WHY THIS IS A COHERENT CHOICE RATHER THAN A RETREAT.  Every moment that
        ! depends on how concentrated drug use is among the poor runs through
        ! kappa, and they disagree with y_u about which end of it they want.
        ! Measured tonight, settled:
        !
        !     kappa 2.0    SUD odds 7.2, homeless odds 3.03, y_u 0.497
        !     kappa 1.4    SUD odds 1.1, homeless odds 1.46, y_u 0.706
        !
        ! The two odds ratios want kappa high and y_u wants it low, and there is
        ! no value satisfying both.  Choosing kappa = 2 buys both odds ratios and
        ! makes y_u a reported gap rather than a target.
        ! LIVE AGAIN 15 September, on the author's instruction: "we need to start
        ! updating kappa to match yu now when b and SUD are close to their
        ! targets". y_u reads 0.94 against 0.74, 27 per cent high, and kappa is
        ! the instrument that owns it.
        !
        ! THE b GATE BELOW IS EXACTLY THE 'WHEN b IS CLOSE' CONDITION. kappa only
        ! steps while |b - target| <= kappa_gate_b_tolerance * target, so it
        ! cannot read a refill as signal. Nothing extra is needed to honour the
        ! instruction; releasing the adjuster is enough.
        !
        ! MEASURED WARNING, and it was taken at a DIFFERENT theta. The kappa
        ! bracket of this morning, at theta 0.108 with b at target, has y_u
        ! NON-MONOTONE in kappa with a minimum near 2.4: 0.572 at 1.9, 0.483 at
        ! 2.4, 0.504 at 3.0. If that survives at theta 0.21 then raising kappa
        ! RAISES y_u, the opposite of what is wanted. It may well not survive --
        ! the drug block is three times the size here -- but read the first few
        ! gated steps before trusting the direction.
        ! PINNED 17 September, on the author's *"Should we move kappa_phid to
        ! about 3.5? And pin it and see what happens?"*, with the level set to
        ! 3.5 from the handover.
        !
        ! IT WAS ALREADY PINNED, BY ACCIDENT. The b gate has been shut for
        ! nearly the whole session -- every block of n47 printed "KAPPA GATE
        ! SHUT" -- so 2.9428 was not a value the bracket chose, it was a value
        ! the gate froze. Pinning deliberately at a level the author has a view
        ! on is strictly better than pinning at an accident.
        !
        ! WHY IT IS A TEST AND NOT A CALIBRATION. The direction is unknown. The
        ! bracket above says y_u is non-monotone in kappa with a minimum near
        ! 2.4, which would mean raising kappa from 2.9428 RAISES y_u -- but that
        ! was measured at theta 0.108 with y_u under 0.6 throughout, and this
        ! economy runs theta 0.19 with y_u at 1.10, so neither its levels nor
        ! its shape can be assumed to carry.
        !
        ! HOW TO READ IT, and this matters more than the result. n47 held kappa
        ! at 2.9428 for ten blocks while b rose 0.0280 -> 0.0639 on its own, and
        ! y_u fell 1.1753 -> 1.1010 and LEVELLED there. So y_u depends on b and
        ! the comparison must be taken at MATCHED b, not at a matched block
        ! number. The reference is y_u = 1.1010 at b = 0.0639, kappa 2.9428. If
        ! y_u at 3.5 comes in below that at the same b, the minimum is below
        ! 2.9428 and kappa should keep rising; if above, the minimum is between
        ! and the next point to take is 2.4.
        real(8) :: kappa_phi_d_level_adjuster=0.d0
        ! ITS OWN RATE, the way theta and omega_c have theirs.
        ! HALVED AGAIN, on the author's instruction of 13 September: *"move
        ! omegac and death rate from drug at maybe 0.05% per iteration, but
        ! allow b to move at 0.5% between iterations"*.  It was 0.1 per cent
        ! from that morning's instruction; this puts the mortality bracket at
        ! the same rate as omega_c's and a tenth of b's.
        !
        ! THE ORDERING IS THE POINT, not the absolute size.  The history above
        ! is a fight between this bracket and lambda_d's over the addict stock,
        ! and the stock's own instrument is addiction_scalar_1.  At 0.05 against
        ! b's 0.5 the mortality bracket moves an order of magnitude slower than
        ! the instrument it was outrunning, which is what the ranking is for.
        ! NOT ON THE DRUG BLOCK'S RATE.  The author, 14 September: *"thats why
        ! the model collpses! keep it fixed at 2 and if you do update it, it
        ! cannot be updated faster than 0.1% per iteration!"*
        !
        ! kappa_phi_d is the mortality multiplier and participation in this
        ! model is a mortality phenomenon -- measured on 14 September, at
        ! kappa_phi_d = 1 drug use runs 95 to 97 per cent and no b over a
        ! twenty-four fold range moves it two points, while at 2 it is single
        ! digits.  So this parameter has more leverage over the drug block than
        ! anything else in the file and it does not belong at the block's rate.
        ! It is pinned by its adjuster in apply_first_block_freeze; this cap is
        ! the second line of defence if it is ever released again.
        ! ONE PER CENT, NOT 0.1, NOW THAT THE STEP IS GATED.  The author, 14
        ! September: *"and I think when you do this you can move kappa a bit
        ! faster. Maybe 1%"*.
        !
        ! The two changes belong together.  0.1 per cent was the right rate for an
        ! UNGATED update, where most steps were taken on a y_u contaminated by the
        ! refill and the rate's job was to limit the damage each misreading did.
        ! Gated, a step is only ever taken when b is within ten per cent of target
        ! and y_u is a composition at a near-fixed count, so the steps are
        ! informative and there is no reason to crawl.
        !
        ! WHAT IT COSTS.  Ten times the speed toward the floor as well.  The file's
        ! own measurement, kappa 8.0 -> 6.5 moving y_u 0.333 -> 0.533, is an
        ! elasticity of -2.26; carried down to kappa 2 it says y_u = 0.740 wants
        ! kappa near 1.62, which is BELOW the 1.85 this run is watched at and
        ! inside the range where the drug block degrades.  That extrapolation is
        ! from a measurement taken at kappa 8 and is not to be trusted at 2 -- it
        ! is a reason to watch, not a prediction.
        real(8) :: kappa_phi_d_max_rel_step=0.01d0
        ! THE GATE: kappa_phi_d ONLY MOVES WHEN b IS NEAR ITS TARGET.
        !
        ! The author, 14 September: *"What you need to check is whether yu is high
        ! when kappa is lower when b is on target. I think maybe the best way to
        ! adjust kappa is only when b is close to target, not when it's far"*.
        !
        ! WHY. kappa_phi_d is bracketed on y_u, the income of drug users, and y_u
        ! is a COMPOSITION statistic: it says who the users are, not how many.
        ! While the drug stock is refilling, the composition changes for reasons
        ! that have nothing to do with kappa -- measured across a restart, y_u
        ! swung 0.20 -> 0.49 -> 0.40 -> 0.48 purely as b climbed from 0.023 to
        ! 0.086. A kappa step taken anywhere in that swing is reading the refill
        ! and calling it a signal, and because the step is ONE-SIGNED while y_u is
        ! below target, every such misreading pushes the same way.
        !
        ! So the gate is not a refinement, it is what makes the bracket mean
        ! anything: only when b sits inside kappa_gate_b_tolerance of its own
        ! target is y_u a statement about composition at a fixed count.
        !
        ! TEN PER CENT, matching the tolerance the author's staged plan uses
        ! elsewhere for "close enough".
        real(8), parameter :: kappa_gate_b_tolerance=0.10d0
        real(8) :: distance_kappa_phi_d_level=1
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_kappa_phi_d_level=10.d0**(-2)
        ! FLOOR AT ONE, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION: *"you should allow
        ! kappa_phid to be as low as 1"*. It was 2, and being a parameter rather
        ! than a variable it could not be reached by the handover either -- the
        ! handover's own clamp already floors at 1, so the two disagreed and the
        ! tighter one won silently inside the update.
        !
        ! The floor matters now because y_u reads 0.41 against a target of 0.74
        ! at the best point, and the author's rule is that y_u below target
        ! means the death risk comes DOWN. A floor of 2 would have stopped the
        ! instrument before its moment, and the log would have shown it stepping
        ! every loop and never arriving.
        real(8), parameter :: kappa_phi_d_level_lo=1.d0
        real(8), parameter :: kappa_phi_d_level_hi=9.d0

        ! THE AUTHOR'S ACCEPTANCE BAND ON y_u, 17 September: *"if yu is close
        ! to target when b is close to target just keep it there and don't
        ! touch it. If yu is around 0.6-0.75 that's ok"*. The target of 0.74
        ! sits near the top of it, so the band is deliberately asymmetric: it
        ! buys room BELOW the target and almost none above.
        !
        ! Same idiom as the h_1 band. Inside it the bracket holds rather than
        ! chasing, which is what stops a parameter walking away from a value
        ! that is already acceptable.
        real(8), parameter :: yu_band_lo=0.60d0
        ! TOP RAISED 17 September, from 0.75. The author: *"if you think that
        ! yu will be close to 0.74 even from above, like 0.78, we should not
        ! move kappa_phid"*, then *"I can accept all yu between 0.6-0.8"*.
        !
        ! IT WAS LEAVING kappa TO CREEP. n32's two gated blocks read y_u at
        ! 0.78692 and 0.77713, both above the old 0.75 top, so the hold branch
        ! never fired and the rule raised kappa on each one -- 2.8280 to 2.8563
        ! to 2.8848. Not moving kappa at 0.78 REQUIRES the band to contain 0.78;
        ! saying so is not enough.
        !
        ! And 0.78 is where b on target puts it. Sorting every n32 block by b:
        ! above six per cent y_u is stable and FALLING in b -- 0.808 at 0.062,
        ! 0.790 at 0.078, 0.787 at 0.093 -- while below three per cent it ranges
        ! 0.31 to 1.81 on a few hundred households and means nothing. The
        ! selection reading explains it: participation needs R above a resource
        ! threshold, so the fewer users there are the more selected and the
        ! richer they are.
        real(8), parameter :: yu_band_hi=0.80d0
        real(8), parameter :: sud_share_of_homeless=0.217

        ! THE SMI COMORBIDITY RATIO, SUD given SMI over SUD given no SMI.
        !
        ! NSDUH 2024, adults 26 and over, weighted by ANALWT2_C, SUD from
        ! svyrdudany in (2,3) and SMI from SMIPY: 0.20426 against 0.02797.
        ! The same file and the same variable the level targets come from, and
        ! the universe check reproduces 0.0890 and 0.0368 exactly.
        !
        ! NOT SCALED BY 1.18, because it is a ratio of two rates and a common
        ! scaling cancels, exactly as for sud_share_of_homeless above.
        !
        ! IT IS THE RATIO AGAINST NON-SMI, NOT AGAINST THE POPULATION. Against
        ! the population it is 5.55, and multiplying a population rate by 7.30
        ! double counts. Against adults with NO mental illness at all it is
        ! 12.77, so 7.30 is the conservative summary: the non-SMI comparison
        ! group contains 38 million adults with mild or moderate mental illness
        ! running at 3.8 and 7.6 times the clean baseline.
        real(8), parameter :: sud_smi_ratio_data=7.30

        ! FLAT EXCESS DEATH RISK FROM HOMELESSNESS. See build_kappa_phi.
        ! The default is Baggett's own implied excess at age 26, so the youngest
        ! household's deterrent is unchanged and only the AGE GRADIENT in it is
        ! removed. It is a handover parameter so it can be calibrated.
        ! 0 = Baggett's age varying ratios, the status quo
        ! 1 = flat excess RISK, so the multiplier falls with age
        ! 2 = flat MULTIPLIER of 1.6, which external_calibration.tex quotes
        ! A vector coordinate, so all three run from one build.
        ! ZERO: THE BAGGETT RELATIVE-RISK PROFILE. The author, 2 September, first
        ! "use =1" and then, once the three settings were laid out, "ah, then use
        ! Baggett relative-risk profile" -- which is this one.
        !
        ! Setting 0 uses rr_homeless_profile(age), Baggett et al.'s measured
        ! age-varying relative risk read from us_mortality_multipliers.csv,
        ! divided by the share of the excess that the SUD mortality already
        ! carries so the two do not double count. Setting 1 is NOT the profile:
        ! it holds the excess RISK flat at Baggett's implied excess AT AGE 26
        ! alone and lets the multiplier fall out of baseline mortality. Setting 2
        ! is a flat 1.6 from Meyer, Wyse and Logani.
        integer :: homeless_death_risk_flat_in_age=0
        ! HOLD BAGGETT'S RELATIVE RISK FLAT ABOVE THIS DATA AGE. Zero is off.
        !
        ! CORRECTION, 4 September: this was first written here as the tail being
        ! PADDING, an interpolation to an assumed floor. That is wrong.
        ! make_mortality_multipliers.py builds rr_homeless from Baggett's three
        ! band ratios -- 9.1 at 25-44, 4.5 at 45-64, 1.1 at 65-84 -- interpolated
        ! between band MIDPOINTS and held flat past the last one at 74.5. So the
        ! 1.100 from 75 to 85 is Baggett's own 65-84 figure, extended. It is data.
        !
        ! THE REASON TO OVERRIDE IT IS NOT THAT IT IS INVENTED BUT THAT IT IS THE
        ! WRONG OBJECT, and make_mortality_multipliers.py says so itself:
        !
        !   "kappa_phi_h is floored at 1.0. At 65+ the arithmetic returns less
        !    than one, which read literally says homelessness protects the old.
        !    That is survivor selection, not causation, and the model cannot
        !    represent selection on unobserved frailty."
        !
        ! Baggett's 1.1 is OBSERVATIONAL: homeless 65-84 year olds die at 1.1
        ! times the general population's rate. What this model needs is CAUSAL:
        ! what happens to a seventy five year old who is put on the street. The
        ! two diverge badly at this age, and both biases push the ratio toward
        ! one -- the homeless who REACH 65 are a heavily selected, unusually
        ! robust group, and the 65+ comparison group includes the frail and the
        ! institutionalised. Taken literally the number says the street is
        ! protective, which is why the code has to floor it at one.
        !
        ! The same script flags the opposite bias young: selection INTO
        ! homelessness is on adverse traits, so 9.1 at 26 probably overstates the
        ! causal effect. Both ends are contaminated, in opposite directions.
        !
        ! The model takes it as one, and it is the biggest deterrent the model
        ! has: sleeping rough multiplies a 26 year old's death hazard by 4.41 and
        ! an 80 year old's by 1.09. The street is nearly free once you are old,
        ! and 88 per cent of the model's homeless are retired against 25 per cent
        ! of the population.
        !
        ! Setting this to 70 holds the relative risk at its last measured value
        ! for every age above 70, leaving every age at or below it exactly as
        ! Baggett measured it. The author, 4 September: "we just hold it after
        ! age 70, let's see if it works", and "i think it's easy to say that we
        ! just compute the death probability and hold it fixed after age 70".
        !
        ! It holds the MULTIPLIER, not the absolute excess probability. Baseline
        ! mortality rises steeply after 70, so holding the multiplier lets the
        ! excess probability rise with age, and holding the excess probability
        ! would instead let the multiplier fall back toward one -- which is the
        ! shape being corrected, in a milder form.
        ! THE RELATIVE RISK ABOVE THE HOLD AGE, CALIBRATED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
        ! Zero leaves the hold behaviour alone, so the default is unchanged.
        !
        ! The author, 4 September: "I think probably the issue is that the
        ! probability of dying if you are homeless at 84-85 is much higher than
        ! baggetes number... We might actually need to set that number for the
        ! old to match their homelessness rate since those data are actually not
        ! reliable probably".
        !
        ! That follows from what holding at 65 already established. Baggett's
        ! 65-84 ratio is an OBSERVED ratio contaminated by survivor selection --
        ! the homeless who reach 65 are unusually robust and the comparison group
        ! includes the frail and the institutionalised -- so it does not identify
        ! the causal effect the model needs, and taken literally it says the
        ! street protects the old. Holding it at its age-65 value replaced an
        ! unusable number with an ASSUMED one. Calibrating it replaces the
        ! assumption with the moment it most directly controls, which is the old
        ! band's share of the homeless.
        !
        ! A number that cannot be measured credibly is better calibrated than
        ! guessed, and this one now has a target instead of a stipulation.
        real(8) :: rr_homeless_old_level=0
        integer :: rr_homeless_hold_age=0
        real(8) :: homeless_excess_death_risk=0.00396d0

        ! FOOD STAMPS PER HOUSEHOLD. cbar is the second level-over-mean moment:
        ! govt_spending_on_food_stamps is an AGGREGATE over the distribution and
        ! model_average_labour_income is a MEAN, so the ratio scaled with total
        ! mass. It cannot be normalised in place the way model_value_of_life
        ! can, because the aggregate is also the government budget's spending
        ! line (12943) and the results file's level (29603). So the per-household
        ! figure is computed once, where the pooled mass is known, and the moment
        ! reads this instead.
        real(8), dimension(transition_length) :: model_food_stamps_per_household=0
        integer :: mortality_profiles_loaded=0
                            
        ! UNEMPLOYMENT INSURANCE IS NOW CERTAIN FOR THE FIRST YEAR OF A SPELL
        ! and pays nothing after it. This removes a parameter rather than a
        ! state: a household in its first year of unemployment and one in its
        ! third still have different income, the replacement rate against zero,
        ! and different continuation values, so the two do not merge. The three
        ! employment states stay, and with them the long spells that are what
        ! actually cost a household its housing.
        ! THE SHARE OF JOB LOSERS WHO GET AN INSURED PERIOD AT ALL.
        !
        ! It was ONE, so every household that lost work entered emp_of 3, drew
        ! the replacement rate for a period, and only then fell to emp_of 2,
        ! which pays zeta_DI times the base and so, at zeta_DI = 0, pays
        ! nothing. The consequence was not the transfer but the STATE: working
        ! age participation came out at exactly 1.000 with and without the
        ! disorder, because emp_of 2 could only ever be reached through an
        ! insured period first and the model had no household that simply had no
        ! income.
        !
        ! That is why the homeless are 89 per cent employed against 46 in the
        ! data. Homelessness could only ever be drawn from the 5.7 per cent
        ! temporarily unemployed on UI, never from households with nothing.
        !
        ! In SCF 2022, 13.9 per cent of households aged 26 to 64 have NO wage
        ! income, and the model had none at all. About a quarter to a third of
        ! unemployed Americans actually receive UI -- most are ineligible, have
        ! exhausted it, or never applied -- and that share is lower still for the
        ! unstable work histories this paper is about. So 0.30, externally
        ! assigned from UI receipt rather than calibrated.
        !
        ! The duration structure needed no change: emp_of 3 already transits to
        ! emp_of 1 on re-employment and to emp_of 2 otherwise, so the insured
        ! period lasts exactly one period and always did. What was wrong was
        ! only that everybody got one.
        real(8), parameter :: probability_of_ui=0.30

        ! UI DURATION. With this at one an unbroken unemployment spell is
        ! insured in its first period only and pays nothing after, which is what
        ! the paper describes and what employment_transition's own comment
        ! claimed it already did. At zero the spell stays in whichever state it
        ! entered, so UI runs for as long as the spell does; that was the
        ! behaviour before, and it is kept switchable because the two differ a
        ! great deal in how costly the disorder's unemployment risk is.
        integer, parameter :: ui_first_period_only=1
                
        real(8) :: utility_improvement=include_addiction*0
        real(8) :: utility_improvement_adjuster=0
        real(8) :: distance_utility_improvement
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_utility_improvement=10.d0**(-3)
        
        real(8), dimension(2) :: ss_decrease_addicted=ss_decrease_for_retired_addicted*&
            include_addiction*(/0,0/)
        
        ! REMOVED FROM THE MODEL. The written budget constraint has no consumption tax.
        ! It was already zero, so nothing changes numerically, but it is kept at
        ! zero as a parameter rather than deleted because it appears in a dozen
        ! expressions and threading it out of all of them risks more than it
        ! gains. Anything that reads it reads a hard zero.
        real(8), parameter :: consumption_tax=0
        ! FOOD STAMPS ARE REMOVED FROM THE MODEL, 12 September, on the author's
        ! instruction: *"i dont need food stamps in this model"*.  At zero,
        ! snap_benefit = max(0, max_food_cost - taper*net_income) is zero for
        ! every household and the programme is gone rather than merely uncalibrated.
        !
        ! WHAT IT CHANGES.  This was a MEANS-TESTED CONSUMPTION FLOOR, so removing
        ! it takes consumption away from the poorest households -- which is exactly
        ! the margin that decides drug use, homelessness, and whether a household's
        ! value of life is positive.  b, h_1, y_u and the VSL all move, and the
        ! point archive resets.  The moment it was calibrated on was ON TARGET when
        ! it was removed, 0.0042438 against 0.0042, so this is a modelling choice
        ! and not a repair.
        !
        ! The author was asked on this once before today and said to KEEP food
        ! stamps; this reverses that, and the reversal is recorded rather than the
        ! earlier answer being quietly overwritten.
        real(8) :: max_food_cost=0.d0
        ! THE BENEFIT REDUCTION RATE, the statutory SNAP taper. The benefit is
        ! the maximum allotment less thirty cents per dollar of net income
        ! rather than a top up to a hard floor. It used to be a hard floor,
        ! which is not the programme and, more to the point, is not a rule the
        ! stylized model of Section 2 can carry: a hard floor gives every
        ! household below it the SAME income, so one atom of households crosses
        ! the homelessness margin together and the rate jumps from zero to 1.72
        ! per cent with nothing in between, never taking the 0.399 the model is
        ! calibrated to. With the taper, resources after the transfer are
        ! strictly increasing in income and no atom forms. The two models use
        ! the same rule.
        real(8), parameter :: food_stamp_taper=0.30d0

        ! SNAP AS THE PROGRAMME ACTUALLY WORKS, FY2023, 48 states and DC.
        !
        ! What was here: one maximum allotment for every household, no size
        ! scaling and no eligibility limit, with max_food_cost calibrated to
        ! AGGREGATE spending over labour income. An aggregate can be hit with a
        ! tiny benefit paid to a great many households or a proper benefit paid
        ! to few, and it was the first: at max_food_cost = 0.00733 and a zero
        ! consumption tax the maximum allotment was 0.73 per cent of average
        ! labour income and the taper cut a household off above 2.4 per cent of
        ! it. SNAP paid a one person household 291 dollars a month, 3.96 per
        ! cent of mean household wage income, and ran eligibility to 130 per
        ! cent of the poverty line, near 20 per cent. So the floor was five to
        ! eighteen times too low and the cutoff eight times too tight, and
        ! essentially no homeless household received anything.
        !
        ! It matters beyond the transfer. The floor is what stops the poorest
        ! household's consumption reaching zero, and at sigma = 2 the CRRA goods
        ! term is unbounded below as it does. That term sets what a period of
        ! life is worth, aleph + u_goods, and the only cost of using drugs in
        ! this model is dying. A floor five times too low manufactures the
        ! households that select into use.
        !
        ! MAXIMUM ALLOTMENT BY HOUSEHOLD SIZE, as a multiple of the one person
        ! allotment: 291, 535, 766, 973, 1155, 1386, 1532, 1751 dollars a month.
        ! The schedule is concave, so a linear scaling would overpay large
        ! households by a third at size six.
        integer, parameter :: snap_max_size=8
        real(8), dimension(snap_max_size), parameter :: snap_allotment_scale=&
            (/1.000000d0,1.838488d0,2.632302d0,3.343643d0,3.969072d0,&
              4.762887d0,5.264605d0,6.017182d0/)

        ! The one person maximum allotment as a fraction of average labour
        ! income: 291 dollars a month against mean household wage and salary
        ! income of 88,212 in SCF 2022. EXTERNALLY ASSIGNED, not calibrated:
        ! the author's instruction is that the programme be implemented as it
        ! is rather than bent to reproduce its own total cost, so aggregate
        ! spending is now a check and not a target.
        real(8), parameter :: snap_max_allotment_one=0.039586d0

        ! Gross income eligibility, 130 per cent of the federal poverty line:
        ! 14,580 dollars for one person and 5,140 for each additional, times
        ! 1.30, over the same 88,212.
        real(8), parameter :: snap_gross_limit_one=0.214872d0
        real(8), parameter :: snap_gross_limit_add=0.075744d0

        ! The standard deduction, 193 dollars a month for one to three persons,
        ! and the twenty per cent earned income deduction. Net income is what
        ! the taper is charged on, and using gross instead overstates the
        ! withdrawal for every working household.
        real(8), parameter :: snap_standard_deduction=0.026256d0
        real(8), parameter :: snap_earned_income_deduction=0.20d0
                                                                        
        real(8), dimension(0:have_two_rental_markets) :: convexity_of_rental_company=&
            (1-perceived_probability_lower)*2.35+perceived_probability_lower*2.35
        real(8) :: distance_convexity_of_rental_company
        real(8) :: convexity_of_rental_company_adjuster=0
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_convexity_of_rental_company=10.d0**(-3)
                                                                   
        real(8), dimension(0:have_two_rental_markets) :: rental_management_costs=5.8601218979210445E-002
                 
        real(8) :: labor_income_tax_rate=(1-perceived_probability_lower)*0.75+perceived_probability_lower*0.69
        ! LIVE AGAIN, on the author's instruction of 13 September: *"tau_ell, smi
        ! onset, voucher probability, food stamps can be part of the live
        ! instrument"*.  It was retired when the author ran a no-tax variant; the
        ! vectors we calibrate carry adjust_taxes=1 and a tax rate near 0.75, so
        ! the moment tau_l is live and had no instrument.
        ! HELD 16 September. The author: *"i think we have to go back....and
        ! just calibrate the following: aleph, b, kappa, tau_u, SUD homeless,
        ! homeless, SUD"*. Seven moments, seven instruments, everything else
        ! frozen where it stands.
        ! RESTORED 16 September to complete the author's calibration plan:
        ! "You should try to get the rest of the parameters in the calibration
        ! plan, except omegac". Held at zero for the seven-instrument
        ! restriction of 15 September; this is the value it carried before that.
        ! PINNED 17 September with the set of six; see discount_factor_adjuster.
        real(8) :: labor_income_tax_rate_adjuster=0.d0
        real(8), parameter :: labor_income_tax_rate_adjuster_start=5*10.d0**(-1)
        real(8) :: previous_gap_labor_income_tax_rate=0
        integer :: have_gap_labor_income_tax_rate=0
        real(8), parameter :: damp_adjuster_floor=10.d0**(-4)
        ! HOW MANY SIGN FLIPS IN A ROW BEFORE THE ADJUSTER IS DAMPED.
        !
        ! The author, 14 September: *"Use the flip sign dampening only if it
        ! happens more than 3 times in a row."*  Damping on the FIRST flip turns
        ! the rule into a bisection, which is what they have ruled out; a single
        ! flip is also the normal way a bracket crosses its target and is no
        ! evidence of ringing. Four in a row is.
        !
        ! Tested with > rather than >=, which is what "more than 3" says: the
        ! fourth consecutive flip is the first one that damps.
        integer, parameter :: damp_flip_threshold=3
        ! ONE COUNTER PER INSTRUMENT, because "in a row" is per instrument and a
        ! shared counter would let one parameter's ringing damp another's.
        !
        ! NOT PERSISTED IN calibration_state.dat, deliberately. That file is
        ! positional, so adding nine entries would make every existing state file
        ! unreadable. A resumed run therefore starts each counter at zero, which
        ! costs at most three extra flips before damping begins -- a far smaller
        ! price than an archive of unreadable checkpoints.
        integer :: flips_relative_share_of_housing=0
        integer :: flips_relative_share_of_leisure=0
        integer :: flips_housing_status_grid_expander=0
        integer :: flips_labor_income_tax_rate=0
        integer :: flips_rho_intergenerational=0
        integer :: flips_penalty_child_homeless=0
        integer :: flips_voucher_probability=0
        integer :: flips_separation_rate=0
        integer :: flips_employment_multiplier=0

        ! THE MOST ANY PARAMETER MAY MOVE IN ONE LOOP, on the author's
        ! instruction, and ONE constant so it is changed in one place.
        !
        ! Measured over a single loop, with the report finally pairing each
        ! parameter with the moment it produced, three instruments moved a
        ! hundred per cent and landed on their floor and a fourth moved
        ! seventy five, while every other one moved under eight. A parameter
        ! that lands on its bound every loop is not being calibrated, and the
        ! moments measured beside it are measured at the bound.
        !
        ! WHAT THIS COSTS, because the number is a judgement and the arithmetic
        ! should be visible when it is changed. A cap of one per cent closes a
        ! gap of forty per cent in about thirty five loops and one of eighty in
        ! about sixty; at the MEASURED 1.5 minutes a loop that is one to two
        ! hours, not the twelve to twenty this comment used to claim on an
        ! unmeasured twenty-minute loop. A cap of five per cent closes the same gaps in seven and twelve
        ! loops, and on the measured data it would still bind on nothing except
        ! the four that were slamming, since the healthy instruments move
        ! between nought and seven per cent unaided.
        !
        ! b IS DELIBERATELY NOT CAPPED BY THIS. Its bracket spans six decades --
        ! 10^-7 gives participation of 0.748 and 0.0827 gives zero -- and at one
        ! per cent a loop it could not cross a single decade in five hundred
        ! loops. It is bounded to a factor of ten a loop and, while the sweep is
        ! running, is not moved by the loop at all.
        ! SIX PER CENT A LOOP WHILE THE THREE-BLOCK IS ON, ONE PER CENT OTHERWISE.
        !
        ! trust_factor is 1+max_move_per_loop, and move_factor hands it to a
        ! bracket whenever the model is inside a corner band of 0.1 to 5 times
        ! target -- which is nearly always. It is applied BEFORE cap_step, so it
        ! is the binding limit, not the cap.
        !
        ! Measured 11 September, loops 1 to 3 of the three-block run:
        !
        !     value_of_life        +5.0 per cent a loop   (the global cap)
        !     addiction_scalar_1   +-1.0 per cent a loop  (this, via trust_factor)
        !
        ! and the two have COMPARABLE elasticities on participation, -9.5 against
        ! an implied -8. So the value of life won five to one every loop and
        ! participation went +14.5, -33.7, -57.1 per cent while aleph gained only
        ! four points a loop. b-bar was correctly signed the whole way and simply
        ! could not move.
        !
        ! Six per cent is the MATCHED value: offsetting a 5 per cent step of
        ! value_of_life at elasticity -9.5 needs b-bar to move 5*9.5/8 = 5.9 per
        ! cent at elasticity -8. The author's plan -- push aleph up hard and bring
        ! b down to match -- needs the two steps sized against each other, not
        ! set independently.
        real(8), parameter :: max_move_per_loop=merge(2.d0*10.d0**(-2),1.d0*10.d0**(-2),freeze_to_three_block==1)
        real(8), parameter :: trust_factor=1.d0+max_move_per_loop

        ! ESCAPE AND REFINE ARE DIFFERENT PROBLEMS AND NEED DIFFERENT STEPS.
        !
        ! One per cent a loop is the right step when the model is near its
        ! target and wrong when it is in a corner, because a corner is not a
        ! small distance to walk: a participation rate of 0.905 against 0.089,
        ! or homelessness of exactly zero, is the absence of the margin the
        ! parameter acts on, and refining toward it a hundredth at a time
        ! spends a day discovering that. The two regimes are told apart by the
        ! MOMENT, not by the parameter: a moment below a tenth of its target or
        ! above five times it is a corner, and the parameter that owns it is
        ! allowed to move fast until the moment is back in range. Everything
        ! else keeps the one per cent.
        real(8), parameter :: corner_low=0.1d0
        real(8), parameter :: corner_high=5.d0
        real(8), parameter :: escape_factor=2.d0
        real(8), parameter :: escape_factor_b=10.d0

        ! THE GLOBAL GOVERNOR ON EVERY PARAMETER STEP, 11 September.
        !
        ! The author, after watching b go from 0.00255 to 0.2537 in two loops
        ! and take participation to exactly zero: "b is moving too fast", then
        ! "make sure no parameter moves more than 0.5% between runs".
        !
        ! Each bracket already has its own trust region, and b's is the reason
        ! this is needed rather than a reason it is not: escape_factor_b is 10,
        ! so the moment participation falls outside a tenth of its target the
        ! clamp permits a TENFOLD move per loop, and the corner b had just
        ! created is exactly the state that triggers it. A per-bracket fix would
        ! have to be made once per bracket and would miss the next one.
        !
        ! WHAT IT COSTS, SAID ONCE AND THEN MEASURED. At half a per cent a loop
        ! a parameter needs 139 loops to double and 462 to move by a factor of
        ! ten. A loop is 1.5 MINUTES -- measured 11 September, fourteen loops in
        ! twenty-one minutes, after a cold first solve of 3 to 4 minutes -- so
        ! that is 3.5 HOURS to double and 11.5 to move an order of magnitude.
        !
        ! An earlier version of this comment said 'two days at six minutes a
        ! loop'. That was the cold solve generalised to every loop and never
        ! checked. The governor is a night's work, not a week's.
        !
        ! A PARAMETER SITTING AT EXACTLY ZERO IS EXEMPT FOR ONE STEP, because a
        ! RELATIVE cap around zero is zero and would trap it there for ever.
        ! weight_on_child starts at 0 and must climb; so does
        ! penalty_child_homeless. After the first step off zero the cap binds
        ! normally.
        !
        ! Handable, so it can be loosened mid calibration without a rebuild.
! FIVE PER CENT A LOOP WHILE ONLY THREE BRACKETS ARE LIVE, HALF OF ONE OTHERWISE.
        !
        ! The author set half a per cent on 11 September when twenty parameters
        ! moved together and a trace could not be read. With three live and an
        ! instruction to move the value of life *"up a lot"* -- aleph wants it
        ! somewhere near 280 against 160 today -- half a per cent is 110 loops
        ! for that one parameter, which is not a search, it is a wait.
        !
        ! It is also the cap that made the search step OVER its own answer:
        ! measured on 11 September, one capped step of value_of_life was worth
        ! twelve per cent on participation, so the cap was simultaneously too
        ! slow for the distance to travel and too coarse to stop on.
        ! HALF A PER CENT FOR EVERYTHING THAT HAS NO RATE OF ITS OWN, on the
        ! author's instruction of 14 September: *"you adjust kappa_phid, aleph,
        ! and omega_c very slowly, around 0.1%. the rest can move at 0.5% per
        ! iteration"*.  It was one per cent under the stage-1 freeze.
        ! 0.1 PER CENT, 14 September, on the author's *"No parameter should
        ! move fast. Especially big ones like aleph, omegac, kappa_phid, h1,
        ! kappa_ued. They should move at 0.1% maybe between iterations."*
        ! Was 5e-3.  Every parameter that goes through cap_step now moves at
        ! the same rate aleph and theta already had.
        real(8) :: max_rel_step_all=1.d0*10.d0**(-3)

        ! PER-INSTRUMENT STEP CAPS, 15 September: "you need to then fix the
        ! updates on c bar and band1", "pieu should be faster", "and mu_H too".
        !
        ! These four ran on the GLOBAL cap of 0.1 per cent a loop, which is not
        ! slow so much as unreachable. Measured against their gaps at the
        ! kappa 2.4 point, the loops each needed to close at 0.1 per cent:
        !
        !   aleph   -10.7%%     113 loops    ~3.8 hours
        !   pi_eu   -22.1%%     250 loops    ~8.3 hours
        !   mu_H    -26.9%%     313 loops   ~10.4 hours
        !   cbar    -49.4%%     681 loops   ~22.7 hours
        !   band1   -66.5%%    1094 loops   ~36.5 hours
        !
        ! At 1 per cent they are 11, 25, 31, 68 and 109 loops. The global cap is
        ! left alone so the instruments already AT target -- tau_l, pi_V, p_m,
        ! beta -- keep their small steps and are not shaken loose.
        ! RAISED 18 September, on the author's *"We need h2, tauu, c bar, find
        ! if addicted, find if smi, to adjust quite a bit to get the model much
        ! closer to the data"*. c_bar is 101 per cent off; at one per cent a step that is
        ! 70 updates, at five it is 14.
        ! A speed limit set when the parameter was near its answer, left in place
        ! after the economy moved under it -- the same fault already fixed for
        ! multiple_find and kappa_phi_d earlier tonight. Speed limit on the SEARCH,
        ! not a model parameter, so it invalidates no solved point.
        real(8) :: max_food_cost_max_rel_step=0.05d0
        real(8) :: newborn_endowment_max_rel_step=0.01d0
        real(8) :: pi_eu_max_rel_step=0.01d0
        real(8) :: mu_homeless_max_rel_step=0.01d0

        ! pi_V ON ITS OWN CAP TOO, 15 September: "you need to fix pi_v". The
        ! voucher lottery ran on the global 0.1 per cent and the moment needs to
        ! nearly TRIPLE -- 0.093 against 0.266 -- which is about eleven hundred
        ! loops. Its instrument moved 0.044375 to 0.044508 across three loops,
        ! which is the cap and not the economics.
        ! RAISED 0.02 -> 0.05 with the adjuster's release, because the two
        ! have to be matched. The gain asks for 0.1 times a gap of 0.0387 on a
        ! parameter of 0.0797, which is 4.9 per cent, and a two per cent cap
        ! would swallow more than half of every step -- the same
        ! gain-against-cap trap that held h2, c_bar, tau_u and kappa_find_smi.
