        ! in every report for a day; the gap was the denominator, not the rent.
        !
        ! AND IT IS A NARROW WINDOW. h_2 is violently powerful: 0.18 and 0.30
        ! both tipped the economy into the 95-per-cent-participation corner, and
        ! this 3.6 per cent step from 0.137 moved the spending-based burden
        ! nearly six-fold and emp_homeless from 0.087 to 0.300. Do not step it
        ! without checking participation afterwards.
        real(8) :: smallest_rental_relative_to_lowest_income=0.142d0
        ! ON, and the sign is the same story as the expander. A larger smallest
        ! unit is a more expensive smallest unit, and the households that took it
        ! have nothing cheaper to move to except the street, so raising it pushes
        ! them out of the housed distribution rather than down it and the share
        ! in the smallest size FALLS. The update therefore adds the gap.
        !
        ! Deliberately smaller than the homelessness adjuster. Both this and
        ! size_homelessness move the bottom of the housing distribution, so they
        ! compete: this one sets where the affordability floor is and that one
        ! sets how bad the alternative to it is. Running them at the same speed
        ! lets them chase each other.
        ! h_2 IS CONCEDED ENTIRELY, 12 September, on the author's "Lets just
        ! concede h2 entirety until we actually can get h1 to 1% and emp
        ! homeless to around 46%".
        !
        ! WHY.  This bracket chases the severe cost burden target by moving the
        ! cheapest rental rung, and over the 209-loop run 7303e4b6ca it walked
        ! the rung from 0.10189 to 0.21030 -- it doubled.  The rung is what
        ! sets the price of the only housing a poor household can reach, and
        ! the measured mapping from that run is brutal:
        !
        !     rung 0.10189  ->  h_1  0.63 per cent
        !     rung 0.10384  ->  h_1  1.16
        !     rung 0.11165  ->  h_1  4.28
        !     rung 0.13661  ->  h_1 10.89
        !     rung 0.21132  ->  h_1 32.49
        !
        ! A four per cent rise in the rung takes homelessness from 0.6 to about
        ! three per cent, and four per cent is four loops at the step cap.  So
        ! h_2 and h_1 cannot both be chased on the same rung, and the author has
        ! chosen h_1.
        !
        ! The do-while term for distance_smallest_house_size is gated on this
        ! adjuster below, so zeroing it here concedes the moment completely
        ! rather than leaving an unclosable distance holding the loop open.
        ! UN-PINNED 15 September: "lets target h2 to get 10.1% of households
        ! spending over 50%". The rung is the only instrument on burden50, which
        ! sat at -95.9 per cent with nothing pushing it.
        ! PINNED, on the author's instruction of 16 September: *"cant we set h2
        ! to some number that likely gives a decent cost burdened households and
        ! pin it?"*.
        !
        ! THE NUMBER IS 0.137, from the one controlled sweep of this parameter
        ! that exists. Loops 24 to 57 of j06 held participation flat at 0.0064,
        ! so they vary h_2 and nothing else:
        !
        !   h_2 param  h_2 moment  burden50  h_3     h_1
        !   0.12694    0.0144      0.045     0.298   0.0059
        !   0.13079    0.0305      0.069     0.253   0.0069
        !   0.13475    0.0469      0.101     0.202   0.0079
        !   0.13746    0.0618      0.120     0.144   0.0096
        !
