        ! ZERO, AND THIS IS WHAT "PINNED" ACTUALLY REQUIRES. The author, 16
        ! September: "i dont think h2 should be adjusted", then "The 30% of
        ! spending is great. Pin it".
        !
        ! I PINNED THE VALUE AND LEFT THE ADJUSTER LIVE, so h_2 walked away from
        ! 0.142 the moment each run started. n13 took it 0.142 -> 0.16486 in
        ! fifteen blocks, a 16 per cent drift, while every report called it
        ! pinned. Measured earlier the same day: h_2 = 0.18 tips the economy into
        ! the 95-per-cent-participation corner, so a run left to drift arrives
        ! there on its own.
        !
        ! The author found it by refusing to accept the explanation: "all you did
        ! was move h2 a bit higher but nothing to collapse the model". Nobody had
        ! moved it. It moved itself.
        !
        ! Stopping the handover for an adjuster is NOT the same as zeroing it,
        ! because the source value is what the run starts from.
        ! LIVE AGAIN FROM 17 September, at its start value. The author:
        ! *"And make h2 a live instrument for it so it doesn't explode"*, on a
        ! run where the burden moment had reached three and a half times its
        ! target while h_2 itself sat pinned and could do nothing about it.
        !
        ! This reverses the pin of 16 September -- *"i dont think h2 should be
        ! adjusted"*, *"just set h2=0.142 and pin it"* -- which was right while
        ! h_2 was bracketed on a moment it could not reach. It is now bracketed
        ! on the renters' 30 per cent cut, which the file's own sweep shows it
        ! moves monotonically, so the instrument has something to do.
        !
        ! The zero-adjuster floor guard stays exactly as it is: it is what makes
        ! a future pin hold, and it costs nothing while the adjuster is live.
        real(8) :: smallest_house_size_adjuster=0.05d0

        ! DAMPING THE TWO HOUSING ADJUSTERS. Both were fixed steps and both
        ! oscillated: the size distribution responds sharply to the size
        ! parameters, so a step that closes the gap from one side overshoots it
        ! from the other and the loop rings instead of settling.
        !
        ! The rule is the standard one for a damped fixed point and it needs no
        ! tuning. Remember the sign of the gap. If it flips, the step jumped the
        ! root, so halve it; if it does not, the step is still walking towards
        ! the root, so let it grow back slowly. The floor stops it damping itself
        ! to a standstill on noise from the distribution iteration, the ceiling
        ! is the original step, so an undamped run is the worst this can do
        ! rather than the best.
        real(8), parameter :: house_size_adjuster_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: house_size_adjuster_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: house_size_adjuster_floor=10.d0**(-3)
        real(8) :: smallest_house_size_adjuster_start=0.05
        real(8) :: largest_house_size_adjuster_start=0.05
