        ! ASSET GRID, and the one number here that is a judgement rather than a
        ! deduction. The a' search is QUADRATIC in this, so ninety to fifty takes the
        ! renter loop from 91 points to 51 and is worth about 3.2x, more than
        ! anything else left on the list.
        !
        ! The reason for caution is that the objective is not single peaked in
        ! a', and the things that break it, voucher eligibility as a step in
        ! income and the food stamp floor, are where the welfare comparison
        ! happens at margins of tenths of a per cent. What makes seventy
        ! defensible is that the spacing is dense near zero and sparse at the
        ! top, so the points removed are the ones above the wealth distribution's
        ! upper decile, where few households sit and where the only notch,
        ! capital income pushing a household over the eligibility threshold, is
        ! already coarsely resolved.
        !
        ! IT IS ONE LINE TO PUT BACK, and it must be validated before any
        ! reported number rests on it: solve the benchmark at seventy and at
        ! ninety and compare the targeted moments and the two policy
        ! counterfactuals, not just the value function.
        ! FORTY, NOT FIFTY, and this is the one cut in the whole exercise that is
        ! a judgement rather than a deduction. The a' search is quadratic in the
        ! grid, so forty against fifty is 1.56x, which is what takes the
        ! calibration under a day once mental illness doubles the state space:
        ! thirty hours becomes nineteen on four cores, about two on forty.
        !
        ! IT MUST BE VALIDATED, and the validation is cheap now that the owner
        ! branch is gone: solve the benchmark at forty and at fifty and compare
        ! the targeted moments and BOTH counterfactuals, not just the value
        ! function. The reason to be careful is specific rather than general. The
        ! objective is not single peaked in a': voucher eligibility is a step
        ! function of income which includes r*a, the food stamp floor kinks, and
        ! housing is discrete. Coarsening this grid coarsens exactly the dimension
        ! that carries the notches.
        ! TWENTY FIVE WAS TRIED AND THE EVIDENCE FOR IT WAS VOID. The argument
        ! was that the coarse grid is only a SEARCH grid, that nothing is read
        ! off it because internal_computations refines a' on a sub grid spanning
        ! the two neighbouring coarse gaps, and that widening the coarse gaps by
        ! 40/25 while raising the sub grid by the same factor therefore holds
        ! precision at 2.56x the speed. Half of that is right: the a' CHOICE is
        ! refined off grid. The other half is not. The a' STATE is on grid, and
        ! every place that asks what a household has -- its financial income,
        ! its wealth, its bequest, whether it clears a voucher's income test --
        ! reads financial_grid(financial_index). The distribution is carried on
        ! the same points. So the coarse grid is the state space, and cutting it
        ! coarsens the state space.
        !
        ! THE MEASUREMENT THAT SEEMED TO SETTLE IT MEASURED SOMETHING ELSE. The
        ! 25 point build's first moment block was held against the 40 point
        ! build's and read as a 10.7 per cent error in average net wealth and a
        ! 619 per cent rise in distribution sweeps. Both builds had loaded a warm
        ! start distribution, and the 25 point build had loaded the 40 point
        ! build's, because read_calibration_distribution did not check the shape:
        ! it filled an 86 element layout from a 101 element file and got a
        ! distribution summing to 45.7 instead of 51.4. The sweeps and the wealth
        ! gap are that, not the grid. The stamp added to
        ! write_calibration_distribution is what stops it happening again.
        !
        ! SO THE GRID IS BACK AT FORTY WHILE THE REAL COMPARISON IS RUN: three
        ! cold solves at 25, 40 and 50 with the warm start deleted on every side
        ! and fin_num_of_internal set so that n x fin is 2500 at each, which
        ! holds both the refinement's cost and its sub grid spacing fixed and
        ! leaves the state grid as the only thing that moves. Forty is itself an
        ! unvalidated cut from the fifty this model was built with, so it is in
        ! that comparison too rather than assumed.
        ! TWENTY FOUR POSITIVE POINTS, SO TWENTY FIVE REACHABLE ONES.
        !
        ! The author's instruction, to buy solve time ahead of a possible split
        ! into patient and impatient types. Index negative_financial_grid_size
        ! holds zero assets and everything below it is a MORTGAGE state, which
        ! no household can occupy: max_amortization_years is 1, so has_house is
        ! always 0 and the renter loop starts at that index. The reachable grid
        ! is therefore zero plus the positive points -- 41 before, 25 now.
        !
        ! The RANGE does not change. The top point is set from
        ! max_times_labour_income_saved*employment_grid(1,employment_grid_size)
        ! and the curvature fills in between, so this is a coarser grid over the
        ! same interval rather than a shorter one. Mass on the top point was
        ! 8.2e-5 and on the top three 2.4e-4, so the endpoint was never binding.
        !
        ! Value function cost is roughly quadratic in this dimension, since every
        ! state searches over future assets, so 41 to 25 is about 2.7 times.
        integer, parameter :: positive_financial_grid_size=24
        ! FOUR POINTS, AND THEY ARE THE BORROWING RANGE. The author, 2 September:
        ! "You can have just 3 or 4 grid points on the negative side that align
        ! with the max being 0.3 times average income, and fix that across
        ! simulations."
        !
        ! It was 61 points spanning -(1-minimum_downpayment) to zero, a MORTGAGE
        ! range, and unsecured borrowing was snapping its floor to whichever of
        ! those happened to lie nearest -0.3 times average income. Four points
        ! -- three negative and zero -- laid out on the borrowing range itself
        ! is exact instead of snapped, and it takes the whole financial grid
        ! from 85 points to 28, which is most of the state space.
        !
        ! IF include_homeownership IS EVER TURNED BACK ON this must be revisited:
        ! a grid bottom near -0.19 cannot carry a mortgage downpayment. It is
        ! zero today and everyone rents.
        ! SEVEN POINTS BELOW ZERO, not three, on the author's instruction of
        ! 2 September. This is the INDEX OF ZERO, so 8 means indices 1-7 are
        ! strictly negative and index 8 is zero; financial_grid_size is
        ! positive_financial_grid_size + this, so the 24 positive points are
        ! untouched and the grid simply grows.
        !
        ! Three points could not represent a borrowing limit of any width. At a
        ! limit of 0.30 of average income they sat 0.1 apart; at 3.00 they sat a
        ! full year of income apart, so a household wanting a tenth of a year in
        ! debt had nowhere to stand and a wide limit could look inert purely
        ! through coarseness.
        ! 8 -> 3, on the author's instruction of 5 September: *"and put only 2
        ! grid points there"*, corrected to *"ah, then put 3 points"* once it was
        ! pointed out that two points give {-limit, 0} and nothing in between.
        !
        ! THREE POINTS GIVE {-limit, -limit/2, 0}, so a household can hold half the
        ! limit as well as none or all of it. That is the minimum that lets debt be
        ! a choice of DEGREE rather than a switch, which matters because the whole
        ! bottom half piled onto the floor at eight points and a limit of 0.30 --
        ! the wealth shares came out in exact 1:5:10:25:50 proportion, the
        ! signature of every household sitting at the same value.
        integer, parameter :: negative_financial_grid_size=3
        
        integer, parameter :: grid_gaps=coarse_grid*10+(1-coarse_grid)
           
        integer, parameter :: financial_grid_size=positive_financial_grid_size+negative_financial_grid_size
                                          
        real(8), dimension(financial_grid_size) :: financial_grid
        real(8), dimension(negative_financial_grid_size) :: financial_grid_distance_negative
        real(8), dimension(financial_grid_size) :: financial_grid_distance_positive
                
        ! HOMEOWNERSHIP IS OUT OF THE MODEL. The revision is a renter model: the
        ! written model has four housing statuses, homelessness and three rental
        ! sizes, no mortgages, and assets that cannot go negative. Rather than
        ! delete two and a half thousand lines of owner and mortgage code, which
        ! would be irreversible and hard to check, ownership is switched off at
        ! the three places that let a household reach it.
        !
        ! THIS IS THE FIRST. has_house is set from years_left_on_mortgage_index at
        ! line 9188, so a loop that runs to one leaves has_house at zero and no
        ! owner state is ever solved. It was two.
        integer, parameter :: max_amortization_years=1
                            
        real(8), parameter :: max_times_labour_income_saved=3.2
                                              
        real(8) :: increase_in_labour_income_tax=is_counterfactual*0

        ! THE TAX SEARCH. Three quantities it needs and did not have.
        !
        ! WHY THE OLD RULE COULD NOT WORK. It was
        !     increase_in_labour_income_tax += 0.005*(revenue - target)
        ! once a loop. Revenue is roughly sum(y - lambda*y**0.85), so
        ! d(revenue)/d(lambda) is about -sum(y**0.85), which is near -0.69 here:
        ! the Newton step is about 1.46 times the gap and the rule took 0.005 of
        ! it, three hundred times too small. No counterfactual could close the
        ! budget inside the eighty loop cap, so every one of them exited on the
        ! loose 2.5e-2 tolerance with the tax still walking.
        !
        ! WORSE, IT WALKED IN THE WRONG DIRECTION FIRST. The counterfactual's
        ! distribution starts far from stationary -- loop one revenue runs 1.4 to
        ! 2.4 against a target of 0.545 -- so the first fifteen or so loops
        ! integrate a cold start that has nothing to do with any policy, and an
        ! integrator has no way to give that back. The signature was plain once
        ! it was looked for: across four runs the final tax tracked the loop-one
        ! revenue and not the policy, and the null with NO policy change at all
        ! walked further than either Treatment First arm.
        ! last_revenue_seen_tax moves EVERY loop and measures drift; the secant
        ! pair moves only when a step is actually taken, so the slope is always
        ! between two points the tax itself separated.
        real(8) :: last_revenue_seen_tax=0
        integer :: have_last_revenue_tax=0
        real(8) :: secant_revenue_tax=0
        real(8) :: secant_tax_level=0
        integer :: have_tax_secant=0
        ! CONVERGE THE DRUG MARKET, THEN MOVE THE TAX. A NESTED FIXED POINT, NOT
        ! A TUNED TOLERANCE.
        !
        ! Two tolerance-shaped attempts at this both failed, and it is worth
        ! recording why rather than trying a third threshold.
        !
        !   FIRST: hold while the revenue drift exceeds a quarter of the GAP.
        !   Backwards -- the gap is huge early, so a drift of 0.078 looked small
        !   beside 1.955 and the hold released on loop two, permissive exactly
        !   when the cold start is worst.
        !
        !   SECOND: hold while the drift exceeds the budget tolerance. Better,
        !   but revenue PLATEAUS TRANSIENTLY. On universal Treatment First it
        !   ran 3.2031, 3.2477, 3.2492 -- a drift of 0.0015 on loop three, well
        !   inside the tolerance -- while distance_drug_market was still swinging
        !   0.108, 0.076, 0.096, twenty times its own stopping rule. The economy
        !   was nowhere near settled and the test believed it was. For scale, the
        !   pre-fix null took eighteen loops to fall from 2.44 to 0.76, so a
        !   two-loop plateau at loop three says nothing at all.
        !
        ! The structural statement is the one to make. Rent and the housing price
        ! are PEGGED in a counterfactual, so their distances are identically
        ! zero and the drug market is the only other live market. "The rest of
        ! the equilibrium has converged at the current tax" therefore reduces to
        ! one testable condition, and the tax becomes the outer loop of a nested
        ! fixed point rather than an instrument fighting a moving economy.
        !
        ! The drift test is kept as a second condition, because the drug market
        ! can clear while the distribution is still settling. Both must hold.
        real(8), parameter :: tax_hold_drift_tolerance_mult=1.0d0
        ! How close the drug market must be to its own stopping rule before the
        ! tax may move. Two, not one, so the tax is not held hostage to the last
        ! digit of a market that the tax itself perturbs.
        real(8), parameter :: drug_settled_mult_for_tax=2.0d0
        ! ... but never hold past this, so a run that drifts forever still moves.
        ! The escape, so a run whose drug market never quite settles still moves
        ! its tax rather than capping with the budget untouched. Raised from 25:
        ! the drug market takes into the twenties to come inside twice its rule.
        integer, parameter :: tax_hold_max_loops=35
        ! Damping on the secant step, and a cap so one bad slope cannot throw the
        ! tax across the plausible range in a single loop.
        real(8), parameter :: tax_step_damping=0.5d0
        ! THE CAP AND THE FALLBACK WERE BOTH SET IN THE WRONG UNITS, and it cost
        ! the null test ten loops.
        !
        ! d(revenue)/d(lambda) is about -0.69 PER HOUSEHOLD. The living
        ! population mass here is about fifty-one, so in the AGGREGATE the
        ! quantity the budget is actually measured in, it is about -23. Measured
        ! rather than assumed this time: the null stepped lambda by +0.02 and
        ! total revenue fell 5.83878 to 5.37369, which is -23.25.
        !
        ! With a fallback of 0.3*gap and a cap of 0.02, the very first step --
        ! taken before any secant exists -- moved revenue by 0.465 against a
        ! budget tolerance of 0.025, an eighteen-fold overshoot, in a direction
        ! chosen by a gap that was itself small. It then took ten loops to walk
        ! back, and every walk-back kicked the drug market out of tolerance for
        ! four more, which is why the null's moments oscillated instead of
        ! settling.
        !
        ! 0.0005 in lambda moves revenue about 0.012, half the tolerance: small
        ! enough that a blind first step cannot overshoot, and the secant takes
        ! over from the second step onward with a real slope.
        ! TWO CAPS, BECAUSE THE TWO KINDS OF STEP FAIL DIFFERENTLY.
        !
        ! A BLIND step -- no secant yet, so the size comes from a guessed gain --
        ! can be arbitrarily wrong and must be capped hard. 0.0005 moves revenue
        ! about 0.015, well inside the 0.025 tolerance, so a blind step cannot
        ! overshoot even when the gain is wrong.
        !
        ! A SECANT step is self-limiting: it is -0.5*gap/slope on a slope the tax
        ! itself measured, so it lands at half the gap by construction and cannot
        ! run away. Capping it at 0.0005 as well was an over-correction, and it
        ! cost the universal Treatment First arm its convergence: at loop 32 the
        ! budget gap sat at 0.63 with a measured slope of -29.7, so the Newton
        ! step was 0.021 and the cap allowed 0.0005 -- forty times too small. The
        ! gap went 0.644, 0.617, 0.614, 0.632 across four loops: flat, because the
        ! steps were smaller than the economy's own movement, and the run would
        ! have hit the eighty loop cap with the budget untouched.
        real(8), parameter :: max_tax_move_blind=0.0005d0
        real(8), parameter :: max_tax_move_secant=0.03d0
        ! A half Newton step on the AGGREGATE slope: 0.5/23.25 = 0.0215.
        real(8), parameter :: tax_fallback_gain=0.0215d0
        real(8) :: tax_gap_1,tax_slope_1,tax_step_1,tax_drift_1,max_tax_move_1
        
        real(8), dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: housing_price
        
        real(8), dimension(transition_length) :: distance_housing_market=1,distance_rental_market=1
        
        real(8) :: rental_market_adjuster
        
        real(8), parameter :: investment_income_tax=0.15             
        
        real(8), dimension(1-solve_transition_dynamics:transition_length) :: property_tax=0
        real(8) :: property_tax_on_rental_housing=is_counterfactual*check_tax_incidence*0
        
        integer :: pay_mortgage_insurance
        real(8) :: mortgage_insurance_premium=0
                
        real(8), parameter :: housing_depreciation_rate=0.006
        real(8), parameter :: cost_of_selling=0.06
        real(8), parameter :: cost_of_buying=0
        
        real(8), parameter :: threshold_income_vouchers=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: rent_to_income_ratio_vouchers=0.3
                                                               
        real(8), parameter :: life_cycle_component_a=real(0.38)/((real(life_span)-real(retirement_span)-11))
        real(8), parameter :: life_cycle_component_b=(real(0.38)-real(0.38))/(10)
        
        ! Three SUD blocks of three employment states each, crossed with SMI.
        ! Without the disorder there is no ever-had-it state to carry and the
        ! grid is the three employment states alone.
        integer, parameter :: unemployment_grid_size=&
            include_addiction*9*number_of_smi_states+(1-include_addiction)*3
                                               
        real(8), dimension(unemployment_grid_size) :: unemployment_grid
        real(8), dimension(life_span,employment_grid_size) :: employment_grid
        ! THE FOUR CLASSES' PRODUCTIVITY FACTORS, index 0 to 3: neither parent
        ! condition, a parent with a substance use disorder (SUD), a parent who
        ! was homeless, and both. Filled in recompute_derived_parameters from
        ! penalty_child_addicted and penalty_child_homeless, so the two penalties
        ! stay the only place either number lives. At child_penalty_classes = 1
        ! only element 0 is ever read and it is one.
        real(8), dimension(0:3) :: child_penalty_factor=1
        integer, dimension(employment_grid_size) :: initial_endowment
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size) :: relative_endowments
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size) :: fraction_with_positive_wealth
        real(8) :: value_of_initial_assets,value_of_bequests
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: value_of_bequests_by_age,bequests_mass_by_age
        
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size) :: unconditional_employment_probability

        ! newborn productivity distribution given the parent's disorder status
        ! (0 or 1) and whether the parent was homeless (1 housed, 2 homeless)
        ! INTERGENERATIONAL PERSISTENCE OF PRODUCTIVITY, rho.
        !
        ! It was ZERO. newborn_employment_probability was indexed by the
        ! parent's disorder and housing status and by nothing else, so a child
        ! of a household at the top node and a child of one at the bottom drew
        ! the SAME distribution. Poverty was redrawn every generation and the
        ! model had no persistent disadvantage at all.
        !
        ! That is an identification problem and not only a realism one.
        ! penalty_child_addicted is calibrated on the child disorder ratio,
        ! which the data puts near two, and with no baseline persistence delta_d
        ! has to explain the whole of it. In reality much of why the children of
        ! addicted parents do badly is that those parents are poor and poor
        ! parents have poor children for ordinary reasons. Attributing all of it
        ! to the disorder biases the paper's central channel upward by
        ! construction.
        !
        ! The intergenerational earnings elasticity, assigned rather than
        ! calibrated. At rho = 0 this reduces exactly to the old behaviour, which
        ! is what makes it testable against it.
        !
        ! SETTABLE from 10 September. It was a parameter at 0.35, which is the
        ! middle of the US estimates, but it is the whole of the model's
        ! parent-to-child transmission for a HOUSING penalty and therefore the
        ! thing that decides the homelessness odds ratio. Fixing it at 0.35 put
        ! that ratio at 2.82 with penalty_child_homeless at zero, above the 2.10
        ! in the data with no penalty left to remove. It is a variable so the
        ! ratio can be read at a rho the literature also supports.
        !
        ! 0.30 IS THE AUTHOR'S ASSIGNED VALUE and it is the default, so the
        ! instrument below is OFF unless a vector asks for it.
        real(8) :: rho_intergenerational=0.50d0
        !
        ! IT IS 0.50, THE UPPER END OF THE LITERATURE, ON THE AUTHOR'S
        ! INSTRUCTION OF 13 SEPTEMBER: *"i think we should set rho=0.5 so that it
        ! is at an upper bound and delta_d can be lower"*, and *"same with the
        ! quant model"*.  It was 0.30.  The stylized model moved with it.
        !
        ! WHY THE UPPER END.  penalty_child_addicted is aimed at the child's SUD
        ! odds ratio.  Income persistence and the penalty are two routes to the
        ! same place, so the more of the odds ratio rho carries, the less the
        ! penalty has to, and choosing rho at the top of the defensible range
        ! makes the penalty the SMALLEST the model can get away with.  That is
        ! the conservative direction for every result that runs through it.
        !
        ! AND IT CUTS THE OTHER WAY ON THE HOMELESSNESS ODDS RATIO, WHICH IS THE
        ! SAME PARAMETER DOING DOUBLE DUTY.  The note below records that with the
        ! homelessness child penalty off entirely the model produces 2.802
        ! against a target of 2.10 -- ALREADY ABOVE IT at rho = 0.30.  Raising
        ! rho raises persistence and pushes that further above target, so the
        ! homelessness odds ratio gets worse in exchange for a smaller SUD
        ! penalty.  It is a trade, it was made deliberately, and it has NOT been
        ! remeasured at 0.50: the number above is at 0.30.
        !
        ! AND IT CAN ALSO BE CALIBRATED, WHICH IS A SEPARATE INSTRUCTION FROM A
        ! SEPARATE MOMENT IN THE SAME DAY. The author, 10 September: *"my
        ! suggestion is to use the one class calibration with delta_d=0.36, and
        ! rho_intergenerational to target the odds ratio of the homeless, you
        ! probably need about 0.1-0.15? and calibrate omega_c at the same time."*
        ! They later assigned 0.30 instead. BOTH readings are kept because the
        ! machinery costs nothing when it is off and rebuilding it would cost a
        ! round: the adjuster defaults to ZERO, which is held, so the assigned
        ! 0.30 above is what every run gets unless the adjuster is handed in.
        !
        ! WHY IT IS THE INSTRUMENT delta_h COULD NEVER BE. The target is 2.10 and
        ! the model produces 2.802 with the homelessness child penalty switched
        ! off ENTIRELY, measured. To reach 2.10 delta_h would have to be -0.2164,
        ! a twenty-two per cent productivity BONUS for the child of a homeless
        ! parent. The excess is this persistence, so the quantity that has to
        ! move is the one transmitting the poverty, not a penalty on top of it.
        !
        ! ONE MOMENT, ONE INSTRUMENT. The update below REPLACES
        ! penalty_child_homeless on that moment rather than joining it: it steps
        ! whichever of the two adjusters is positive and stops if both are,
        ! because two instruments on one target is one object with two names and
        ! neither is identified.
        !
        ! THE CAVEAT TRAVELS WITH THE NUMBER. The literature runs 0.3 to 0.5, and
        ! the frontier says this moment wants roughly 0.11 -- a factor of three
        ! below that range. That departure is a finding rather than a fit, and it
        ! may be fixing the symptom in the wrong place: the excess could be rho,
        ! or the model's homeless being 7.1x less productive than its housed, or
        ! homelessness being too steep in income. rho is not free either -- it
        ! feeds delta_d's channel and the dispersion sigma_y is calibrated on.
        !
        ! ITS ONLY TWO USES ARE IN create_newborn_employment_probability, the
        ! innovation variance and the AR(1) mean, and both are executable. That
        ! is why it can stop being a `parameter` at all, and why the update below
        ! rebuilds the newborn draw exactly as delta_d's and delta_h's do.
        real(8) :: rho_intergenerational_adjuster=0.d0
        real(8), parameter :: rho_intergenerational_adjuster_start=5*10.d0**(-2)
        ! THE BOUNDS MATCH THE HANDOVER'S CLAMP, deliberately, so a value the
        ! handover accepts is a value the search can also reach. In particular
        ! the floor is ZERO and not a small positive number: at rho = 0 a child
        ! of a household at the top node and a child of one at the bottom draw
        ! the SAME distribution, which is the behaviour this parameter was
        ! introduced to remove, and the comment above says that case is what
        ! makes the whole channel testable. Barring it would bar the null test.
        real(8), parameter :: rho_intergenerational_floor=0.d0
        real(8), parameter :: rho_intergenerational_cap=0.999d0
        real(8) :: rho_intergenerational_step_1=0

        ! The node boundaries in LOG productivity, filled where the grid is
        ! built. Needed because the child's conditional distribution is
        ! integrated over each node's interval rather than split between two
        ! neighbours, which is exact where the two point split was not.
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size+1) :: log_y_edge=0

        ! CHILD node by PARENT node by the parent's disorder and housing status.
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size,employment_grid_size,0:1,1:2) :: &
            newborn_employment_probability

        ! WHOSE CHILDREN THE NEWBORNS ARE. newborn_employment_probability is a
        ! draw conditional on the parent's disorder and housing status, so using
        ! it needs the shares of the households actually being replaced, which is
        ! the mass dying in each state rather than the mass living in it. The
        ! initial value puts every parent in the clean housed cell, which makes
        ! the newborn draw the unconditional one until a distribution has been
        ! solved and can say otherwise.
        ! The dying mass now has to be resolved by the parent's productivity
        ! node as well, because that is what the child's draw conditions on. The
        ! initial value puts every parent in the clean housed cell at the node
        ! whose y is nearest one, so a newborn draws close to the unconditional
        ! distribution until a solved distribution can say otherwise.
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size,0:1,1:2) :: newborn_parent_share=0
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size,0:1,1:2) :: newborn_parent_share_acc=0

        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size) :: newborn_productivity_draw
        real(8), dimension(threshold_addiction_index) :: newborn_state_draw
        integer :: newborn_d_index_1,newborn_h_index_1,newborn_parent_node_1
        real(8) :: newborn_smi_at_birth

        ! THE CHILD IN THE CONTINUATION, AT EVERY AGE. The written model has the
        ! household value its child at weight omega whenever it does not survive,
        ! so the continuation is
        !
        !   beta*phi_j*E V_{j+1}  +  beta*(1 - phi_j)*omega*E V_1(0, y_child, ...)
        !
        ! rather than the child appearing only in a terminal condition. It matters
        ! because phi_j itself depends on the housing chosen and, through d, on
        ! use, so a household that uses raises the chance that it is the child's
        ! term and not its own that is realised. expected_child_value holds
        ! E V_1(0, y_child, ...) by the parent's SUD status and by whether it was
        ! homeless, which is what the newborn productivity penalty conditions on.
        !
        ! IT IS ON. omega is the parameter the paper's welfare reversal turns on,
        ! and leaving the term switched off meant the model that produces the
        ! numbers did not contain the externality the paper is about.
        !
        ! HOW THE FIXED POINT IS HANDLED. The age one value function enters every
        ! age's continuation, which is a fixed point across ages rather than a
        ! backward recursion. It is carried the way the outer loop already
        ! carries prices, taxes and inheritance: expected_child_value is computed
        ! once at the head of each backward pass from the PREVIOUS pass's age one
        ! values, so within a pass it is a constant and the recursion is still
        ! backward. Convergence of the outer loop is then convergence of this
        ! too, and it shows up in the loop's own distances rather than needing a
        ! separate test. On the first pass the value function is zero, so the
        ! child term is zero and the model starts where it used to be.
        integer, parameter :: include_child_in_continuation=1
        ! BY THE PARENT'S NODE TOO, now that the child's draw conditions on it.
        ! A parent at the top node and one at the bottom no longer face the same
        ! loss from passing a penalty on, because the child's whole distribution
        ! differs and the value function is concave.

        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size,0:1,1:2) :: expected_child_value=0

        ! ONE COPY PER ECONOMY, BECAUSE THE TWO WERE FEEDING EACH OTHER.
        !
        ! compute_expected_child_value reads whatever value function is in
        ! memory. A calibration loop solves the omega counterfactual FIRST and
        ! the benchmark second, so the benchmark's child penalty was built from
        ! the COUNTERFACTUAL's value function -- a world in which parents place
        ! no weight on children -- and the next counterfactual's from the
        ! benchmark's. Neither leg ever iterated against itself.
        !
        ! Measured, node 1, on the benchmark vector: 0 (priming), -69.162
        ! (benchmark, off the priming solve), -69.203 (counterfactual, off the
        ! benchmark), -64.009 (benchmark, off the counterfactual), -67.088
        ! (counterfactual, off the benchmark). A two-cycle, not a convergence.
        !
        ! And not merely biased but UNSTABLE: two runs a quarter of one per cent
        ! apart in value_of_life, every other parameter tracking to 0.03 per
        ! cent, agreed for four loops and then separated, the implied elasticity
        ! of b on value_of_life going -8, -8, -63, -80. The first two are the
        ! model; the last two are no slope this model has.
        !
        ! So EACH ECONOMY KEEPS ITS OWN child penalty, computed at the end of its
        ! solve from that solve's own value function and read back at the start of
        ! its next one -- an ordinary fixed point iteration per economy, riding
        ! along with the calibration and used in the household's problem
        ! throughout.
        !
        ! The author's instruction arrived in three steps and the last governs:
        ! *"when you solve with omega_c=0 you just use the V(1) from the
        ! calibrated omega_c"*, then *"well, actually when omega_c=0 you dont need
        ! v(1)"*, then *"save the V(1) from the omega_c>0, but also a different
        ! V(1) for omega_c=0"*. Two slots is the strictest of the three.
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size,0:1,1:2) :: expected_child_value_own=0
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size,0:1,1:2) :: expected_child_value_cf=0
        integer :: solving_omega_counterfactual=0

        ! GUESS AND VERIFY ON V(1). The author, 11 September: *"you need to
        ! converge on v(1)...guess and verify"* and *"you have to make sure that
        ! the V(1) computed and the one guessed converge"*.
        !
        ! A solve is HANDED a child penalty and PRODUCES one, and the two are the
        ! same object. One update a calibration loop is a guess that is never
        ! verified: the parameters move underneath it, so it chases a target that
        ! is itself moving and never actually reaches a fixed point. The main
        ! solve is now iterated at FIXED parameters until the penalty it is given
        ! and the penalty it returns agree.
        !
        ! Only the calibrated economy needs this. At omega_c=0 the term is
        ! multiplied by omega_c in the Bellman, so it is exactly zero whatever
        ! V(1) says, and there is nothing to converge.
        !
        ! The test is RELATIVE because the penalty is order 60 to 70 in
        ! magnitude, and the cap exists because an uncapped inner loop inside an
        ! uncapped outer loop is two ways to hang instead of one.
        real(8), dimension(employment_grid_size,0:1,1:2) :: expected_child_value_guessed=0
        real(8) :: child_value_distance=1
        real(8), parameter :: stopping_rule_child_value=1.d0*10.d0**(-3)
        integer :: child_value_pass=0
        integer, parameter :: max_child_value_passes=12

        ! WHETHER expected_child_value HAS EVER BEEN COMPUTED FOR REAL.
        ! compute_expected_child_value returns zero while age one is still
        ! the sentinel, and it is the sentinel on the first solve, so the
        ! term was designed to switch on at the SECOND pass. The
        ! calibration loop has one; a point run does not, so the child
        ! externality was off in every point this fleet ever measured -- 19
        ! points carrying MOMENT omega all reported a use gap of exactly
        ! zero, which is what a no-op counterfactual looks like. This flag
        ! lets a point run pay for one priming solve and then run with the
        ! term live.
        integer :: child_value_ready=0
        real(8), dimension(threshold_addiction_index) :: unconditional_unemployment_probability
        real(8), dimension(life_span,employment_grid_size,employment_grid_size) :: employment_process
        real(8), allocatable, dimension(:,:,:,:,:,:) :: unemployment_process
        ! THE NEWBORN'S TRANSITION, WHICH IS NOT THE PARENT'S.
        !
        ! A newborn's SMI state was being drawn from unemployment_process applied
        ! to the DYING PARENT'S index, so it inherited the parent's mental illness
        ! with probability 1-lambda_m. The note has m exogenous with onset by age
        ! and the onset identity reproduces the observed profile only if the age
        ! one mass carries prevalence p(1). It did not, so the profile was never
        ! reproduced: the model's cohort weighted prevalence came out near 2 per
        ! cent against a profile averaging 5, and every SMI moment inherited that
        ! error, including the three the SMI parameters are calibrated to.
        !
        ! The transition factorises into an employment leg and an SMI leg, so
        ! this array is the same product with the SMI leg replaced by a draw from
        ! p(1). The employment leg is left alone, since the intergenerational
        ! link in productivity is deliberate.
        real(8), allocatable, dimension(:,:,:,:,:,:) :: newborn_unemployment_process
        
        ! psi, THE UNEMPLOYMENT REPLACEMENT RATE. A VARIABLE SINCE 6 SEPTEMBER,
        ! on the author's instruction: *"cant you just build it into my method to
        ! target these three moments with the 3 instruments?"*, *"i think the
        ! method should allow you to deliver all 3"*.
        !
        ! WHY IT HAD TO STOP BEING A CONSTANT. The author's design is three
        ! instruments for three moments -- the bottom node's mass for the employed
        ! share of the homeless, the street for the homelessness rate, the cheapest
        ! rung for the cost burden -- and all three were measured on 6 September.
        ! Each does what it should. The design still could not reach h_1, and the
        ! reason is none of them:
        !
        !   at street 0.0008, rung 0.070, bottom nodes merged so that nobody is
        !   priced out on employment income, h_1 = 0.00725 with
        !       homeless by choice                 = 0.0000
        !       homeless who could afford it       = 0.0000
        !       employed share of the homeless     = 0.0000
        !       homelessness among the UNEMPLOYED  = 0.0720
        !
        ! EVERY REMAINING HOMELESS HOUSEHOLD IS ONE THAT LOST ITS JOB. And 0.00725
        ! is 1.8 times the WHOLE h_1 target of 0.00399, so a COMPONENT of the
        ! moment floors above the moment itself. No instrument that redistributes
        ! among the housed can take a total below a component's floor, which is
        ! why three good instruments were not enough.
        !
        ! AND THE FLOOR IS AN ARTEFACT OF THE PERIOD LENGTH. One period is a YEAR.
        ! A household flagged unemployed receives psi times its capped base for the
        ! WHOLE year. Real unemployment insurance runs about 26 weeks and mean
        ! duration is near 22, so a worker with a spell keeps far more than half of
        ! ANNUAL earnings. psi = 0.5 is the right statutory number attached to the
        ! wrong horizon, and it manufactures precisely the homelessness that was
        ! blocking the calibration.
        !
        ! 0.5 REPRODUCES THE OLD MODEL EXACTLY, so this widens the parameter space
        ! rather than moving the benchmark.
        real(8) :: replacement_rate_ui=0.5d0

        ! Social security replaces this share of the productivity state, the
        ! 0.40 of eq:fincome.
        real(8), parameter :: ss_replacement_rate=0.40
        ! THE US SOCIAL SECURITY BENEFIT FORMULA. See the block beside
        ! model_ss_benefits for the source and the arithmetic.
        integer :: use_pia_formula=1
        real(8) :: ss_bend_1=0.2208d0
        real(8) :: ss_bend_2=1.3314d0
        real(8) :: ss_taxable_max=2.6428d0
        real(8) :: ss_pia_rate_1=0.90d0
        real(8) :: ss_pia_rate_2=0.32d0
        real(8) :: ss_pia_rate_3=0.15d0
        real(8) :: ssi_floor_level=0.1774d0
        real(8) :: ssi_floor_takeup=0.0d0
        real(8) :: pia_y_1
        ! ybar, THE CAP ON THE BASE psi MULTIPLIES, in units of average income.
        ! Freed alongside psi on 6 September: the two together are the level and
        ! the ceiling of out-of-work income, and calibrating one while the other
        ! is welded shut puts the whole adjustment into one margin. 1 reproduces
