        real(8) :: max_ui_as_perc_of_avg_inc=1.d0
        ! bar pi^ue, the annual job finding rate. The note's external table sets it at
        ! 0.55; the code had 0.2974.
        real(8) :: probability_becoming_employed=0.55
        real(8) :: probability_becoming_unemployed=&
            (1-perceived_probability_lower)*0.0155+perceived_probability_lower*0.0143
        
        real(8) :: multiple_of_probabilities_of_employment_if_addicted=&
            have_increased_unemployment_from_addiction*2.7+(1-have_increased_unemployment_from_addiction)*size_of_penalty*1.7
        ! MULTIPLE_FIND_IF_ADDICTED SPLITS THE DISORDER'S JOB-FINDING PENALTY OFF
        ! ITS SEPARATION PENALTY, 7 September, on the author's instruction. They
        ! had already asked for exactly this split on the SMI side -- kappa_uem
        ! for separation, kappa_find_smi for finding -- and then: *"And I think
        ! for Sud we might just want to have the same thing...?"*, and *"Let's do
        ! the split now"*.
        !
        ! multiple_of_probabilities_of_employment_if_addicted used to do BOTH,
        ! sep*M and find/M, so the disorder's unemployment RATE and its spell
        ! DURATION were one knob. The rate is pbu*M^2/(pbu*M^2+p_find) and the
        ! duration is M/p_find, so no single M can put the rate where the data
        ! wants it and the duration wherever homelessness needs it. That is the
        ! same argument that split kappa_uem on 6 September, and it applies here
        ! unchanged.
        !
        ! Split, the multiplier sets the rate and this sets the duration: longer
        ! spells drain wealth, and wealth depletion is what puts a household with
        ! the disorder on the street. That is the channel sud_homeless needs.
        !
        ! At 1.0 the finding margin is unpenalised, which is NOT the old
        ! behaviour: the old code divided finding by the multiplier. A point that
        ! wants the old economy passes multiple_find_if_addicted equal to
        ! multiple_of_probabilities_of_employment_if_addicted.
        real(8) :: multiple_find_if_addicted=1.0
        ! OPENED 11 September, REVERSING AN INSTRUCTION FROM EARLIER THE SAME DAY.
        ! The author had said "keep multiple_find_if_addicted to the current value
        ! (about 28), and score it, but dont adjust it", and the comment at the
        ! sud_homeless print still recorded that the moment deliberately had no
        ! instrument. They have now said: "I think you should open the job finding
        ! rate for those with Sud who are unemployed. It should hit the target of
        ! homeless with Sud. The finding rate should rise I think, since we have
        ! too many homeless with Sud". So it is opened, and that comment is
        ! corrected rather than left to be read as still in force.
        !
        ! THE SIGN, AND IT IS A TRAP. This parameter is a DIVISOR:
        !     probability_becoming_employed_if_addicted =
        !         probability_becoming_employed / multiple_find_if_addicted
        ! so at 28.54 the addicted find work 28.5 times LESS easily, and RAISING it
        ! LOWERS the finding rate. The author's intent -- the finding rate should
        ! rise -- therefore calls for this parameter to FALL. The instrument moves
        ! opposite to the rate it is named after.
        !
        ! So sud_homeless ABOVE target wants a LOWER multiple, and the step is
        ! multiple - adjuster*(model - target), which is negative-going exactly
        ! then.
        !
        ! BOUNDS [1,100] are this parameter's own handover clamp. At 1.0 the
        ! finding margin is unpenalised -- the addicted find work as easily as
        ! anyone -- so 1.0 is a genuine floor: if the moment is still above target
        ! there, no value of this parameter closes it.
        ! RETIRED 12 September.  The employment multiplier takes sud_homeless on
        ! the author's instruction, and two instruments cannot chase one moment.
        ! Its distance is zeroed with it at 43264, which is the standing rule:
        ! a bracket with a zero adjuster and a LIVE distance cannot converge and
        ! the do-while has no cap to save it.
        ! LIVE, ON THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION: *"this should move to match the SUD
        ! homeless multiple_find_if_addicted"*. It was held at zero, and the
        ! consequence was that THE SUD SHARE OF THE HOMELESS HAD NO WORKING
        ! INSTRUMENT AT ALL. Both parameters bracketed on that moment were out
        ! of action at once and in different ways, which is why it sat 56 per
        ! cent above target across every point of 14 September without moving:
        !
        !   multiple_of_probabilities_of_employment_if_addicted -- adjuster live
        !     at 0.5, but the parameter sits at 1, which is its floor, and the
        !     moment is now ABOVE target so the step asks it to go lower. The
        !     code comment beside it was written when the moment was BELOW
        !     target (0.0814 against 0.217); the sign has flipped since;
        !   multiple_find_if_addicted -- room from 21.98 down to a floor of 1,
        !     and an adjuster of zero.
        !
        ! The live one could not move and the movable one was not live. This
        ! makes the second live; the first is pinned at 1 on the author's
        ! *"this multiple_of_probabilities_of_employment_if_addicted should stay
        ! at 1"*, which its own merge() already retires the distance for.
        ! GAIN RAISED 18 September, on the author's *"we need to move those
        ! parameters I wrote above much faster so they converge in 15 iterations"*.
        ! Realised step was 3.48 per cent with the SUD share of the
        ! homeless 33 per cent off; 5.74 gives ten per cent.
        ! Raising the STEP CAP did nothing for it: the cap was not binding, the
        ! GAIN was. The gap is measured on one scale and the parameter lives on
        ! another, so the product was a fraction of a per cent a step. Set so the
        ! ten per cent cap governs and the existing damping handles sign flips.
        real(8) :: multiple_find_adjuster=8.6d0

        ! ITS OWN CAP, 15 September: "multiple_find_if_addicted should be lower
        ! ....you need to update it faster to match the share of homeless with
        ! SUD". It ran on drug_block_max_rel_step, 0.5 per cent a loop, which it
        ! shares with addiction_scalar; raising that would speed b's instrument
        ! too, so this gets its own.
        !
        ! The direction was never wrong. This parameter DIVIDES the finding
        ! rate, so a fall raises it, fewer addicts end up on the street and the
        ! SUD share falls -- and it has fallen, 19.5 to 13.4 across the session.
        ! The moment still rose, from +27 to +34 per cent, so something else is
        ! pushing it up faster than 0.5 per cent a loop can pull it down.
        ! RAISED TO A TENTH, 17 September, on the author's *"Find if addicted has
        ! to rise a lot"*. It has to TRAVEL, which is the same argument the file
        ! already made for bottom_productivity_floor_rate. The moment is now 68
        ! per cent BELOW target rather than above it -- 0.070 against 0.217 -- and
        ! the parameter sits at 4.167 where the comment above records it at 19.5
        ! and 13.4 earlier in the project. At two per cent a step that is 59
        ! updates, and the b gate is shut for most of them; at a tenth it is
        ! twelve. This is a speed limit on the SEARCH and not a model parameter,
        ! so it invalidates nothing about any point already solved.
        real(8) :: multiple_find_max_rel_step=0.10d0
        real(8) :: distance_multiple_find=1
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_multiple_find=10.d0**(-2)
        real(8), parameter :: multiple_find_lo=1.d0
        real(8), parameter :: multiple_find_hi=100.d0
        real(8), dimension(0:include_addiction,0:include_addiction,&
            0:include_addiction+include_heavy_vs_light_use_transitions,1:2) :: &
            addiction_probabilities,addiction_probabilities_percieved
        real(8) :: probability_of_stopping_addiction=0.025
        ! mu_H, RECOVERY ON THE STREET AS A MULTIPLE OF RECOVERY WHILE HOUSED.
        !
        ! IT WAS 1/6 AND THE NOTE'S OWN MOMENT SAYS 0.679, so the code was
        ! asserting a recovery gradient four times the one it claims to be
        ! calibrated to. Milby et al. (2005) randomised cocaine dependent homeless
        ! adults and measured abstinent weeks of 6.60 in the housed arm against
        ! 4.48 unhoused, a ratio of 1.473. At 1/6 the model delivers a ratio near
        ! 6.0 instead, whatever the trial length is taken to be.
        !
        ! The mapping is the inverse ratio, and it is not sensitive to the horizon.
        ! With a per period recovery hazard h over a trial of T periods, expected
        ! abstinent time is sum_t [1-(1-h)^t], which for a small h*T is close to
        ! h*T(T+1)/2, so the ratio of abstinent time is the ratio of hazards.
        ! Evaluated at the housed hazard of 0.025 a year, mu_H = 4.48/6.60 gives a
        ! model ratio of 1.472 at twelve weeks, 1.471 at twenty six and 1.469 at
        ! fifty two. The old 1/6 gives 5.99, 5.98 and 5.96.
        !
        ! WHICH WAY THE ERROR RAN. mu_H is the counterweight to the moral hazard in
        ! pi_V: a small mu_H means a voucher buys a great deal of recovery, so the
        ! insurance effect is outweighed and Housing First looks better. The old
        ! value therefore biased the comparison TOWARDS Housing First, against the
        ! paper's own conclusion, so correcting it should strengthen the result
        ! rather than rescue it.
        !
        ! This is still the short trial approximation. Closing it exactly is the
        ! second stage's job: a cohort experiment at benchmark prices that
        ! reproduces Milby's two arms inside the model. There is no such loop yet,
        ! which is why the declared value matters at all.
        real(8) :: mu_homeless=4.48d0/6.60d0
        real(8) :: probability_of_stopping_addiction_if_homeless=(4.48d0/6.60d0)*0.025
        real(8) :: probability_becoming_unemployed_if_addicted=0
        real(8) :: probability_becoming_employed_if_addicted=0
        
